export default {"model":"BAAI/bge-small-en-v1.5 (ONNX, int8 dynamic quantization from Xenova/bge-small-en-v1.5)","modelFile":"bge-small-en-v1.5-int8.onnx","modelSha256":"6c9c6101a956d62dfb5e7190c538226c0c5bb9cb27b651234b6df063ee7dbfe4","dims":384,"notes":[{"symbol":"addition","source":"symbolic_memory/math/topics.json","kind":"math","hash":"014f5e51","text":"Addition combines quantities. a + b is the sum. This is definitional arithmetic, not a computer-algebra system."},{"symbol":"subtraction","source":"symbolic_memory/math/topics.json","kind":"math","hash":"04491d58","text":"Subtraction removes one quantity from another. a - b is the difference."},{"symbol":"multiplication","source":"symbolic_memory/math/topics.json","kind":"math","hash":"b99233d6","text":"Multiplication is repeated addition. a * b is the product."},{"symbol":"division","source":"symbolic_memory/math/topics.json","kind":"math","hash":"ab573491","text":"Division splits a quantity into equal parts. Division by zero is undefined."},{"symbol":"exponent","source":"symbolic_memory/math/topics.json","kind":"math","hash":"1b2996af","text":"An exponent repeats multiplication. a^2 is a squared. a^0 is 1 for nonzero a."},{"symbol":"variable","source":"symbolic_memory/math/topics.json","kind":"math","hash":"ff253c63","text":"A variable is a symbol that stands for an unknown or changing number, often written as x or y."},{"symbol":"equation","source":"symbolic_memory/math/topics.json","kind":"math","hash":"e4274236","text":"An equation says two expressions are equal. Solving it means finding values that make both sides the same."},{"symbol":"triangle","source":"symbolic_memory/math/topics.json","kind":"math","hash":"7a26e4da","text":"A triangle is a polygon with three sides. In a flat triangle the interior angles add up to 180 degrees."},{"symbol":"circle","source":"symbolic_memory/math/topics.json","kind":"math","hash":"858defe8","text":"A circle is the set of points at a fixed radius from a center. Circumference is pi times the diameter. Area is pi times radius squared."},{"symbol":"pythagorean_relation","source":"symbolic_memory/math/topics.json","kind":"math","hash":"aee66120","text":"In a right triangle, the square of the hypotenuse equals the sum of the squares of the other two sides."},{"symbol":"derivative","source":"symbolic_memory/math/topics.json","kind":"math","hash":"d9c18f71","text":"A derivative is the instantaneous rate of change of a function. It is the slope of the tangent line."},{"symbol":"integral","source":"symbolic_memory/math/topics.json","kind":"math","hash":"0110675d","text":"An integral accumulates a quantity. A definite integral is the signed area between a curve and the axis over an interval."},{"symbol":"limit","source":"symbolic_memory/math/topics.json","kind":"math","hash":"9710cbe6","text":"A limit is the value a function approaches as its input approaches some point. Calculus builds on limits."},{"symbol":"poison_hemlock","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/entries_foraging.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"30ccfc91","text":"Poison hemlock is a deadly poisonous plant in the carrot family, often mistaken for wild carrot, parsley, or parsnip. Also called Conium maculatum, poison parsley, tall white umbrella flower, hemlock plant. Look for smooth, hairless, hollow stalk with purple blotches; fern-like, finely divided leaves that smell musty when crushed."},{"symbol":"wild_carrot_lookalike","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/entries_foraging.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"6fc90693","text":"Wild carrot is a common white umbrella wildflower with hairy stems and a carrot-smelling root, and it is easy to confuse with deadly hemlocks. Also called Daucus carota, Queen Anne's lace, bird's nest flower, wild carrot. Look for hairy, solid stems; carrot smell from a crushed root or leaf. Use it only as an identification lesson. A beginner should not eat any carrot-family plant from the wild."},{"symbol":"water_hemlock","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/entries_foraging.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"1f5ab9b1","text":"Water hemlock is one of the most poisonous plants in North America; a small amount of the root can cause fatal seizures. Also called Cicuta maculata, spotted water hemlock, cowbane, deadly umbrella plant in wet ground. Look for grows in wet ground: stream banks, ditches, marshes; smooth hollow stems streaked or spotted with purple. Use only as a warning. Stay out of wet-ground carrot-family plants."},{"symbol":"poison_ivy_id","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/entries_foraging.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"714639f0","text":"Poison ivy is a common plant of New England with three-leaflet leaves and an oil, urushiol, that causes an itchy, blistering rash on contact. Also called leaves of three, Toxicodendron radicans, hairy rope vine, itchy vine, three leaf plant. Look for three leaflets, with the middle leaflet on a longer stalk; grows as a vine with hairy aerial roots, a shrub, or ground cover."},{"symbol":"poison_sumac","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/entries_foraging.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"11ee78b9","text":"Poison sumac is a swamp shrub with 7 to 13 smooth-edged leaflets and white berries; it causes the same rash as poison ivy. Also called Toxicodendron vernix, poison oak, swamp sumac, white berries sumac. Look for leaves of 7 to 13 smooth leaflets on a red stem; grows in wet swamps and bogs. Stay out of swamp shrubs you cannot name, and wash after contact like poison ivy."},{"symbol":"mushrooms_never_from_photo","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/entries_foraging.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"a4fcde68","text":"A photo, an app, or a camera match can never confirm a wild mushroom is safe; deadly species resemble edible ones, and cooking does not remove most toxins. Also called can I eat this mushroom, is this mushroom edible, mushroom identification app, identify a mushroom. Look for many deadly species look like edible ones; key features like spore print, base, and habitat do not show in a photo."},{"symbol":"jack_o_lantern_mushroom","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/entries_foraging.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"53c81d3a","text":"The Jack-o'-lantern is an orange, poisonous mushroom that grows in clusters on wood and causes severe vomiting and cramps; it is often mistaken for a chanterelle. Also called Omphalotus illudens, orange poisonous mushroom, false chanterelle, orange mushroom on a stump. Look for orange, funnel-shaped caps growing in clusters from wood or buried roots; true, thin, sharp-edged gills running down the stem."},{"symbol":"chanterelle_vs_lookalikes","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/entries_foraging.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"38f2077c","text":"True chanterelles are golden, funnel-shaped mushrooms with blunt, forked ridges instead of thin gills, but only an expert should confirm them. Also called Cantharellus, golden chanterelle, false chanterelle, chanterelles in New England. Look for blunt, forked ridges that run down the stem and look like folds; grows from soil near trees, usually singly or scattered. Learn them in person with a local expert or society, never from a photo."},{"symbol":"amanita_death_cap","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/entries_foraging.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"ef942cf7","text":"Destroying angels and death caps are deadly mushrooms; a small bite can destroy the liver, and symptoms may be delayed for hours. Also called Amanita bisporigera, Amanita phalloides, death cap, destroying angel, white mushroom in the woods. Look for white or greenish cap with white gills; a skirt-like ring on the stem and a cup or sac (volva) at the base, which may be buried."},{"symbol":"eaten_poison_plant_what_to_do","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/entries_foraging.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"d1597a49","text":"If someone may have eaten a poisonous plant or mushroom, call Poison Control or 911 immediately, before symptoms appear. Also called ate a wild plant, child ate a berry, ate a mushroom and sick, pet ate a mushroom. Look for any wild plant or mushroom swallowed by a child or pet; vomiting, belly pain, drooling, confusion, or seizures."},{"symbol":"plant_safe_positive_id","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/entries_foraging.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"64ceaa1a","text":"A wild plant is only safe to eat when a person has positively identified it by several features and ruled out its poisonous look-alikes; if you are unsure, the answer is no. Also called can I eat this plant, is this berry safe, wild plant edible, hungry and found a plant. Look for more than one feature checked: leaf, flower, fruit, stem, habitat; look-alikes ruled out."},{"symbol":"foraging_false_rules","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/entries_foraging.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"74308d59","text":"Rules of thumb such as 'animals eat it', 'boiling makes it safe', or 'avoid red plants' are false and have killed people. Also called universal edibility test, animals eat it so it is safe, red berries rule, boiling removes poison, taste test. Look for animals can eat plants that poison people; boiling does not destroy many toxins."},{"symbol":"toxic_berries_new_england","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/entries_foraging.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"5b39d854","text":"Several New England berries are poisonous, including baneberry, pokeweed, bittersweet nightshade, yew, and Virginia creeper. Also called baneberry, doll's eyes, pokeweed berries, nightshade, yew berries. Look for baneberry: white or red berries with a dark dot on stalks; pokeweed: dark purple berries on pink stems. Teach children not to eat any wild berry, and keep these plants out of reach. Leave unknown berries alone."},{"symbol":"berries_edible_new_england","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/entries_foraging.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"a4e9f38a","text":"Wild blueberries, blackberries, and raspberries are common New England fruits with few dangerous look-alikes, but always confirm them in person. Also called wild blueberries, blackberries, raspberry canes, wild berries Maine. Look for blueberry: small blue berry with a five-pointed crown on the end; blackberry and raspberry: clusters of tiny drupelets on thorny canes; raspberries pull off hollow."},{"symbol":"morel_true_vs_false","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/entries_foraging.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"074c45e2","text":"True morels have a honeycomb cap attached to a hollow stem, while false morels have a wrinkled, brain-like cap and cottony or chambered inside and can be toxic. Also called Morchella, false morel, Gyromitra, brain mushroom, morel hunting. Look for true morel: pitted honeycomb cap, hollow from tip to base when cut lengthwise; false morel: wrinkled, lobed cap that hangs free, with cottony or chambered interior."},{"symbol":"fiddleheads_ostrich_fern","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/entries_foraging.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"21a98bb9","text":"Fiddleheads are the coiled young fronds of the ostrich fern, a spring food in Maine; they must be identified correctly and cooked thoroughly or they can cause illness. Also called fiddlehead fern, ostrich fern, bracken fern, edible ferns. Look for a smooth stem with a deep U-shaped groove on the inside; brown papery scales on the coil."},{"symbol":"acorns_leaching","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/entries_foraging.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"427ef2a7","text":"Acorns are high in tannins that taste bitter and upset the stomach, so they must be shelled, ground, and leached in many changes of water before use. Also called eat acorns, acorn flour, tannins in acorns, white oak acorns. Look for bitter, astringent taste when raw; white oak acorns have less tannin. If you want to use acorns, follow a sourced method and expect hours of leaching. In a survival situation, they are not a first food."},{"symbol":"wild_nuts_new_england","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/entries_foraging.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"778268e5","text":"Hazelnuts, beechnuts, hickory nuts, black walnuts, and butternuts are native New England nuts, but they need positive identification and proper processing. Also called nuts you can eat, black walnut, butternut, beechnut, hickory. Look for each tree has distinct leaves, bark, and husk; black walnut and butternut have thick, stain-prone husks. Identify the tree in person, check for allergies, shell and eat only sound nuts."},{"symbol":"cattail_lookalikes","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/entries_foraging.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"539b7b27","text":"Cattails are tall wetland plants with brown sausage-shaped spikes, but young shoots can be confused with poisonous wild iris. Also called Typha, bulrush, cattail shoots, wild iris lookalike. Look for brown cylindrical flower spike above long flat leaves; grows in standing water or marshes. Learn them with an expert; cattails absorb pollutants, so use only from clean water."},{"symbol":"dandelion_greens","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/entries_foraging.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"2fd9e296","text":"Dandelion and broadleaf plantain are common lawn and field weeds that can be eaten as greens when picked from clean, unsprayed places. Also called dandelion, edible weeds, plantain leaves. Look for dandelion: toothed rosette leaves and a single hollow stem with one yellow flower; plantain: oval ribbed leaves in a rosette with a spike of seeds. Pick young leaves from clean, unsprayed ground, wash them, and cook or eat as a small addition, not as free calories."},{"symbol":"poison_contact_eating_smoke","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/entries_foraging.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"73525956","text":"Plants can harm by contact (poison ivy, wild parsnip, giant hogweed), by eating (hemlocks, baneberry), or by smoke (burning poison ivy). Also called giant hogweed, wild parsnip burns, stinging nettle, poisonous plants overview. Look for wild parsnip and giant hogweed sap causes burns in sunlight; poison ivy oil causes a rash. Learn the plants of your area, wear long sleeves and gloves in brush, and wash exposed skin after contact. Keep children and pets from unknown plants."},{"symbol":"fly_agaric","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/entries_fungi.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"7a23fef9","text":"Fly agaric is a red or orange Amanita mushroom with white warts that is poisonous and related to the deadly Amanitas. Also called Amanita muscaria, red mushroom with white spots, toadstool. Look for red or orange cap with white warts, white gills, a ring on the stem and a bulb at the base; warts are remnants of a veil and can wash off in rain. Learn it as a warning. Toxic. Related to the deadly Amanitas."},{"symbol":"deadly_galerina","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/entries_fungi.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"44c06649","text":"Deadly galerina is a small brown mushroom on rotting wood that contains the same liver toxins as the death cap. Also called Galerina marginata, funeral bell, autumn skullcap. Look for small brown mushroom on rotting wood with a thin ring and rusty brown spores; cap 1 to 4 cm, sticky when wet. Learn it as a warning. Deadly. Grows on the same logs as honey mushrooms."},{"symbol":"webcaps","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/entries_fungi.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"f2aa959b","text":"Webcaps are brown Cortinarius mushrooms with a cobweb veil, and some damage the kidneys days after eating. Also called Cortinarius, cortinarius, webcap. Look for rusty-brown spores, a web-like veil on young caps, often on the ground in woods; rusty-brown spore print. Learn it as a warning. Some are deadly to the kidneys. Never eat a brown mushroom you cannot identify."},{"symbol":"chicken_of_the_woods","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/entries_fungi.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"0e831aea","text":"Chicken of the woods is a bright orange and yellow shelf fungus on trees with pores, not gills. Also called Laetiporus, sulphur shelf, chicken mushroom, Laetiporus sulphureus. Look for bright orange and yellow shelves with no gills on trees, soft when young; no gills; tiny pores underneath. Use it as an identification and look-alike lesson; only an expert who sees it in person can confirm it. Some people react badly, and growths on conifers or eucalyptus can be harmful."},{"symbol":"hen_of_the_woods","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/entries_fungi.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"3ad221b4","text":"Hen of the woods is a large gray-brown clustered mushroom at the base of oaks, with pores instead of gills. Also called maitake, Grifola frondosa, sheep's head. Look for big clump of grey-brown overlapping fronds at the base of oaks; pores instead of gills. Use it as an identification and look-alike lesson; only an expert who sees it in person can confirm it."},{"symbol":"giant_puffball","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/entries_fungi.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"5ea05fe3","text":"A giant puffball is a round white mushroom with no stem or gills that is solid white inside when young. Also called puffball, Calvatia, puffballs, Calvatia gigantea. Look for round white ball with no stem or gills; solid white inside when cut. Use it as an identification and look-alike lesson; only an expert who sees it in person can confirm it. A young Amanita looks like a puffball. Cut it open: any outline of a cap and stem means leave it."},{"symbol":"lions_mane","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/entries_fungi.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"6276784d","text":"Lion's mane is a white mushroom of hanging icicle-like spines that grows on hardwood trees. Also called Hericium, bearded tooth, pom pom mushroom. Look for white hanging icicle-like spines on trees; no cap and no gills. Use it as an identification and look-alike lesson; only an expert who sees it in person can confirm it."},{"symbol":"oyster_mushroom","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/entries_fungi.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"120e95c8","text":"The oyster mushroom is a gray-tan shelf mushroom on wood with gills running down to a short side stem. Also called Pleurotus, oyster mushrooms, tree oyster, Pleurotus ostreatus. Look for shelf-shaped grey-tan caps with gills running down to a short side stem, on wood; white to lilac spore print. Use it as an identification and look-alike lesson; only an expert who sees it in person can confirm it. Do not confuse it with other shelf fungi that have gills."},{"symbol":"turkey_tail","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/entries_fungi.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"dd86be64","text":"Turkey tail is a thin, tough shelf fungus on dead wood with colored bands and white pores underneath. Also called Trametes versicolor, turkey tails. Look for thin tough shelves with colored concentric bands and a white pore underside; tough and leathery, not fleshy. Use it as an identification lesson. Yehsa does not give doses or remedies with wild fungi."},{"symbol":"birch_polypore","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/entries_fungi.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"e8eeb4a4","text":"Birch polypore is a rounded gray-white shelf fungus on dead birch, used historically as a razor strop and tinder. Also called razor strop, Fomitopsis betulina, birch bracket. Look for rounded grey-white shelf on dead birch; the pore side is white. Use it as an identification lesson. Yehsa does not give doses or remedies with wild fungi. Survival history only. Do not eat it."},{"symbol":"reishi","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/entries_fungi.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"f1ae4694","text":"Reishi, or hemlock varnish shelf, is a glossy red-brown shelf fungus on hemlock and other conifers. Also called hemlock varnish shelf, Ganoderma, lingzhi, Ganoderma tsugae. Look for glossy varnished red-brown shelf on a stalk or the side of a tree; shiny, varnished-looking top. Use it as an identification lesson. Yehsa does not give doses or remedies with wild fungi."},{"symbol":"honey_mushroom","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/entries_fungi.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"e4b81a1a","text":"Honey mushrooms are clusters of honey-colored capped mushrooms with a ring that grow on wood and roots. Also called Armillaria, honey fungus, bootlace fungus. Look for clusters of honey-coloured caps on wood with a ring; white spores. Use it as an identification and look-alike lesson; only an expert who sees it in person can confirm it. The deadly galerina looks like it."},{"symbol":"shaggy_mane","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/entries_fungi.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"48069e31","text":"Shaggy mane is a tall white shaggy-capped mushroom that melts into black ink as it ages. Also called Coprinus comatus, lawyer's wig, inky cap. Look for tall white cylinder cap with shaggy scales that melts into ink; gills turn black and dissolve from the bottom up. Use it as an identification and look-alike lesson; only an expert who sees it in person can confirm it."},{"symbol":"bolete","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/entries_fungi.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"9d5e0a15","text":"A bolete is a fleshy mushroom with a sponge of pores instead of gills under the cap. Also called Boletus, boletes, porcini, king bolete. Look for fat stem with a sponge of pores instead of gills under the cap; pores instead of gills under the cap. Use it as an identification and look-alike lesson; only an expert who sees it in person can confirm it. Red-pored and bitter ones are toxic. Do not eat on a photo."},{"symbol":"green_spored_parasol","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/entries_fungi.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"f585b405","text":"The green-spored parasol is a large lawn mushroom whose gills turn green with age, and it causes the most mushroom poisonings in North America. Also called Chlorophyllum molybdites, false parasol, green-spored lepiota, vomiter. Look for big scaly cap with a ring; gills turn green with age. Learn it as a warning. The most common mushroom poisoning in North America."},{"symbol":"hedgehog_mushroom","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/entries_fungi.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"5af3cf92","text":"The hedgehog mushroom is a pale orange mushroom with soft spines under the cap instead of gills. Also called Hydnum repandum, sweet tooth, hedgehog. Look for pale orange cap with soft spines instead of gills; small spines under the cap instead of gills. Use it as an identification and look-alike lesson; only an expert who sees it in person can confirm it."},{"symbol":"tinder_fungus","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/entries_fungi.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"731ff7ad","text":"Tinder fungus is a hoof-shaped gray woody shelf on birch and beech whose inner layer was used to carry fire. Also called horse hoof fungus, Fomes fomentarius, amadou, hoof fungus. Look for hoof-shaped grey woody shelf on birch and beech; used for tinder. Use it as an identification lesson. Yehsa does not give doses or remedies with wild fungi. Survival use for fire only."},{"symbol":"chaga","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/entries_fungi.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"4b518419","text":"Chaga is a black, cracked, charcoal-like growth on birch that is orange-brown inside. Also called Inonotus obliquus, clinker fungus, birch conk. Look for black charcoal-like crust on birch with an orange inside; cracked black outer crust. Use it as an identification lesson. Yehsa does not give doses or remedies with wild fungi."},{"symbol":"ramps","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/entries_plants.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"bfb048ee","text":"Ramps are wild leeks with two or three broad smooth leaves in spring and a strong onion-garlic smell. Also called wild leek, Allium tricoccum, ramp. Look for two or three broad smooth leaves per plant in spring, red-purple stalk base, strong onion and garlic smell; every leaf smells of onion when crushed."},{"symbol":"false_hellebore","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/entries_plants.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"1c2f12f4","text":"False hellebore is a wetland plant with large pleated leaves spiraling up the stem, and it is deadly. Also called Indian poke, Veratrum, green hellebore, corn lily, Veratrum viride. Look for big pleated leaves that wrap the stem in a spiral; wet woods and meadows. Learn it so you can avoid it and warn others. Sometimes mistaken for ramps. Deadly."},{"symbol":"lily_of_valley","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/entries_plants.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"6f285a05","text":"Lily of the valley is a garden and wild plant with small white bell flowers, and every part is deadly to the heart. Also called lily of the valley, Convallaria, May bells, Convallaria majalis. Look for two smooth upright leaves, small white bells on an arching stem, red berries; no onion smell, unlike ramps. Learn it so you can avoid it and warn others. Mistaken for ramps. Every part is deadly."},{"symbol":"chicory","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/entries_plants.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"67c9a9b5","text":"Chicory is a roadside plant with sky-blue flowers on stiff stems whose leaves and roasted roots are used as food. Also called blue sailors, Cichorium, coffee weed, succory, Cichorium intybus. Look for sky blue flowers on stiff, nearly leafless stems along roadsides; milky sap."},{"symbol":"red_clover","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/entries_plants.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"9f46ea38","text":"Red clover is a common field plant with round pink-purple flower heads and three leaflets marked with a pale V. Also called Trifolium pratense, clover. Look for pink-purple round flower heads; three leaflets with a pale V mark. Learn it as an identification first. Eat it only after you have matched every feature in person, ideally with someone who knows the plant, from ground that was not sprayed or beside a busy road, and start with a small amount."},{"symbol":"white_clover","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/entries_plants.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"a0187bb3","text":"White clover is a low creeping lawn plant with round white flower heads and three leaflets. Also called Trifolium repens, Dutch clover, lawn clover. Look for low creeping plant, white round flower heads, three leaflets often with a pale band; stems creep and root along the ground."},{"symbol":"burdock","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/entries_plants.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"6861da8d","text":"Burdock is a large weed with huge heart-shaped leaves and hooked burrs that stick to clothing; its first-year root is eaten cooked. Also called Arctium, gobo, great burdock, beggar's buttons. Look for very large heart-shaped leaves, hooked burr heads, purple thistle-like flowers; leaves pale and woolly underneath."},{"symbol":"foxglove","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/entries_plants.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"009be02b","text":"Foxglove is a garden and wild plant with a tall spike of tubular pink-purple flowers, and every part is deadly to the heart. Also called Digitalis, fairy gloves, purple foxglove, Digitalis purpurea. Look for fuzzy leaves in a rosette, tall spike of tubular pink-purple bells; spotted throat inside each bell. Learn it so you can avoid it and warn others. Deadly to the heart. Often confused with comfrey in the first year."},{"symbol":"comfrey","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/entries_plants.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"20ffe845","text":"Comfrey is a hairy-leaved garden plant with curled clusters of bell flowers that contains liver-damaging compounds. Also called Symphytum, knitbone, boneset, Symphytum officinale. Look for rough hairy leaves, bell flowers in curled clusters; leaves run down onto the stem as wings. Learn it so you can avoid it and warn others. Contains liver-damaging compounds. Do not eat or brew it."},{"symbol":"iris_flag","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/entries_plants.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"f3906335","text":"Wild iris, or blue flag, is a wetland plant with flat sword-shaped leaves in a fan, and it is poisonous. Also called blue flag, yellow flag, flag iris, Iris versicolor. Look for flat sword-shaped leaves in a fan; blue-violet or yellow flowers. Learn it so you can avoid it and warn others. Toxic if eaten. Often mistaken for cattail shoots."},{"symbol":"wild_strawberry","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/entries_plants.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"5a565a4f","text":"Wild strawberry is a low native plant whose tiny red berries carry their seeds on the outside and taste like small garden strawberries. Also called Fragaria, woodland strawberry, wild strawberries, Fragaria virginiana. Look for three toothed leaflets, white five-petal flowers, tiny red berries with seeds on the outside; runners that root along the ground."},{"symbol":"brambles","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/entries_plants.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"3278b7b3","text":"Brambles are the prickly canes of blackberries and raspberries, whose berries are built from many small beads. Also called blackberry, raspberry, Rubus, blackcap, dewberry. Look for arching prickly canes, toothed compound leaves, clustered berries made of tiny beads; raspberries pull off hollow like a cap; blackberries keep a white core."},{"symbol":"blueberry","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/entries_plants.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"2320e399","text":"Lowbush blueberry is a small shrub of sunny barrens and woods whose blue berries end in a five-point crown. Also called lowbush blueberry, wild blueberry, huckleberry, Vaccinium angustifolium. Look for low shrub, small oval leaves, blue berries with a five-point crown at the tip; many tiny seeds inside, not one stone."},{"symbol":"cranberry","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/entries_plants.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"14f78462","text":"Cranberry is a trailing evergreen vine of bogs whose sour red berries ripen in fall. Also called wild cranberry, bog cranberry, Vaccinium macrocarpon. Look for trailing evergreen vine in bogs, tiny leaves, red berries; berries bounce and have air pockets inside."},{"symbol":"elderberry","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/entries_plants.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"fbe5702f","text":"Elderberry is a shrub with flat clusters of tiny white flowers and dark purple berries that are only safe to eat cooked and ripe. Also called elder, Sambucus, black elderberry, elderflower, Sambucus canadensis. Look for opposite compound leaves, flat clusters of white flowers, dark purple (or red) berries, pithy stems; stems have soft white pith inside."},{"symbol":"pokeweed","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/entries_plants.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"bcbcc7a0","text":"Pokeweed is a tall plant with purple-red stems and drooping clusters of dark berries, and its berries, roots, and mature leaves are poisonous. Also called poke, pokeberry, poke salad, inkberry, Phytolacca. Look for purple-red stems, large leaves, drooping clusters of dark purple berries; can grow taller than a person in one season. Learn it so you can avoid it and warn others. Berries, roots and mature leaves are toxic."},{"symbol":"staghorn_sumac","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/entries_plants.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"398cca3b","text":"Staghorn sumac is a shrub with velvety twigs and upright cones of red fuzzy berries; it is not poison sumac. Also called red sumac, sumac berries, sumac lemonade, Rhus typhina. Look for fuzzy velvet twigs, long compound leaves, upright red fuzzy berry cones; berries are red and point up, never white and drooping."},{"symbol":"virginia_creeper","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/entries_plants.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"2e7bc280","text":"Virginia creeper is a climbing vine with five leaflets spread like a hand, often confused with poison ivy, and its berries are poisonous. Also called five-leaved ivy, woodbine, Parthenocissus, Parthenocissus quinquefolia. Look for five leaflets spread like a hand; climbing vine with tendrils. Learn it so you can avoid it and warn others. Berries are toxic. Sometimes mistaken for poison ivy."},{"symbol":"stinging_nettle","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/entries_plants.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"0dd71e12","text":"Stinging nettle is a plant covered in fine stinging hairs that burn the skin on contact; cooking or drying removes the sting. Also called nettle, nettles, Urtica, Urtica dioica. Look for opposite toothed leaves with stinging hairs, square stem, small green flower strings; a burning, tingling rash right after you brush it."},{"symbol":"lambsquarters","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/entries_plants.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"5e10b9e9","text":"Lambsquarters is a common garden weed whose diamond-shaped leaves have a white mealy coating underneath. Also called goosefoot, pigweed, wild spinach, Chenopodium, Chenopodium album. Look for diamond-shaped leaves with a white mealy powder underneath; water beads on the leaves."},{"symbol":"wood_sorrel","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/entries_plants.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"15921c49","text":"Wood sorrel is a small plant with heart-shaped leaflets in threes and a sour, lemony taste from oxalic acid. Also called sour grass, Oxalis, yellow wood sorrel, sheep sorrel, Oxalis stricta. Look for heart-shaped leaflets in threes like a clover, small yellow or pink five-petal flowers; sour taste."},{"symbol":"jewelweed","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/entries_plants.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"471d230e","text":"Jewelweed is a soft wet-ground plant with orange spotted trumpet flowers and seed pods that burst when touched. Also called spotted touch-me-not, touch-me-not, Impatiens capensis. Look for orange or yellow spotted trumpet flowers, pale watery stems, leaves bead up water; seed pods burst when touched."},{"symbol":"bracken","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/entries_plants.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"e2d633a2","text":"Bracken is a large common fern with triangular fronds; it is toxic and not a safe fiddlehead. Also called bracken fern, brake fern, Pteridium, Pteridium aquilinum. Look for large triangular fronds on a single stalk, fuzzy coiled shoots, open ground; young shoots are fuzzy, not smooth and grooved. Learn it so you can avoid it and warn others. Toxic and linked to cancer. Do not eat."},{"symbol":"common_milkweed","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/entries_plants.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"9778a209","text":"Common milkweed is a tall plant with milky sap, broad paired leaves, and warty pods, and it is toxic raw. Also called milkweed, Asclepias syriaca, monarch plant. Look for thick stem with milky white sap, big opposite oval leaves, pink ball flower clusters, warty pods; pods full of silky seeds in fall. Learn it so you can avoid it and warn others. Toxic raw. Only a trained forager should use it, and not by photo."},{"symbol":"dogbane","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/entries_plants.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"c5704591","text":"Dogbane is a milky-sapped plant with red-tinged stems and slim paired pods that is poisonous to the heart and looks like milkweed. Also called Indian hemp, hemp dogbane, Apocynum, Apocynum cannabinum. Look for red-tinged stems with milky sap, pointed opposite leaves, tiny white bells, slim paired pods; branches near the top, unlike young milkweed. Learn it so you can avoid it and warn others. Toxic to the heart. Looks like milkweed."},{"symbol":"mullein","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/entries_plants.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"d6addbbe","text":"Mullein is a tall roadside plant with large, soft, gray, fuzzy leaves and a yellow flower spike in its second year. Also called common mullein, flannel leaf, cowboy toilet paper, Verbascum, Verbascum thapsus. Look for large grey fuzzy leaves in a rosette and a tall yellow flower spike in the second year; dry stalks stand through winter."},{"symbol":"st_johns_wort","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/entries_plants.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"5f435cde","text":"St. John's wort is a yellow-flowered plant whose leaves show tiny see-through dots when held to the light. Also called Hypericum, St Johns wort, goatweed, Hypericum perforatum. Look for opposite small leaves with see-through dots when held to light; yellow five-petal flowers that stain purple."},{"symbol":"self_heal","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/entries_plants.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"e10ccb78","text":"Self-heal is a low lawn and trail plant with a square stem and a spiky purple flower head. Also called heal-all, Prunella, selfheal, Prunella vulgaris. Look for low plant, square stem, spiky purple flower head with ruffled lips; opposite leaves on a square stem. Older manuals and folk use mention it, but Yehsa does not give doses or treatments with wild plants. Use it as an identification lesson, and use tested first-aid supplies for care."},{"symbol":"goldenrod","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/entries_plants.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"4760b2e3","text":"Goldenrod is a tall late-summer plant with plumes of small yellow flowers; it is often blamed for hay fever caused by ragweed. Also called Solidago, goldenrods. Look for tall plume of tiny yellow flowers in late summer, alternate narrow leaves; stands in old fields and roadsides by the thousands. Older manuals and folk use mention it, but Yehsa does not give doses or treatments with wild plants. Use it as an identification lesson, and use tested first-aid supplies for care."},{"symbol":"white_snakeroot","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/entries_plants.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"4fc221b5","text":"White snakeroot is a woodland plant with fuzzy white fall flowers that poisons livestock and can pass into their milk. Also called snakeroot, Ageratina, milk sickness plant, Ageratina altissima. Look for opposite toothed leaves on a branching stem, fuzzy white flower clusters in autumn; grows in shade at woods edges. Learn it so you can avoid it and warn others. Poisons livestock and milk. Do not eat."},{"symbol":"jerusalem_artichoke","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/entries_plants.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"7f56ebfe","text":"Jerusalem artichoke is a tall wild sunflower whose knobby underground tubers are edible. Also called sunchoke, sunroot, Helianthus tuberosus. Look for tall rough sunflower-like plant with small yellow flowers and knobby tubers; leaves rough like sandpaper."},{"symbol":"evening_primrose","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/entries_plants.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"8ef82500","text":"Evening primrose is a tall plant with four-petal yellow flowers that open at dusk. Also called Oenothera, common evening primrose, Oenothera biennis. Look for tall plant with four-petal yellow flowers that open at dusk, reddish stem; first-year rosette with a pink-tinged midrib."},{"symbol":"pineapple_weed","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/entries_plants.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"b371e68a","text":"Pineapple weed is a low weed of packed paths whose petal-less yellow-green flower cones smell like pineapple when crushed. Also called wild chamomile, disc mayweed, Matricaria discoidea. Look for low plant with feathery leaves, yellow-green cone flowers with no petals; smells like pineapple when crushed."},{"symbol":"wild_bergamot","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/entries_plants.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"3cfc28f2","text":"Wild bergamot is a mint-family wildflower with lavender shaggy flower heads and an oregano-like smell. Also called bee balm, Monarda, horsemint, Monarda fistulosa. Look for square stem, lavender shaggy flower heads, minty oregano smell; square stem and paired leaves like other mints. Older manuals and folk use mention it, but Yehsa does not give doses or treatments with wild plants. Use it as an identification lesson, and use tested first-aid supplies for care."},{"symbol":"mayapple","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/entries_plants.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"13fe7023","text":"Mayapple is a spring woodland plant with umbrella-like leaves, and every part except the fully ripe fruit is poisonous. Also called American mandrake, Podophyllum, umbrella plant, Podophyllum peltatum. Look for umbrella-like lobed leaves, a single white flower under the leaves, one green-yellow fruit; grows in large colonies on the forest floor. Learn it so you can avoid it and warn others. Everything except the ripe fruit is toxic."},{"symbol":"bloodroot","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/entries_plants.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"712f1725","text":"Bloodroot is an early-spring wildflower with one lobed leaf and a white flower whose root bleeds red-orange sap, and it is poisonous. Also called Sanguinaria, red puccoon, Sanguinaria canadensis. Look for one clasped rounded lobed leaf and a white eight-petal flower in early spring; red-orange sap. Learn it so you can avoid it and warn others. Toxic."},{"symbol":"baneberry","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/entries_plants.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"5e3bf7a3","text":"Baneberry, or doll's eyes, is a woodland plant whose white or red berries on thick red stalks are deadly. Also called doll's eyes, white baneberry, red baneberry, Actaea, Actaea pachypoda. Look for compound toothed leaves, a white or red berry cluster on thick red stalks (doll's eyes); each white berry has a black dot like an eye. Learn it so you can avoid it and warn others. Berries are deadly."},{"symbol":"jimsonweed","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/entries_plants.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"225d5cda","text":"Jimsonweed is a rank-smelling weed with large white trumpet flowers and spiny seed pods, and every part is deadly. Also called Datura, thorn apple, devil's trumpet, locoweed, Datura stramonium. Look for large white trumpet flowers, spiny egg-shaped seed pods, strong smell; grows in disturbed ground and barnyards. Learn it so you can avoid it and warn others. Every part is deadly."},{"symbol":"bittersweet_nightshade","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/entries_plants.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"283ecceb","text":"Bittersweet nightshade is a climbing vine with small purple star flowers and red egg-shaped berries that are poisonous. Also called climbing nightshade, woody nightshade, Solanum dulcamara. Look for climbing vine, purple flowers with a yellow cone, red egg-shaped berries; green berries turn orange, then red. Learn it so you can avoid it and warn others. Berries are toxic."},{"symbol":"monkshood","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/entries_plants.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"4786802e","text":"Monkshood is a garden and wild plant with hooded blue-purple flowers, and every part is deadly, even through the skin. Also called Aconitum, wolfsbane, aconite. Look for deeply cut leaves and tall spikes of hooded blue-purple flowers; top petal shaped like a monk's hood. Learn it so you can avoid it and warn others. Deadly. Even skin contact can numb or harm."},{"symbol":"giant_hogweed","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/entries_plants.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"38e7cdef","text":"Giant hogweed is a huge carrot-family plant whose sap, with sunlight, causes severe burns and blisters on skin. Also called hogweed, Heracleum mantegazzianum. Look for huge: stem 5-10 cm wide with purple blotches and stiff hairs, umbrella flower head up to 1 m; can be taller than a person, with a flower umbrella over a meter across. Learn it so you can avoid it and warn others. Sap plus sunlight causes severe burns. Do not touch."},{"symbol":"cow_parsnip","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/entries_plants.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"a74e46e4","text":"Cow parsnip is a large native carrot-family plant with maple-like leaves whose sap can burn sunlit skin. Also called Heracleum maximum, Indian celery. Look for big maple-like leaves, white flat umbrella, hairy hollow stem; smaller than giant hogweed, with a greener, less blotched stem. Learn it so you can avoid it and warn others. Sap can cause burns on sunny skin."},{"symbol":"wild_parsnip","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/entries_plants.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"1d4d4e20","text":"Wild parsnip is a yellow-flowered carrot-family roadside plant whose sap, with sunlight, causes burns and blisters. Also called Pastinaca, poison parsnip, Pastinaca sativa. Look for yellow umbrella flowers, grooved hollow stem, toothed leaflets; flat yellow umbrella flowers in midsummer. Learn it so you can avoid it and warn others. Sap plus sunlight causes burns and blisters."},{"symbol":"skunk_cabbage","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/entries_plants.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"8e36dfe7","text":"Skunk cabbage is a wetland plant with a mottled hooded flower in late winter and big leaves that smell skunky when torn; it is toxic raw. Also called Symplocarpus, swamp cabbage, eastern skunk cabbage, Symplocarpus foetidus. Look for wet ground; mottled purple-maroon hooded flower in late winter. Learn it so you can avoid it and warn others. Toxic raw."},{"symbol":"japanese_knotweed","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/entries_plants.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"6a9c2af2","text":"Japanese knotweed is an invasive plant with hollow, bamboo-like jointed canes that forms dense stands. Also called knotweed, Fallopia japonica, Mexican bamboo, Reynoutria, Reynoutria japonica. Look for hollow bamboo-like reddish canes, heart-shaped leaves, dense stands; zigzag stems with a leaf at each joint."},{"symbol":"curly_dock","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/entries_plants.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"40c76419","text":"Curly dock is a common weed with long, wavy-edged leaves and a tall rust-red seed stalk. Also called yellow dock, sour dock, Rumex crispus, dock. Look for long leaves with wavy ruffled edges, tall rust-red seed stalk; papery sheath where each leaf meets the stem."},{"symbol":"wintergreen","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/entries_plants.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"a5fd1368","text":"Wintergreen is a low evergreen ground plant whose glossy leaves smell of wintergreen when crushed and whose berries are red. Also called teaberry, checkerberry, Gaultheria, Gaultheria procumbens. Look for low evergreen with glossy leaves that smell of wintergreen when crushed; red berries."},{"symbol":"wild_rose","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/entries_plants.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"849a501b","text":"Wild rose is a prickly shrub with five-petal pink flowers that leave red rose hips in fall. Also called rose hips, pasture rose, Rosa, multiflora rose, Rosa carolina. Look for prickly canes, five-petal pink flowers, red rose hips; hips stay on the canes into winter."},{"symbol":"hawthorn","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/entries_plants.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"1e6ae659","text":"Hawthorn is a thorny small tree with lobed leaves and small red apple-like fruits called haws. Also called Crataegus, thornapple, haws, whitethorn. Look for thorny small tree or shrub with lobed leaves and red apple-like haws; long straight thorns on the twigs."},{"symbol":"mountain_laurel","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/entries_plants.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"593c2d18","text":"Mountain laurel is an evergreen shrub with clusters of pink-white cup flowers, and its leaves, flowers, and honey are poisonous. Also called Kalmia, calico bush, spoonwood, Kalmia latifolia. Look for evergreen glossy leaves, clusters of pink-white cup flowers; forms dense thickets on rocky slopes. Learn it so you can avoid it and warn others. Leaves, flowers and honey from it are toxic."},{"symbol":"sphagnum","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/entries_plants.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"7461684a","text":"Sphagnum is the spongy bog moss that holds many times its weight in water. Also called peat moss, bog moss, sphagnum moss, Sphagnum. Look for pale green to red spongy moss in bogs that holds a lot of water; grows in bogs in green, red, or brown carpets."},{"symbol":"usnea","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/entries_plants.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"c5c73c65","text":"Usnea, or old man's beard, is a pale green hair-like lichen on branches whose strands have a white elastic core. Also called old man's beard, beard lichen, Usnea lichen, Usnea. Look for pale green hair-like lichen on branches; pull it and the core is a white elastic thread. Older manuals and folk use mention it, but Yehsa does not give doses or treatments with wild plants. Use it as an identification lesson, and use tested first-aid supplies for care."},{"symbol":"cleavers","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/entries_plants.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"76510756","text":"Cleavers is a sprawling plant whose square stems and narrow whorled leaves stick to clothing with tiny hooks. Also called bedstraw, stickyweed, goosegrass, Galium, Galium aparine. Look for sprawling square stem with whorls of narrow leaves that cling by tiny hooks; sticks to your sleeve when you brush it."},{"symbol":"moonseed","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/entries_plants.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"53c27e2f","text":"Canada moonseed is a climbing vine with grape-like berries that are deadly; each berry holds one crescent-shaped seed. Also called moonseed vine, Menispermum, yellow parilla, Menispermum canadense. Look for climbing vine with big lobed leaves, dark blue-black berries each with a single crescent seed; no tendrils, unlike grape. Learn it so you can avoid it and warn others. Looks like wild grape. Grapes have several seeds and tendrils. Moonseed has neither."},{"symbol":"wild_grape","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/entries_plants.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"01f16f85","text":"Wild grape is a climbing vine with tendrils, toothed lobed leaves, and bunches of small dark grapes. Also called riverbank grape, fox grape, Vitis, Vitis riparia. Look for climbing vine with tendrils, toothed lobed leaves, round berries in a bunch; shreddy bark on older vines."},{"symbol":"thistle","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/entries_plants.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"9695e36d","text":"Thistle is a spiny plant with round purple flower heads whose peeled young stems and roots are edible. Also called bull thistle, Cirsium, spear thistle, Cirsium vulgare. Look for spiny leaves and stem, round purple flower heads; fluffy seed heads in late summer."},{"symbol":"spurge","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/entries_plants.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"e08aba0a","text":"Spotted spurge is a flat mat-forming weed whose milky sap irritates skin and which is toxic if eaten. Also called Euphorbia, spotted spurge, prostrate spurge, Euphorbia maculata. Look for flat mat of small oval leaves with a dark spot, milky sap; thin wiry stems, not fat and succulent. Learn it so you can avoid it and warn others. Sap irritates. Mistaken for purslane."},{"symbol":"purslane","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/entries_plants.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"1c91ce1b","text":"Purslane is a low succulent weed with thick paddle-shaped leaves and reddish stems, a common edible green. Also called Portulaca, pigweed, verdolaga, little hogweed, Portulaca oleracea. Look for flat mat of thick succulent paddle leaves with reddish stems; clear sap."},{"symbol":"chickweed","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/entries_plants.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"dd103b03","text":"Chickweed is a small, soft, sprawling weed with tiny white star flowers and a single line of hairs along the stem. Also called Stellaria media, common chickweed, starweed. Look for low plant with small oval leaves and one line of hairs along the stem; tiny white deep-notched petals."},{"symbol":"common_mallow","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/entries_plants.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"a6459c46","text":"Common mallow is a low weed with round lobed leaves and small pale pink flowers that leave cheese-wheel shaped seeds. Also called cheeseweed, Malva, buttonweed, Malva neglecta. Look for round lobed leaves on long stalks, small pale pink flowers, round cheese-wheel fruits; slimy texture when the leaves are chewed or crushed."},{"symbol":"garlic_mustard","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/entries_plants.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"787dde99","text":"Garlic mustard is an invasive woodland plant whose heart-shaped leaves smell of garlic when crushed. Also called Alliaria, hedge garlic, jack-by-the-hedge, Alliaria petiolata. Look for heart-shaped toothed leaves smelling of garlic, small white four-petal flowers; first-year rosettes stay green through winter."},{"symbol":"common_juniper","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/entries_trees.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"a9eeab73","text":"Common juniper is a low, spreading evergreen shrub with sharp needles in threes and blue berry-like cones. Also called juniper, Juniperus communis, juniper berries. Look for low spreading evergreen shrub with sharp needles in threes and blue berry-like cones; needles have a white stripe on top. Knowing the tree helps you pick firewood, shelter sites, and landmarks. Do not mix it up with yew, which is deadly."},{"symbol":"yew","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/entries_trees.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"8d55f4df","text":"Canada yew is a low evergreen shrub with flat needles and red fleshy berries whose seeds, needles, and bark are deadly. Also called Canada yew, ground hemlock, Taxus, yew berries, Taxus canadensis. Look for flat dark green needles with two pale lines underneath, red fleshy cup berries; low shrub. Knowing the tree helps you pick firewood, shelter sites, and landmarks. Needles, seeds and bark are deadly."},{"symbol":"balsam_fir","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/entries_trees.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"6dcfdf0c","text":"Balsam fir is a northern evergreen with flat, blunt, fragrant needles and resin blisters on smooth bark. Also called fir, Abies balsamea, Christmas tree fir, balsam. Look for flat blunt needles with two white lines underneath, upright cones, smooth bark with resin blisters, spicy smell; cones sit upright on top branches. Resin blisters burn well as fire starter, and boughs make soft bedding."},{"symbol":"red_spruce","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/entries_trees.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"c467d4a6","text":"Red spruce is a northern evergreen with stiff, square needles that roll between the fingers and small hanging cones. Also called spruce, Picea rubens, eastern spruce. Look for square four-sided stiff needles that roll between your fingers, hanging cones, reddish scaly bark; sharp needles on small woody pegs. Spruce boughs make shelter roofing and bedding; resin helps start fires."},{"symbol":"white_spruce","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/entries_trees.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"97e86c7f","text":"White spruce is a northern evergreen with blue-green four-sided needles that smell strong when crushed. Also called Picea glauca, cat spruce, skunk spruce. Look for four-sided blue-green needles, slim hanging cones; needles smell strongly when crushed. Spruce boughs make shelter roofing and bedding; spruce roots make cordage."},{"symbol":"black_spruce","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/entries_trees.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"9e9bdd95","text":"Black spruce is a slim bog spruce with short needles and small round cones that stay on the tree for years. Also called bog spruce, Picea mariana. Look for bog tree with a thin top, short needles, small round cones that stay on the tree; club-like dense top. Its presence usually means wet, boggy ground, so plan a route around it."},{"symbol":"white_pine","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/entries_trees.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"deb8d0fa","text":"Eastern white pine is a tall evergreen with soft needles in bundles of five and long slim cones. Also called white pine, Pinus strobus, eastern pine. Look for soft needles in bundles of five, long slim cones, tiered branches; one needle bundle has five needles, like the letters in white. Pitch from wounds is a fire starter, and inner bark is a famine food."},{"symbol":"red_pine","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/entries_trees.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"5976164b","text":"Red pine is an evergreen with long needles in pairs that snap cleanly when bent and orange-red flaky bark. Also called Norway pine, Pinus resinosa. Look for long needles in bundles of two that snap cleanly, orange-red flaky bark; often in straight planted rows. Dead lower limbs are dry fuel, and fatwood makes strong tinder."},{"symbol":"eastern_hemlock","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/entries_trees.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"ed128f9c","text":"Eastern hemlock is a shade-loving evergreen tree with short flat needles; it is not the poisonous hemlock plants. Also called hemlock tree, Tsuga canadensis, Canada hemlock. Look for flat short needles with two white lines underneath on tiny stalks, tiny cones, drooping top; tiny cones at the branch tips. Dense hemlock gives good shelter from wind and snow. The tree is not the poison hemlock plants. Poison and water hemlock are herbs with umbrella flowers."},{"symbol":"eastern_red_cedar","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/entries_trees.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"12eecf27","text":"Eastern red cedar is a juniper tree with scaly leaves, blue berry-like cones, and fragrant reddish wood. Also called red cedar, Juniperus virginiana, aromatic cedar. Look for scaly green leaves or sharp awls, blue berry-like cones, shredding reddish bark; narrow cone shape in old fields. Shredded bark makes good tinder."},{"symbol":"northern_white_cedar","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/entries_trees.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"b4216109","text":"Northern white cedar is an evergreen with flat fans of scale-like leaves and stringy bark, common in wet ground. Also called arborvitae, Thuja, swamp cedar, Thuja occidentalis. Look for flat fans of scale-like leaves, small cones, stringy bark; wet ground. Its inner bark makes cordage and tinder."},{"symbol":"tamarack","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/entries_trees.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"64b86f21","text":"Tamarack is a larch, a conifer whose soft tufted needles turn gold and drop each fall. Also called larch, eastern larch, Larix laricina, hackmatack. Look for soft needles in tufts that turn gold and drop in autumn; bare in winter, unlike other conifers. It marks wet, boggy ground."},{"symbol":"sugar_maple","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/entries_trees.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"c4e38a47","text":"Sugar maple is a hardwood with five-lobed leaves and rounded gaps, tapped in late winter for maple syrup. Also called hard maple, rock maple, Acer saccharum. Look for five-lobed leaf with smooth rounded gaps, opposite branches, grey furrowed bark; winged seeds in pairs. It is excellent firewood and the classic syrup tree."},{"symbol":"red_maple","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/entries_trees.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"000a7d1d","text":"Red maple is a common hardwood with three- to five-lobed toothed leaves and red twigs, buds, and fall color. Also called swamp maple, soft maple, Acer rubrum. Look for three- to five-lobed leaf with toothed edges and sharp gaps, red twigs and buds; red flowers before the leaves in spring. Its sap can be boiled for syrup too, with a lower sugar content."},{"symbol":"paper_birch","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/entries_trees.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"94c1286f","text":"Paper birch is a hardwood with white bark that peels in papery sheets and burns even when damp. Also called white birch, canoe birch, Betula papyrifera. Look for white bark peeling in papery sheets with dark marks; toothed oval leaves. Peel bark only from dead or downed trees; it is one of the best natural tinders."},{"symbol":"yellow_birch","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/entries_trees.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"601f182b","text":"Yellow birch is a hardwood with silver-yellow bark peeling in curly strips and twigs that smell of wintergreen. Also called Betula alleghaniensis, gold birch. Look for silver-yellow bark that peels in curly strips; twigs smell of wintergreen when scratched. Curly bark shreds catch a spark and burn wet."},{"symbol":"american_beech","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/entries_trees.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"e4b0c065","text":"American beech is a hardwood with smooth gray bark and pale papery leaves that cling through winter. Also called beech, Fagus grandifolia, beechnut tree. Look for smooth blue-grey bark, toothed oval leaves with straight veins, pale leaves that stay in winter; long pointed cigar-shaped buds. Its small triangular beechnuts are edible in fall."},{"symbol":"white_oak","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/entries_trees.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"12bded9b","text":"White oak is a hardwood with rounded leaf lobes and pale, scaly bark. Also called Quercus alba, white oaks. Look for rounded leaf lobes, pale scaly bark; acorns mature in one year and are less bitter. Acorns need leaching before eating."},{"symbol":"red_oak","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/entries_trees.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"c641f6a5","text":"Northern red oak is a hardwood with pointed, bristle-tipped leaf lobes and ridged bark with shiny stripes. Also called northern red oak, Quercus rubra. Look for pointed leaf lobes with bristle tips, ridged bark with pale stripes; acorns take two years to ripen and are bitter. Acorns need long leaching before eating."},{"symbol":"american_elm","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/entries_trees.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"d41c5d2a","text":"American elm is a hardwood with lopsided, double-toothed leaves and a vase-shaped crown. Also called elm, Ulmus americana, white elm. Look for leaves with uneven bases and double teeth, vase shape; many killed by Dutch elm disease. Inner bark makes cordage."},{"symbol":"white_ash","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/entries_trees.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"bf531304","text":"White ash is a hardwood with opposite compound leaves and bark ridged in a diamond pattern. Also called ash, Fraxinus americana, American ash. Look for opposite compound leaves, diamond-pattern bark; opposite branches, unlike most trees. Ash splits easily and burns well even when fairly green."},{"symbol":"quaking_aspen","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/entries_trees.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"a98b10d4","text":"Quaking aspen is a hardwood with round leaves that tremble on flat stalks and smooth pale green-white bark. Also called aspen, trembling aspen, popple, Populus tremuloides. Look for round leaves on flat stalks that tremble, smooth pale green-white bark; grows in clones of many trunks. Its bark rubs off a white powder some use as sunblock in a pinch."},{"symbol":"black_cherry","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/entries_trees.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"0f7e9632","text":"Black cherry is a hardwood with dark, flaky bark like burnt potato chips; its wilted leaves, pits, and bark are poisonous. Also called wild black cherry, Prunus serotina, rum cherry. Look for dark scaly bark like burnt potato chips, glossy toothed leaves, dark berries; almond smell when a twig is scratched. Only the ripe fruit flesh is eaten, never the pits. Leaves, pits and bark contain cyanide compounds. Wilted leaves are dangerous."},{"symbol":"shagbark_hickory","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/entries_trees.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"840c515d","text":"Shagbark hickory is a hardwood whose bark hangs in long loose plates and whose nuts are sweet and edible. Also called hickory, Carya ovata. Look for long loose plates of bark curling at the ends; compound leaves. Hickory is top firewood and smoking wood."},{"symbol":"basswood","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/entries_trees.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"6f67c27f","text":"Basswood is a hardwood with large lopsided heart-shaped leaves and fragrant summer flowers. Also called linden, lime tree, Tilia americana, bee tree. Look for large heart-shaped lopsided leaves, nut-like balls hanging from a leafy strip; nutlets hang from a leaf-like strip. Its inner bark is one of the best natural cordage fibers."},{"symbol":"black_walnut","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/entries_trees.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"ba2b4c49","text":"Black walnut is a hardwood with long compound leaves and round green-husked nuts whose meat is edible. Also called walnut, Juglans nigra. Look for compound leaves of many leaflets, dark ridged bark, round green husked nuts; husks stain hands dark brown. Its roots release a chemical that stunts many plants beneath it."},{"symbol":"sassafras","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/entries_trees.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"f1b939f0","text":"Sassafras is a small tree with three leaf shapes on one tree and a spicy, root-beer smell when crushed. Also called Sassafras albidum, mitten tree. Look for leaves of three shapes on one tree (oval, mitten, three-lobed); spicy smell when crushed. The root contains safrole, which FDA has banned as a food additive, so do not brew it."},{"symbol":"sycamore","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/entries_trees.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"81619d55","text":"American sycamore is a large streamside tree whose bark flakes off in white, green, and brown patches. Also called buttonwood, plane tree, Platanus occidentalis. Look for bark flaking off in patches of white and brown, big lobed leaves, ball fruits; white upper limbs visible from far away. It almost always grows near water."},{"symbol":"witch_hazel","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/entries_trees.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"aa23e51c","text":"Witch hazel is an understory shrub with wavy-edged leaves and yellow ribbon flowers in late fall. Also called Hamamelis, winterbloom, Hamamelis virginiana. Look for wavy-edged uneven leaves, yellow ribbon flowers in late autumn; flowers after the leaves drop. Knowing the tree helps you pick firewood, shelter sites, and landmarks."},{"symbol":"unknown_plant","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/notes.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"33cd9939","text":"Do not eat a plant you have not identified. Edible species can have toxic lookalikes. This is an offline caution, not a field guide or medical advice."},{"symbol":"plantain_plantago","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/notes.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"560d4fca","text":"Plantago, often called plantain, is a common lawn weed with parallel veins. Crushed plantain (Plantago) leaves are a traditional poultice for an itchy insect bite, and only after the plant is identified. Not a treatment."},{"symbol":"willow_bark","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/notes.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"18f392c1","text":"Willow bark (Salix) is traditionally used for aches, not as a medicine. Salicin from willow is described as an analgesic, and astringent tannins show up in the bark. Identify the tree before any use. This is not a dose or a substitute for emergency care."},{"symbol":"toxic_vs_edible","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/notes.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"0a6505ef","text":"Milky sap, bulb leaves, or umbrella flower clusters are reasons to stop and not taste a plant. Cultivated food is safer than foraging when identification is uncertain."},{"symbol":"yarrow_achillea","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/notes.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"c5d0823e","text":"Yarrow (Achillea millefolium) is a field plant for a small bleeding scrape, and only after the plant is identified. Feathery leaves and a flat white flower cluster are the usual look, and lookalikes are easy to mistake. Do not use a plant you have not identified. This is not a dose or a substitute for emergency care."},{"symbol":"hemlock_carrot_lookalike","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/notes.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"d22532ee","text":"Wild carrot (Queen Anne's lace) can look like poison hemlock and water hemlock, which are deadly. Poison hemlock has smooth purple-spotted stems and a musty smell. Water hemlock grows in wet ground and has chambered roots. Wild carrot often has one dark floret in the center and a hairy stem. Never taste an umbrella-flowered plant. A picture is not an identification. Call emergency services if any was eaten."},{"symbol":"camas_onion_lookalike","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/notes.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"41fcb69e","text":"Death camas is a toxic bulb that can look like a wild onion. A wild onion smells strongly of onion when the bulb or leaf is crushed. Death camas does not smell like onion and its flowers are not an onion umbel. If there is no onion smell, do not eat it. Call emergency services if a bulb was eaten. A picture is not an identification."},{"symbol":"toxic_plant_facts","source":"symbolic_books/herbology/notes.json","kind":"herbology","hash":"728d28cf","text":"Many toxic plants share milky sap, bulb leaves, or white umbrella flower clusters. Never taste a plant to identify it. A small amount of water hemlock, poison hemlock, or death camas can be deadly. Identify every part, and if anyone eats an unknown plant, call emergency services. This is not a dose."},{"symbol":"cumulonimbus_cloud","source":"symbolic_books/landscape/entries_weather.json","kind":"landscape","hash":"f85a5dd3","text":"A cumulonimbus is a very tall, dense storm cloud with a flat anvil top that produces thunderstorms, lightning, heavy rain, hail, and sometimes tornadoes. Also called thunderhead, thunderstorm cloud, anvil cloud, towering storm cloud. Look for towering cauliflower shape that reaches very high; flat, spreading anvil at the top."},{"symbol":"cumulus_cloud","source":"symbolic_books/landscape/entries_weather.json","kind":"landscape","hash":"b5b4e0d6","text":"Cumulus are puffy, flat-bottomed clouds with a cotton-ball look that usually mean fair weather unless they grow tall. Also called fair weather clouds, puffy clouds, cotton ball clouds. Look for distinct, rounded puffs with sharp edges; flat bases. Small, scattered cumulus mean fair weather. If they build taller through the day and darken, watch for afternoon showers or storms."},{"symbol":"stratus_cloud","source":"symbolic_books/landscape/entries_weather.json","kind":"landscape","hash":"fa4dda87","text":"Stratus is a flat, gray, featureless layer of low cloud, like fog that has lifted off the ground. Also called low gray clouds, overcast low sky, gray blanket cloud, high fog. Look for uniform gray layer with no shape; very low base, sometimes touching hills. Expect gloomy, damp, cool conditions with little wind and perhaps drizzle. Visibility is poor, so use care navigating."},{"symbol":"nimbostratus_cloud","source":"symbolic_books/landscape/entries_weather.json","kind":"landscape","hash":"d332a850","text":"Nimbostratus is a thick, dark gray layer that blocks the sun and brings steady rain or snow for hours. Also called rain clouds, steady rain cloud, dark gray rain layer. Look for dark, even gray sky with no visible sun; steady, continuous rain or snow. Plan for long, soaking rain: set up shelter, keep gear dry, and avoid low spots and dry washes that can flood."},{"symbol":"cirrus_cloud","source":"symbolic_books/landscape/entries_weather.json","kind":"landscape","hash":"3e336ad5","text":"Cirrus are thin, wispy, white streaks high in the sky made of ice crystals, and they often come before a change in weather. Also called mares tails, wispy high clouds, feathery clouds, ice crystal clouds. Look for thin white hair-like streaks; very high in a blue sky. A few mean fair weather. If they thicken and lower into a sheet over a day, a warm front and rain may follow in about 24 hours."},{"symbol":"cirrostratus_halo","source":"symbolic_books/landscape/entries_weather.json","kind":"landscape","hash":"df7d295a","text":"Cirrostratus is a thin, milky veil of high cloud that makes a ring (halo) around the sun or moon, and it often means rain or snow is coming within a day. Also called ring around the moon, halo around the sun, milky sky, sun dog. Look for bright ring around the sun or moon; thin, milky sheet across the sky. Expect weather to turn within roughly 12 to 24 hours. Use the time to prepare shelter and gear."},{"symbol":"altocumulus_cloud","source":"symbolic_books/landscape/entries_weather.json","kind":"landscape","hash":"d37c6d81","text":"Altocumulus are white or gray patches or rolls of cloud at mid-levels, and in a warm humid morning they can mean afternoon thunderstorms. Also called mackerel sky, sheep back clouds, patchy mid-level clouds, castellanus. Look for rows or patches of rounded cloudlets; mid-level, smaller than cumulus. If you see turrets or they appear on a warm, sticky morning, watch for storms by afternoon."},{"symbol":"altostratus_cloud","source":"symbolic_books/landscape/entries_weather.json","kind":"landscape","hash":"253aad3c","text":"Altostratus is a gray or blue-gray mid-level sheet through which the sun appears dimly, like looking through frosted glass. Also called watery sun, gray veil, sun looks like through ground glass. Look for gray sheet with a dim, watery sun; no halo. It usually thickens ahead of a warm front, so expect steady rain or snow within several hours."},{"symbol":"stratocumulus_cloud","source":"symbolic_books/landscape/entries_weather.json","kind":"landscape","hash":"eed5eeb5","text":"Stratocumulus are low, lumpy gray and white clouds in rolls or patches that cover much of the sky, often with little rain. Also called low lumpy gray clouds, rolls of low cloud. Look for rounded masses joined in rows; gray with brighter gaps. Usually dry or light drizzle at most. Conditions often clear toward evening."},{"symbol":"lenticular_cloud","source":"symbolic_books/landscape/entries_weather.json","kind":"landscape","hash":"664e5eff","text":"A lenticular is a smooth, lens-shaped cloud that sits over or downwind of mountains where strong winds flow in waves. Also called UFO cloud, lens shaped cloud, stacked pancake cloud, wave cloud. Look for smooth lens or stacked-plate shape; stays in one place while air streams through. Treat it as a sign of strong winds aloft and possibly rough air and gusty winds at the summit or downwind. Weather may be changing."},{"symbol":"mammatus_cloud","source":"symbolic_books/landscape/entries_weather.json","kind":"landscape","hash":"67bb18be","text":"Mammatus are pouch-like bulges that hang from the underside of a cloud, usually a thunderstorm anvil. Also called pouch clouds, bubbly cloud underside, udder clouds. Look for lumpy pouches under an anvil; often seen after a storm has passed. They mark a strong storm system. Stay alert for severe weather and move to shelter if a storm is still near."},{"symbol":"shelf_cloud","source":"symbolic_books/landscape/entries_weather.json","kind":"landscape","hash":"dee995cc","text":"A shelf cloud is a low, wedge-shaped wall of cloud on the leading edge of a thunderstorm's cold outflow, with strong straight-line winds right behind it. Also called arcus cloud, roll cloud, wall of cloud ahead of storm, storm front line. Look for long horizontal band with a smooth leading edge; dark, ragged cloud behind it. Take shelter now. Damaging gusts, heavy rain, and hail arrive within minutes of the shelf cloud."},{"symbol":"wall_cloud","source":"symbolic_books/landscape/entries_weather.json","kind":"landscape","hash":"516bca37","text":"A wall cloud is a localized lowering of the rain-free base of a thunderstorm, and rotation there can lead to a tornado; a funnel cloud is a rotating condensation funnel that has not reached the ground. Also called rotating cloud base, funnel cloud, lowering under a storm, wall cloud tornado. Look for a lowering that looks like a wall under the storm base; visible rotation or rapid upward motion."},{"symbol":"contrails_persistent","source":"symbolic_books/landscape/entries_weather.json","kind":"landscape","hash":"b23ff939","text":"Contrails are the trails of cloud behind high aircraft; if they stay long and spread, the high air is moist and weather may change. Also called vapor trails, airplane trails spreading, long contrails. Look for short, quickly vanishing contrails mean dry upper air; long lasting, spreading trails mean moist upper air. Use them as a hint of a coming change, along with other signs like wind shift and falling pressure."},{"symbol":"fog_types","source":"symbolic_books/landscape/entries_weather.json","kind":"landscape","hash":"0282f149","text":"Fog is a cloud at ground level; it forms when air cools to its dew point, most often on calm, clear nights or when moist air moves over a cold surface. Also called morning fog, mist, fog in valleys, fog on the water, low visibility. Look for radiation or valley fog: calm, clear night, fog in low spots; advection fog: warm moist air moving over cold water or land."},{"symbol":"thunderstorm_stages","source":"symbolic_books/landscape/entries_weather.json","kind":"landscape","hash":"8d81114f","text":"A thunderstorm grows from rising warm moist air, matures with heavy rain and lightning, and then weakens as downdrafts cut off the updraft. Also called how a thunderstorm forms, towering cumulus, downdraft, storm stages. Look for towering cumulus growing fast; anvil top and rain, hail, and lightning at maturity. Watch for fast-growing towers on warm afternoons and plan to be off ridges early. Storms often last 30 minutes to an hour but can repeat along a line."},{"symbol":"flash_to_bang","source":"symbolic_books/landscape/entries_weather.json","kind":"landscape","hash":"ae13852d","text":"Count the seconds from a lightning flash to its thunder; every five seconds is about one mile (three seconds is about one kilometer). Also called how far away is the lightning, count between flash and thunder, 30 30 rule, thunder distance. Look for see the flash, start counting; hear the thunder, stop. If you hear thunder, you are close enough to be struck. Go to safe shelter and wait 30 minutes after the last thunder."},{"symbol":"tornado_signs","source":"symbolic_books/landscape/entries_weather.json","kind":"landscape","hash":"94edd528","text":"Tornado signs are a dark, greenish sky, large hail, a rotating wall cloud, a loud roar, and a debris cloud at ground level. Also called how to know a tornado is coming, green sky, debris cloud, roaring sound like a freight train. Look for dark, green-tinged sky and large hail; rotating, lowering cloud base. If a tornado warning is issued or you see these signs, go to shelter now. A tornado watch means conditions are favorable, so stay alert."},{"symbol":"tornado_safety","source":"symbolic_books/landscape/entries_weather.json","kind":"landscape","hash":"f7832590","text":"In a tornado, get to the lowest floor of a sturdy building, in an interior room away from windows, and protect your head and neck. Also called what to do in a tornado, tornado shelter, basement during a tornado, caught outside in a tornado. Look for interior room, closet, or bathroom on the lowest floor; basement or storm shelter is best."},{"symbol":"efscale_tornado","source":"symbolic_books/landscape/entries_weather.json","kind":"landscape","hash":"2aafa7ea","text":"The EF scale rates a tornado from EF0 to EF5 by the damage it causes and the wind speeds estimated from it. Also called tornado rating, EF5, how strong is a tornado. Look for EF0: 65 to 85 mph, light damage; EF2: 111 to 135 mph, considerable damage. Use it to understand reports after a tornado. The rating is assigned after the damage survey, not in advance."},{"symbol":"hurricane_categories","source":"symbolic_books/landscape/entries_weather.json","kind":"landscape","hash":"04674641","text":"The Saffir-Simpson scale rates hurricanes from category 1 to 5 by sustained wind speed, but flooding and storm surge cause most deaths. Also called category 3 hurricane, how strong is a hurricane, typhoon, cyclone wind speeds. Look for category 1: 74 to 95 mph; category 3: 111 to 129 mph, a major hurricane. Prepare early by category and by the local storm surge and rainfall forecasts. Follow evacuation orders for surge zones."},{"symbol":"storm_surge","source":"symbolic_books/landscape/entries_weather.json","kind":"landscape","hash":"436c89a0","text":"Storm surge is the rise in sea level pushed ashore by a hurricane's winds, and it is the biggest killer in hurricanes. Also called hurricane flooding from the sea, wall of water, coastal flooding. Look for water rising far above normal high tide; strongest to the right of the storm's track in the north hemisphere. Leave when told to evacuate a surge zone, and go inland or uphill. Plan the route before the storm."},{"symbol":"hurricane_safety","source":"symbolic_books/landscape/entries_weather.json","kind":"landscape","hash":"d0be51e1","text":"Hurricane safety means knowing your evacuation zone, leaving early if told to, and sheltering in a safe interior room if you stay. Also called hurricane preparedness, what to do in a hurricane, tropical storm prep. Look for know your zone and route; supplies for several days: water, food, medicine, radio, power bank."},{"symbol":"watch_vs_warning","source":"symbolic_books/landscape/entries_weather.json","kind":"landscape","hash":"503cf31f","text":"A watch means conditions are favorable for the hazard; a warning means it is happening or about to happen; an advisory means it will be a nuisance. Also called difference between a watch and a warning, tornado watch, flood warning, weather alert meaning. Look for watch: be prepared; warning: take action now. When a warning is issued for your location, act at once. When a watch is issued, review your plan and keep a radio on."},{"symbol":"noaa_weather_radio","source":"symbolic_books/landscape/entries_weather.json","kind":"landscape","hash":"66966442","text":"NOAA Weather Radio broadcasts National Weather Service forecasts and alerts around the clock on seven VHF frequencies. Also called weather radio, same alert radio, emergency alert radio. Look for frequencies between 162.400 and 162.550 MHz; a tone alert and SAME code for your county. Keep a weather radio with fresh batteries, set to your county's SAME code, as a way to get warnings when cell service and power are out."},{"symbol":"blizzard_safety","source":"symbolic_books/landscape/entries_weather.json","kind":"landscape","hash":"6efe0299","text":"A blizzard is a storm with sustained winds or gusts of 35 mph or more and low visibility from falling or blowing snow for at least three hours. Also called whiteout, heavy snow storm, stuck in a snowstorm, winter storm. Look for falling or blowing snow with very low visibility; strong winds and drifting. Stay inside, stay put in a vehicle if stranded (run the engine only briefly with the tailpipe clear), and keep warm and hydrated. Use layers, a hat, and bright markers."},{"symbol":"ice_storm_freezing_rain","source":"symbolic_books/landscape/entries_weather.json","kind":"landscape","hash":"1eb2c841","text":"Freezing rain falls as liquid and freezes on contact, coating everything with ice; it is far more damaging than sleet. Also called glaze ice, icy roads, sleet versus freezing rain, ice on power lines. Look for glazed trees, wires, and roads; sleet bounces and rattles, freezing rain coats and sticks. Stay off roads, stay clear of trees and power lines, and prepare for outages. Keep warm and do not use generators indoors."},{"symbol":"wind_chill","source":"symbolic_books/landscape/entries_weather.json","kind":"landscape","hash":"e3f2e669","text":"Wind chill is how cold the air feels on bare skin because wind carries away body heat; it affects people and animals, not objects. Also called feels like temperature, cold wind, frostbite time. Look for a stronger wind makes the same temperature feel colder; at around minus 20 F (minus 29 C) wind chill, frostbite can occur in about 30 minutes. Dress for the wind chill: windproof outer layer, covered skin, and warm insulation, and check for frostbite on exposed skin."},{"symbol":"heat_index","source":"symbolic_books/landscape/entries_weather.json","kind":"landscape","hash":"ebd10420","text":"The heat index combines air temperature and humidity to show how hot it really feels, because sweat cannot cool you when the air is humid. Also called feels like in the heat, humid and hot, heat advisory. Look for high humidity makes it feel hotter; heat index above 103 F (39 C) is dangerous. Do hard work in the early morning or evening, rest in the shade, and drink water. Check on older people, children, and pets."},{"symbol":"dew_point","source":"symbolic_books/landscape/entries_weather.json","kind":"landscape","hash":"35bc40b8","text":"The dew point is the temperature at which air becomes saturated and dew or fog forms; a higher dew point means muggier air. Also called dewpoint, why is it sticky, dew on the grass, fog forms when. Look for dew point under 10 C (50 F): dry, comfortable; above 18 C (65 F): sticky. If the night temperature will fall to the dew point, expect dew or fog and condensation on gear. Put gear in a tent or bag to keep it dry."},{"symbol":"barometer_trend","source":"symbolic_books/landscape/entries_weather.json","kind":"landscape","hash":"4adba891","text":"A barometer measures air pressure; how it changes over a few hours tells more than the number itself. Also called falling pressure, rising pressure, barometer, altimeter weather change, pressure drop. Look for falling slowly: clouds and weather deteriorating; falling fast: a storm is near. Note the pressure every few hours. A fast fall, such as 3 hPa or more in three hours, calls for preparing for rough weather. Watch altimeter drift as a pressure change."},{"symbol":"cold_front","source":"symbolic_books/landscape/entries_weather.json","kind":"landscape","hash":"20903c78","text":"A cold front is the leading edge of a cooler air mass that pushes under warmer air, causing a narrow band of storms, a wind shift, and a drop in temperature. Also called front passing, storm line, temperature drop and wind shift, squall line. Look for a line of towering clouds and showers or storms; wind shifts to the west or northwest in the northern hemisphere."},{"symbol":"warm_front","source":"symbolic_books/landscape/entries_weather.json","kind":"landscape","hash":"5c0f7159","text":"A warm front is where warm air slides over cooler air, bringing a long period of thickening clouds and steady rain or snow, then milder weather. Also called slow moving rain, thickening clouds, steady rain ahead of warm air. Look for cirrus, then cirrostratus, then altostratus, then nimbostratus; pressure falls steadily. Expect a day or so of build-up, then hours of steady precipitation and warmer, muggier air after it passes."},{"symbol":"wind_shift_signs","source":"symbolic_books/landscape/entries_weather.json","kind":"landscape","hash":"0d1d257d","text":"Wind shifts show a passing front: in the northern hemisphere, stand with your back to the wind and the low pressure is on your left. Also called wind changed direction, wind backing, which way is the low pressure, how to find the storm. Look for wind backing (counterclockwise) with falling pressure ahead of a low; sudden shift to the northwest with a cold front."},{"symbol":"beaufort_scale","source":"symbolic_books/landscape/entries_weather.json","kind":"landscape","hash":"0043f228","text":"The Beaufort scale describes wind force from 0 (calm) to 12 (hurricane) using what you can see. Also called wind force, gale, how strong is the wind, wind speed words. Look for force 4: moderate breeze, dust and small branches move (11-16 knots); force 6: strong breeze, large branches sway, umbrellas hard to use (22-27 knots). Use it to judge conditions and decide to stay off the water, ridgelines, and exposed trails at force 6 and above."},{"symbol":"mountain_weather","source":"symbolic_books/landscape/entries_weather.json","kind":"landscape","hash":"0b155daf","text":"Mountain weather changes fast: temperatures fall with height, winds strengthen, and storms often build by early afternoon. Also called weather in the mountains, storms on peaks, summit weather, afternoon thunderstorms. Look for temperature drops about 6.5 C per 1,000 m (3.5 F per 1,000 ft); afternoon cumulus towers in summer. Start early, be off summits and ridges by noon, and turn back when clouds build. Carry rain and warm layers even on fair days."},{"symbol":"microburst","source":"symbolic_books/landscape/entries_weather.json","kind":"landscape","hash":"a5f59eb9","text":"A microburst is a small, intense downdraft from a thunderstorm that spreads out at the ground and can knock down trees in one direction. Also called straight-line winds, sudden gust, storm winds knocked trees down. Look for sudden strong wind at the leading edge of a storm; dust or rain streaking down. Move away from trees and into a sturdy shelter at the first sign of a strong, sudden gust ahead of a storm."},{"symbol":"flash_flood_risk","source":"symbolic_books/landscape/entries_weather.json","kind":"landscape","hash":"f3305b7c","text":"A flash flood is a rapid rise of water, often from storms miles upstream, and it can reach you under a clear sky. Also called flood watch, rising creek, dry wash flood, slot canyon flood, dry gully. Look for heavy rain upstream, rising and muddy creeks; a rumbling roar in a canyon, or debris in the water. Move to high ground at once, avoid canyons and washes in storm season, and never drive or walk through floodwater. Turn around, don't drown."},{"symbol":"wildfire_smoke_aqi","source":"symbolic_books/landscape/entries_weather.json","kind":"landscape","hash":"2a4d1b13","text":"Wildfire smoke contains fine particles that can harm the lungs and heart; the air quality index (AQI) shows when to limit time outside. Also called smoky air, air quality index, AQI, fire smoke, N95 for smoke. Look for AQI above 100 is unhealthy for sensitive groups; above 150 is unhealthy for all. Stay indoors with windows closed, run a HEPA filter, and use a well-fitted N95 respirator outside if you must. Limit exertion."},{"symbol":"wildfire_evacuation","source":"symbolic_books/landscape/entries_weather.json","kind":"landscape","hash":"0bc603ce","text":"For a wildfire, leave early when told to or when smoke and flames are near; waiting is the biggest mistake. Also called fire is coming, wildfire go bag, leave before the fire, fire evacuation zone. Look for official evacuation order or warning; smoke, ember showers, and a wind shift toward you. Take people, pets, medicines, papers, and a go bag, close windows and doors, and follow known evacuation routes. Tell someone where you are going."},{"symbol":"dust_storm_haboob","source":"symbolic_books/landscape/entries_weather.json","kind":"landscape","hash":"2dfdf969","text":"A haboob is a wall of dust pushed ahead of a thunderstorm's outflow, dropping visibility to near zero within seconds. Also called dust wall, sandstorm, blowing dust. Look for a towering brown wall approaching; sudden strong gusts and a drop in visibility. Pull off the road as far as you can, turn off lights, set the parking brake, and keep your foot off the brake pedal. Cover your nose and mouth."},{"symbol":"temperature_inversion","source":"symbolic_books/landscape/entries_weather.json","kind":"landscape","hash":"a54c45ff","text":"On calm clear nights, cold air sinks and pools in valleys while the slopes above stay warmer; this is a temperature inversion. Also called cold in the valley at night, frost hollows, smoke lies flat, inversion. Look for smoke or fog layers lie flat; valley floors much colder than the slopes. Camp on the slope or bench, not the valley floor. Frost and fog form in the low spots first."},{"symbol":"weather_folk_signs","source":"symbolic_books/landscape/entries_weather.json","kind":"landscape","hash":"7151f738","text":"Some old sayings work because they match real changes in air pressure and moisture; others are myths. Also called red sky at night, cows lying down, birds flying low, weather sayings, ants before rain. Look for red sky at night, shepherd's delight: often works in mid-latitudes where weather moves west to east; a ring around the moon: thin high cloud ahead of a front."},{"symbol":"lightning_myths","source":"symbolic_books/landscape/entries_weather.json","kind":"landscape","hash":"c6d07002","text":"Lightning can strike the same place many times, can reach 10 miles from a storm, and rubber tires do not protect you; a hard-topped metal vehicle does. Also called does lightning strike twice, rubber tires protect, if the sky is clear lightning cannot hit, bolt from the blue. Look for bolts from the blue strike under clear skies near a storm; tall isolated objects are struck repeatedly."},{"symbol":"power_out_safety","source":"symbolic_books/landscape/entries_weather.json","kind":"landscape","hash":"a6278b0a","text":"In a storm outage, keep warm, use flashlights, keep fridge and freezer doors closed, and run generators outside, far from the house. Also called lights are out, storm knocked out power, candles safety, fridge during outage. Look for flashlights and battery lantern, not candles; closed fridge keeps food safe about four hours, a full freezer about 48 hours."},{"symbol":"weather_trend_reading","source":"symbolic_books/landscape/entries_weather.json","kind":"landscape","hash":"af601e18","text":"The weather trend is the direction air pressure and the sky are moving over the last few hours, read from this phone's barometer together with the saved forecast and how old it is. Also called weather trend, is the weather getting worse, will it rain, pressure trend, barometer says. Look for pressure falling 1 hPa or more per hour: weather is worsening, often rain or wind; pressure steady: no big change soon."},{"symbol":"forecast_staleness","source":"symbolic_books/landscape/entries_weather.json","kind":"landscape","hash":"3005e7cf","text":"A saved forecast or alert is a snapshot; it gets less reliable with age, and every NWS alert carries its own expiry time. Also called is the forecast out of date, stale forecast, when was the alert issued, alert expires. Look for the issue time on the forecast; the 'until' or expiry time on an alert."},{"symbol":"hypothermia_mild_weather","source":"symbolic_books/landscape/entries_weather.json","kind":"landscape","hash":"216329b2","text":"Hypothermia can happen well above freezing: cool air plus rain, wind, and wet clothes steals body heat fast. Also called cold and wet, hypothermia at 50 degrees, rain and wind chill, cold drizzle. Look for air temperatures from 30 to 50 F (minus 1 to 10 C) with rain or wind; shivering, clumsiness, and mumbling. Put on a waterproof shell and a hat, swap wet layers for dry ones, eat and drink, and get out of the wind. Stop and warm up at the first shivering."},{"symbol":"fog_hunting_visibility","source":"symbolic_books/landscape/entries_weather.json","kind":"landscape","hash":"2939a413","text":"Fog cuts visibility, hides landmarks, and can make a known place unfamiliar; it also muffles sound. Also called foggy morning hunt, can't see in the fog, low light and mist. Look for valley fog at dawn on calm clear mornings; visibility dropping below a few hundred feet."},{"symbol":"thunderstorm_hunting_water","source":"symbolic_books/landscape/entries_weather.json","kind":"landscape","hash":"a7451b35","text":"If thunder is heard, get to a vehicle, a building, or low ground at once, and get off the water, ridges, and open fields. Also called storm while hunting, lightning in the woods, storm on the boat, lightning on the lake. Look for thunder or a flash within 30 seconds; dark towers and a sudden cool breeze."},{"symbol":"tree_ice_branches","source":"symbolic_books/landscape/entries_weather.json","kind":"landscape","hash":"36859a42","text":"After ice or wind storms, ice-loaded limbs and downed lines are the main hazards, and any line on the ground should be treated as live. Also called tree limb down, power line on the ground, ice storm cleanup, widowmaker after a storm. Look for ice-coated or cracked limbs overhead; wires on the ground or hanging in trees."},{"symbol":"red_flag_fire_weather","source":"symbolic_books/landscape/entries_weather.json","kind":"landscape","hash":"398a3a0b","text":"A red flag warning means warm temperatures, very low humidity, and strong winds are creating critical fire conditions. Also called red flag warning, fire weather watch, dry windy day, drought and fire risk. Look for low humidity, dry vegetation, and gusty winds; a burn ban or an NWS red flag warning. Do not burn outdoors, keep vehicles off dry grass, and have an escape route ready. Check local burn bans. Put out any fire completely."},{"symbol":"rain_on_snow_flood","source":"symbolic_books/landscape/entries_weather.json","kind":"landscape","hash":"f2d58379","text":"Rain on deep snow plus warm air can melt it fast and send a surge of water into streams, and ice jams can back up rivers. Also called thaw flooding, ice jam, snowmelt flood, spring flood. Look for warm rain on a deep snowpack; rapidly rising streams and cracking river ice. Stay away from rivers and stream banks, move to higher ground if water rises, and avoid crossing flooded roads. Move vehicles and valuables from low spots early."},{"symbol":"small_craft_marine_forecast","source":"symbolic_books/landscape/entries_weather.json","kind":"landscape","hash":"ef210143","text":"A small craft advisory warns that winds or seas are hazardous for small boats, and a marine forecast gives wind, seas, and visibility for coastal waters. Also called small craft advisory, coastal waters forecast, boating weather, choppy seas. Look for winds around 20 to 33 knots and rough seas; fog and reduced visibility."},{"symbol":"dew_frost_overnight","source":"symbolic_books/landscape/entries_weather.json","kind":"landscape","hash":"ddb9923d","text":"Dew forms when surfaces cool to the dew point, and frost forms when they cool below freezing on a clear, calm night. Also called will it freeze tonight, frost forecast, ground frost, heavy dew. Look for clear skies and calm winds cool the ground fastest; wet grass or a white coating at dawn. Expect a frost when the forecast low is near 36 F (2 C) on a clear, calm night. Cover gear, bring water filters and batteries inside, and sleep above the cold air in the valley."},{"symbol":"whiteout_snow","source":"symbolic_books/landscape/entries_weather.json","kind":"landscape","hash":"536d0709","text":"A whiteout is a loss of depth and horizon in blowing snow or flat light, and it makes it easy to lose direction or walk off an edge. Also called can't see in the snow, blowing snow, snow squall, white out. Look for no visible horizon or shadows; blowing snow and low visibility. Stop moving unless you are on a sure handrail such as a fence or a wall. Put on warm layers, take shelter, and wait for visibility to improve. Use a compass if you must move."},{"symbol":"weather_signals_combined","source":"symbolic_books/landscape/entries_weather.json","kind":"landscape","hash":"0d23a68f","text":"A better local forecast comes from combining pressure trend, wind shift, cloud change, and temperature rather than relying on any one sign. Also called how do I forecast the weather myself, signs of a storm coming, weather clues, predict rain. Look for falling pressure plus thickening high cloud: wet weather in a day; backing wind plus low, dark cloud: worsening."},{"symbol":"tropical_remnants_coastal_flooding","source":"symbolic_books/landscape/entries_weather.json","kind":"landscape","hash":"8ef2fc5b","text":"Remnants of tropical storms and nor'easters bring heavy rain, wind, and high tides that flood coasts and rivers well inland. Also called hurricane remnants, nor'easter, coastal flood, storm tide. Look for tropical storm or nor'easter in the forecast; heavy rain bands and rising rivers. Follow evacuation orders, move to higher ground, secure outside items, and avoid flooded roads. Have supplies for several days."},{"symbol":"biome_bands","source":"symbolic_books/landscape/notes.json","kind":"landscape","hash":"fd790708","text":"Biomes are broad living landscapes: forest, grassland, desert, tundra, and coast, set by climate and water more than by towns. Plants and the ground underfoot tell you which pattern you are in. A biome is not a line you can pace to a city limit. Weather still changes inside it."},{"symbol":"terrain_types","source":"symbolic_books/landscape/notes.json","kind":"landscape","hash":"792fade7","text":"Terrain is the shape you walk: flat, rolling, steep, broken, swampy, or cliffed. Steep ground costs time. Broken rock hides ankles. Soft ground hides depth. Choose the line that keeps you on ground you can see. This is a travel cue, not a soil survey or a road map."},{"symbol":"watershed","source":"symbolic_books/landscape/notes.json","kind":"landscape","hash":"4a1e8372","text":"A watershed is the land that drains to one stream, lake, or coast. Ridges are the divides. Water runs away from them into the basin. Following a creek downhill moves you toward that basin's trunk stream, not necessarily toward help. A drainage is not a trail."},{"symbol":"coasts","source":"symbolic_books/landscape/notes.json","kind":"landscape","hash":"d5f4cdad","text":"A coast is where land meets a large body of water. Tides, cliffs, and headlands change the walkable strip through the day. A headland is a natural landmark. A harbor town is not stored here. Stay off wet rock when the sea is rising, and do not guess a tide table you do not have."},{"symbol":"mountains","source":"symbolic_books/landscape/notes.json","kind":"landscape","hash":"06e16ae2","text":"Mountains stack climate. Higher is usually colder, windier, and slower. A pass is a low saddle between higher ground. A peak is a landmark only while you can still see it. Weather arrives fast on ridges. Turn back when the route you can see runs out. This is not a climb grade."},{"symbol":"deserts","source":"symbolic_books/landscape/notes.json","kind":"landscape","hash":"650b3a0c","text":"Deserts are water-poor landscapes, hot or cold, where shade and the water you carry matter more than speed. Washes can flood from rain you do not see. Walk the firm edge of a wash, not its throat. Do not chase a shimmer toward a town. Night can turn sharply cold."},{"symbol":"forests","source":"symbolic_books/landscape/notes.json","kind":"landscape","hash":"f577fb8f","text":"Forests hide the sun and the distant ridge. Travel is slower, and a handrail such as a stream or a ridge matters more than a bearing you cannot check. Moss is not a compass. Mark the way back while the last sure point is still in sight. This is not a timber or trail catalog."},{"symbol":"arctic_tundra","source":"symbolic_books/landscape/notes.json","kind":"landscape","hash":"8a5e4661","text":"Arctic and tundra country is open, cold, and often wet underfoot, with few trees to use as landmarks. When the horizon disappears, a short bearing and a counted pace are safer than a long walk. Summer light can last all day. Winter dark does the opposite. Ice is not a path this note approves."},{"symbol":"river_travel","source":"symbolic_books/landscape/notes.json","kind":"landscape","hash":"a3fd890a","text":"River cues: a V of ripples often points at a rock, and smooth dark water can be deep. The outside of a bend cuts the bank. The inside piles gravel. Current is a force, not a trail. Wade only at a wide shallow place you have walked, and do not enter flood water."},{"symbol":"natural_landmarks","source":"symbolic_books/landscape/notes.json","kind":"landscape","hash":"320b4038","text":"Useful landmarks are natural and stable: a peak, a saddle, a lone butte, a confluence, a waterfall, a headland, a lake outlet. Name them on the map you carry and in a waypoint you save yourself. This store has no city, street, or business directory. If the landmark clouds over, rejoin a handrail."},{"symbol":"climate_bands","source":"symbolic_books/landscape/notes.json","kind":"landscape","hash":"31196d3a","text":"Climate bands follow latitude and altitude in a rough way: tropical, subtropical, temperate, polar, plus dry belts where rain rarely falls. A band says what the season tends to do, not what tomorrow's storm will do. Height can copy a colder band. Watch the sky you actually have."},{"symbol":"magnetic_declination","source":"symbolic_books/landscape/notes.json","kind":"landscape","hash":"d561f00d","text":"Magnetic declination is the angle between magnetic north and true north at a place. It changes slowly over years and with where you stand. A compass needle points magnetic. A map's meridians are the true frame. Set the declination printed on your map before you trust a bearing. There is no live declination feed."},{"symbol":"true_north","source":"symbolic_books/landscape/notes.json","kind":"landscape","hash":"c3df749e","text":"True north follows the planet's axis, toward the geographic pole. Magnetic north is where the needle points, and the gap between them is declination. East declination means magnetic north sits east of true north. Add east declination to a magnetic bearing to plot true. A position lock is a point, not a north reference."},{"symbol":"contour_reading","source":"symbolic_books/landscape/notes.json","kind":"landscape","hash":"b022af9b","text":"Contour lines join points of equal elevation. Lines close together mean steep ground. Wide gaps mean flatter ground. A V in the contours often points upstream in a valley. Index contours are the labeled ones. They show shape, not a trail name. The interval is printed on the map you carry."},{"symbol":"orienteering_map_compass","source":"symbolic_books/landscape/notes.json","kind":"landscape","hash":"c70634c7","text":"Map and compass: set the map to the land, lay the compass edge on the line you mean to walk, turn the housing to north on the map, then turn your body until the needle agrees. Walk a short leg to a landmark you named and count paces. This is a method sketch, not a course certificate."},{"symbol":"elevation_concepts","source":"symbolic_books/landscape/notes.json","kind":"landscape","hash":"f8b98e61","text":"Elevation is height above a datum, often mean sea level. A GPS altitude, when a consented fix includes one, is a rough height and is weaker than the horizontal fix. If the lock has no altitude, say so and do not invent one. Contours show shape. A barometer drifts with the weather."},{"symbol":"great_circle","source":"symbolic_books/landscape/notes.json","kind":"landscape","hash":"107a557b","text":"A great-circle course is the shortest path on a sphere between two latitudes and longitudes. The initial bearing is the direction you start, and it can change as you travel. Reverse bearing is the direction back. These tools use that spherical estimate. They do not follow roads and they do not download tiles."},{"symbol":"track_log_note","source":"symbolic_books/landscape/notes.json","kind":"landscape","hash":"235343f2","text":"A track log is a list of your own points, saved on this device only after you consent. Distance is the sum of the great-circle legs. Elapsed time is counted only when those points carry timestamps. It is not someone else's path, and it is not sent anywhere unless you say so in your own words."},{"symbol":"no_city_maps","source":"symbolic_books/landscape/notes.json","kind":"landscape","hash":"0f813cc6","text":"Landscape navigation here is terrain, water, coast, and sky: distance, bearing, waypoints, and a track you record yourself. It deliberately has no city map, no street list, and no points of interest that go stale as towns change. If you need a town, use a map you brought, not this store."},{"symbol":"wound_cleaning","source":"symbolic_books/medical/entries_firstaid.json","kind":"medical","hash":"fc59885c","text":"Cleaning a wound means flushing dirt and germs out with plenty of clean running water after bleeding is under control. Also called wash a cut, rinse a scrape, irrigate a wound. Look for bleeding has stopped or is controlled; visible dirt or grit in the wound. Rinse for several minutes with clean water, use mild soap on the skin around it, and lift out loose grit with clean tweezers. Pat dry and cover."},{"symbol":"wound_infection_signs","source":"symbolic_books/medical/entries_firstaid.json","kind":"medical","hash":"fdb2d4fe","text":"A wound is likely infected when it gets worse over days instead of better, with growing redness, warmth, swelling, pus, or pain. Also called infected cut, cellulitis, pus in a wound, red streaks. Look for redness that spreads outward from the wound; warmth, swelling, and increasing pain after the first day or two."},{"symbol":"sepsis_signs","source":"symbolic_books/medical/entries_firstaid.json","kind":"medical","hash":"f6c3de22","text":"Sepsis is the body's dangerous overreaction to an infection, and it can quickly harm organs and cause death if not treated. Also called blood poisoning, septic, infection spreading through the body. Look for fever or shivering, or feeling very cold; fast heartbeat and fast breathing. Treat it as an emergency in anyone with a known or suspected infection who has these signs. Get to hospital care fast; sepsis needs medical treatment."},{"symbol":"bandaging_basics","source":"symbolic_books/medical/entries_firstaid.json","kind":"medical","hash":"5dd6c032","text":"A dressing is the clean pad that sits on the wound, and a bandage is what holds it in place without cutting off blood flow. Also called dressing a wound, wrap a cut, cover a wound, gauze. Look for clean, non-stick or gauze pad; wrap snug but loose enough for a finger beneath. Cover the wound with a clean dressing, wrap over it firmly, and check the fingers or toes beyond it for color, warmth, and feeling every little while."},{"symbol":"pressure_bandage","source":"symbolic_books/medical/entries_firstaid.json","kind":"medical","hash":"598b76b7","text":"A pressure bandage is a thick pad held over a bleeding wound with a firm wrap, so you can keep pressure on without your hands. Also called emergency bandage, israeli bandage, wrap that stops bleeding. Look for thick pad directly on the wound; firm wrap that presses the pad down. Use it after direct pressure when bleeding is steady and you need your hands free. Add more padding on top if blood soaks through; do not remove the first layer."},{"symbol":"arterial_bleeding","source":"symbolic_books/medical/entries_firstaid.json","kind":"medical","hash":"eb6707ed","text":"Arterial bleeding is blood from an artery: bright red and often spurting with each heartbeat, and it can be fatal within minutes. Also called spurting blood, bright red bleeding, life-threatening bleeding. Look for bright red blood that spurts or pulses; blood that quickly pools or soaks clothing."},{"symbol":"internal_bleeding","source":"symbolic_books/medical/entries_firstaid.json","kind":"medical","hash":"7444aaeb","text":"Internal bleeding is blood loss inside the body that you cannot see, often after a hard blow, a fall, a crash, or a deep injury to the torso. Also called bleeding inside the body, hidden bleeding, blood loss you cannot see. Look for bruising, swelling, or a hard painful belly after a blow; pale, cold, clammy skin, fast weak pulse, dizziness."},{"symbol":"abdominal_injury","source":"symbolic_books/medical/entries_firstaid.json","kind":"medical","hash":"a466182c","text":"A serious belly injury can damage organs and bleed inside, and an open wound can let intestines show. Also called stomach wound, bowel sticking out, gut injury, blow to the belly. Look for open wound or protruding bowel; a rigid, swollen, very painful belly. Keep the person lying on their back with knees bent if comfortable, cover any exposed organs loosely with a clean moist cloth, and get emergency help."},{"symbol":"chest_wound","source":"symbolic_books/medical/entries_firstaid.json","kind":"medical","hash":"0042c528","text":"A penetrating chest wound can let air into the chest around the lung, making breathing hard and getting worse. Also called punctured lung, stab to the chest, penetrating chest injury, pneumothorax. Look for hole in the chest, sometimes with a sucking sound or bubbling blood; trouble breathing and chest pain."},{"symbol":"impaled_object","source":"symbolic_books/medical/entries_firstaid.json","kind":"medical","hash":"986ebd03","text":"An embedded object plugs the wound it caused, and pulling it out can release heavy bleeding. Also called impaled object, stabbed, something stuck in the leg, embedded object. Look for object visibly stuck in the body; bleeding around, not through, the object. Leave it in place. Pad around it to hold it still and stop it moving, control bleeding by pressing around it, and get emergency help."},{"symbol":"amputation_care","source":"symbolic_books/medical/entries_firstaid.json","kind":"medical","hash":"69bea78d","text":"After an amputation, the first job is to stop the bleeding and treat shock; the severed part is saved wrapped and cool so it might be reattached. Also called severed finger, cut off hand, body part cut off, avulsion. Look for heavy bleeding from the stump; a separated finger, hand, or limb."},{"symbol":"crush_injury","source":"symbolic_books/medical/entries_firstaid.json","kind":"medical","hash":"ac77a1a5","text":"A crush injury is damage from a heavy weight pressing a body part, and releasing a long-trapped limb can suddenly cause shock or kidney trouble. Also called pinned under something, trapped limb, crushed leg, building collapse injury. Look for limb pinned under a heavy object; swelling, numbness, and pain. Get emergency help first. If trapped less than about 15 minutes and the load is safe to lift, free the person, control bleeding, and treat for shock. Keep them still."},{"symbol":"broken_bone_signs","source":"symbolic_books/medical/entries_firstaid.json","kind":"medical","hash":"d8c3767b","text":"A fracture is a break or crack in a bone, and the signs are pain that is worse with movement, swelling, bruising, and an odd shape or loss of use. Also called fracture, broken arm, broken leg, did I break it, cracked bone. Look for sharp pain at one spot that is worse with use; swelling, bruising, and a limb that looks bent or short."},{"symbol":"open_fracture","source":"symbolic_books/medical/entries_firstaid.json","kind":"medical","hash":"cd30fd9f","text":"An open fracture is a broken bone with an open wound, which means a high risk of infection and bleeding. Also called bone sticking out, compound fracture, bone through skin. Look for bone end visible or a wound over a deformed limb; bleeding at the break. Control bleeding with pressure around the bone, not on it. Cover the wound loosely with a clean moist dressing, splint without pushing the bone back, and get emergency help."},{"symbol":"sling_and_swathe","source":"symbolic_books/medical/entries_firstaid.json","kind":"medical","hash":"69b75910","text":"A sling holds the forearm up, and a swathe is a second bandage that binds the arm against the chest so it cannot swing. Also called arm sling, triangle bandage sling, broken collarbone sling, shoulder injury support. Look for a triangular bandage or a shirt tail; forearm rests higher than the elbow. Use it for a broken arm, collarbone, shoulder or dislocation, after you have checked the fingers for color and feeling. Put padding between the arm and body."},{"symbol":"dislocation","source":"symbolic_books/medical/entries_firstaid.json","kind":"medical","hash":"d9e15387","text":"A dislocation is when the ends of the bones in a joint are forced out of place, leaving the joint looking wrong and unable to move. Also called dislocated shoulder, dislocated finger, joint out of place, popped out of socket. Look for joint looks deformed or out of position; severe pain and swelling and unable to move it. Support the joint in the position found with padding and a sling or splint, apply a cool pack for pain, and get it put back by a clinician."},{"symbol":"concussion_signs","source":"symbolic_books/medical/entries_firstaid.json","kind":"medical","hash":"67a3c1ff","text":"A concussion is a mild brain injury from a blow or jolt to the head, and the danger is that a serious bleed can build slowly. Also called hit head, bump on the head, knocked out, head trauma, brain injury. Look for headache, dizziness, confusion, or memory gaps; nausea or vomiting, or blurred vision."},{"symbol":"unconscious_person","source":"symbolic_books/medical/entries_firstaid.json","kind":"medical","hash":"8230cf2d","text":"An unresponsive person who is still breathing normally needs their airway kept open and an ambulance, because they can choke on their own tongue or vomit. Also called passed out, fainted, not waking up, unresponsive. Look for does not respond to voice or a shoulder tap; chest rises and falls with normal breathing."},{"symbol":"fainting","source":"symbolic_books/medical/entries_firstaid.json","kind":"medical","hash":"3c431400","text":"Fainting is a short loss of consciousness when the brain briefly gets too little blood, often from standing too long, heat, fear, pain, or dehydration. Also called passing out, about to faint, blacked out, lightheaded and dizzy. Look for lightheaded, pale, sweaty, vision narrowing; collapse that lasts under a minute or two."},{"symbol":"eye_object","source":"symbolic_books/medical/entries_firstaid.json","kind":"medical","hash":"d0ac9456","text":"A speck or grit in the eye usually washes out with tears or clean water, and anything stuck or sharp needs a clinician. Also called speck in eye, grit in eye, eyelash in eye, foreign body in eye, eye pain. Look for watering, blinking, redness, a gritty feel; pain that is worse when blinking. Do not rub. Blink, then flush the eye with clean water or saline for several minutes. Pull the upper lid over the lower to let lashes sweep it out."},{"symbol":"snow_blindness","source":"symbolic_books/medical/entries_firstaid.json","kind":"medical","hash":"f9b402e6","text":"Snow blindness is a painful sunburn of the surface of the eye from bright ultraviolet light reflecting off snow, ice, or water. Also called sun burned eyes, photokeratitis, eye sunburn, welder flash. Look for gritty, burning pain and tears hours after exposure; red, swollen lids and sensitivity to light. Get out of the light, cover both eyes with a dark soft cloth, rest, and use cool damp cloths for comfort. Prevent it with dark tinted glasses with side shields."},{"symbol":"tooth_knocked_out","source":"symbolic_books/medical/entries_firstaid.json","kind":"medical","hash":"83c76338","text":"A knocked-out adult tooth can sometimes be saved if it is kept moist and put back or seen by a dentist within about an hour. Also called lost a tooth, avulsed tooth, broken tooth, tooth fell out. Look for a whole tooth out with the root attached; bleeding in the socket. Pick up the tooth by the crown, not the root. Rinse gently with milk or saline if dirty and try to push it back into the socket; otherwise keep it in milk or against the cheek. See a dentist right away."},{"symbol":"toothache_field","source":"symbolic_books/medical/entries_firstaid.json","kind":"medical","hash":"4c4a1d62","text":"A toothache is pain from decay, a crack, or an abscess, and swelling or fever means the infection may be spreading. Also called tooth pain, abscess, swollen gum, dental pain. Look for steady throbbing pain, worse with hot or cold; swollen gum or cheek, a bad taste. Rinse with warm salt water, keep the mouth clean, and use over-the-counter pain relievers as directed on the label. See a dentist as soon as you can."},{"symbol":"fishhook_removal","source":"symbolic_books/medical/entries_firstaid.json","kind":"medical","hash":"0fb223d7","text":"A fishhook is barbed, so pulling straight back tears the skin; it is pushed through or backed out along its curve. Also called hook in finger, fish hook stuck, hook in skin. Look for hook embedded in finger, arm, or face; barb under the skin. For a shallow hook, push the point through, snip off the barb with clippers, and back the shaft out. Clean the wound and cover it. Check your tetanus status."},{"symbol":"tetanus_shot","source":"symbolic_books/medical/entries_firstaid.json","kind":"medical","hash":"4bfc215a","text":"Tetanus is a serious infection from bacteria in soil and dirt that enter a wound, and it is prevented by vaccination with boosters. Also called lockjaw, tetanus booster, rusty nail, dirty wound vaccine. Look for a dirty, deep, or puncture wound; animal bite or rusty or soil-contaminated injury. Ask a clinician about a booster after a dirty or deep wound, usually if you have not had one in the last five years for dirty wounds or ten years for clean ones."},{"symbol":"animal_bite","source":"symbolic_books/medical/entries_firstaid.json","kind":"medical","hash":"d6c0a99f","text":"An animal bite can cause infection and, from wild animals, rabies, so it needs washing and a call to a clinician or health department. Also called dog bite, cat bite, bitten by a wild animal, rabies exposure, bat in the house. Look for puncture or torn skin from teeth or claws; a wild, strange-acting, or unprovoked animal."},{"symbol":"splinter_removal","source":"symbolic_books/medical/entries_firstaid.json","kind":"medical","hash":"38a5523d","text":"A splinter is a small piece of wood, thorn, or glass in the skin that is removed with clean tweezers along the angle it went in. Also called sliver, thorn in the skin, wood splinter, cactus spines. Look for a visible end sticking out; tenderness at the spot. Wash the skin, clean the tweezers with alcohol or boiling water, pull the splinter out in the line it entered, and wash again. Tape can lift fine cactus spines."},{"symbol":"blister_care","source":"symbolic_books/medical/entries_firstaid.json","kind":"medical","hash":"ce3cc025","text":"A blister is a fluid-filled pocket from friction or heat, and a hot spot is the warm, tender patch that warns one is coming. Also called foot blister, rubbing boot, hot spot on heel, blister on foot. Look for a warm, red, tender patch where a boot rubs; a raised bubble of clear fluid."},{"symbol":"foot_care_hiking","source":"symbolic_books/medical/entries_firstaid.json","kind":"medical","hash":"f445c94d","text":"Foot care is keeping feet dry, clean, and padded so friction, wet, and cold do not disable you. Also called sore feet, care for feet when hiking, preventing blisters, dry socks. Look for changing to dry socks at breaks; trimmed toenails and well-fitting boots. Stop at the first hot spot, dry feet at rests, rotate socks, and use powder or tape where you rub. Air feet at night and dry socks against your body."},{"symbol":"trench_foot","source":"symbolic_books/medical/entries_firstaid.json","kind":"medical","hash":"01f6042e","text":"Trench foot is damage to the feet from long exposure to wet and cold, even above freezing, and it can lead to tissue loss. Also called wet foot injury, immersion foot, feet numb and swollen from wet. Look for cold, numb, tingling feet that may look pale, blue, or red; swelling and a heavy, 'wooden' feel. Get out of wet footwear, dry and gently warm the feet, keep them raised and clean, and get medical care. Prevent it with dry socks and regular foot checks."},{"symbol":"sunburn","source":"symbolic_books/medical/entries_firstaid.json","kind":"medical","hash":"fd84d680","text":"Sunburn is skin damage from ultraviolet light that shows up as red, painful skin hours after exposure. Also called burned by the sun, red skin, sun poisoning. Look for red, hot, painful skin that appears hours later; blisters on bad burns. Get out of the sun, cool the skin with cool water, drink fluids, and use moisturizer. Cover up and keep burned skin out of the sun. Prevent it with shade, clothing, a hat, and sunscreen."},{"symbol":"recovery_position","source":"symbolic_books/medical/entries_firstaid.json","kind":"medical","hash":"6f8e0635","text":"The recovery position lays an unresponsive person who is breathing on their side so the tongue falls forward and fluid drains out of the mouth. Also called lateral position, roll someone on their side, put them on their side, keep airway open. Look for person is unresponsive but breathing normally; lying on their side with the top leg bent for stability."},{"symbol":"heat_exhaustion_signs","source":"symbolic_books/medical/entries_firstaid.json","kind":"medical","hash":"143d465d","text":"Heat exhaustion is the body overheating from heat and fluid loss, with heavy sweating, weakness, and dizziness. It can progress to heat stroke. Also called overheated, heavy sweating and weak, too hot and dizzy, heat sickness. Look for heavy sweating, pale or clammy skin; headache, dizziness, nausea, and weakness. Move to shade or a cool place, loosen clothes, cool with wet cloths or a fan, and give small sips of water. Rest and do not go back to hard work that day."},{"symbol":"heat_stroke_signs","source":"symbolic_books/medical/entries_firstaid.json","kind":"medical","hash":"26013d45","text":"Heat stroke is a life-threatening emergency in which the body temperature rises dangerously high and the brain is affected. Also called sunstroke, hyperthermia, very hot skin and confused, body overheated. Look for hot, red, dry or damp skin; sweating may stop; confusion, slurred speech, or odd behavior."},{"symbol":"heat_cramps","source":"symbolic_books/medical/entries_firstaid.json","kind":"medical","hash":"87448371","text":"Heat cramps are painful muscle spasms during or after hard work in the heat, usually from fluid and salt loss. Also called muscle cramps in the heat, leg cramps from sweating, cramping while hiking. Look for cramping in the legs, arms, or belly; heavy sweating. Stop, rest in the shade, stretch and gently massage the cramping muscle, and sip water or a drink with electrolytes."},{"symbol":"hydration_basics","source":"symbolic_books/medical/entries_firstaid.json","kind":"medical","hash":"452bab04","text":"Staying hydrated means drinking steadily so urine stays pale; thirst lags behind, so drink before you are thirsty during work or heat. Also called how much water to drink, drink more water, thirst, pale urine. Look for pale yellow urine means good hydration; dark urine, headache, dry mouth, and tiredness mean you need more. Sip regularly through the day and more in heat, at altitude, or when ill. Add food and some salt when sweating heavily for hours."},{"symbol":"hyponatremia","source":"symbolic_books/medical/entries_firstaid.json","kind":"medical","hash":"18cfa2a7","text":"Drinking far more water than you sweat out can dilute the salt in the blood, causing headache, nausea, and confusion. Also called drinking too much water, hyponatremia, overhydration. Look for headache, nausea, and bloated feeling after drinking lots of water; confusion, weakness, or seizures in severe cases. Stop drinking plain water and eat salty snacks. Get medical help for confusion or severe symptoms."},{"symbol":"hypothermia_stages","source":"symbolic_books/medical/entries_firstaid.json","kind":"medical","hash":"30d06c64","text":"Hypothermia is when body temperature falls below normal, and the signs progress from shivering to clumsiness and confusion to stopped shivering. Also called too cold, shivering uncontrollably, umbles, stumbling and mumbling, cold exposure. Look for mild: shivering, cold hands, clumsy; moderate: stumbling, slurred speech, apathy, confusion."},{"symbol":"rewarming_safely","source":"symbolic_books/medical/entries_firstaid.json","kind":"medical","hash":"977da058","text":"Rewarming a cold person means stopping heat loss first and then adding gentle heat to the trunk, not to the limbs. Also called warm someone up, how to warm a hypothermic person, sleeping bag with another person. Look for dry insulation under and over them; warm packs or bottles wrapped in cloth at chest, armpits, groin."},{"symbol":"drowning_rescue","source":"symbolic_books/medical/entries_firstaid.json","kind":"medical","hash":"b8fba89e","text":"Drowning is trouble breathing from submersion; the first moves are to reach the person safely, then get them breathing again. Also called near drowning, pulled from water, pulled someone out of the water, cold water immersion. Look for person in the water who is quiet, vertical, and not waving; not breathing or breathing with difficulty after being rescued."},{"symbol":"electric_shock","source":"symbolic_books/medical/entries_firstaid.json","kind":"medical","hash":"8a5cbf7f","text":"An electric shock can stop the heart and cause internal burns that are bigger than they look, so every significant shock needs a medical check. Also called electrocuted, got shocked, downed power line, live wire. Look for person still touching a live source or near a downed wire; burns at the entry and exit points."},{"symbol":"lightning_strike_first_aid","source":"symbolic_books/medical/entries_firstaid.json","kind":"medical","hash":"4ea59270","text":"A lightning strike victim does not carry a charge, and they often need CPR because the heart or breathing may stop. Also called struck by lightning, lightning injury, hit by lightning. Look for unconscious or not breathing; burns, confusion, hearing or vision loss. Move them to a safer place away from the storm, call for help, check breathing and pulse, and start CPR if needed. Treat burns and fractures after the person is breathing."},{"symbol":"carbon_monoxide_poisoning","source":"symbolic_books/medical/entries_firstaid.json","kind":"medical","hash":"e6b8b214","text":"Carbon monoxide is an invisible, odorless gas from burning fuel that keeps blood from carrying oxygen. Also called CO poisoning, gas stove in tent, generator fumes, headache and dizzy in a closed room, carbon monoxide. Look for headache, dizziness, nausea, confusion; several people ill at the same time, or pets sick."},{"symbol":"smoke_inhalation","source":"symbolic_books/medical/entries_firstaid.json","kind":"medical","hash":"b14d0327","text":"Breathing smoke can burn the airway and carry poisonous gases, even if the skin is not burned. Also called breathed smoke, house fire fumes, smoke in the lungs. Look for coughing, hoarse voice, black spit; singed nose hairs or soot around the mouth and nose. Move the person into fresh air, call emergency services, and keep them sitting upright and calm. Start CPR if they stop breathing."},{"symbol":"burn_degrees","source":"symbolic_books/medical/entries_firstaid.json","kind":"medical","hash":"f274f6dc","text":"Burns are graded by depth: first-degree is red and dry, second-degree blisters, and third-degree is white, brown, or charred and may be numb. Also called how bad is the burn, burn blisters, charred skin, degrees of burns. Look for first degree: red, dry, painful, no blisters; second degree: blisters, moist, very painful."},{"symbol":"chemical_burn_skin","source":"symbolic_books/medical/entries_firstaid.json","kind":"medical","hash":"e97af4cb","text":"A chemical burn keeps damaging skin until the chemical is removed, so flushing starts immediately. Also called acid on skin, bleach on skin, lye, caustic burn. Look for burning, redness, or blistering after contact; chemical container or label nearby. Remove contaminated clothing, brush off dry powder first, and flush with lots of cool running water for at least 20 minutes. Cover loosely and call poison control or emergency services."},{"symbol":"stroke_fast","source":"symbolic_books/medical/entries_firstaid.json","kind":"medical","hash":"2ae3f5ef","text":"A stroke is a blocked or burst blood vessel in the brain, and fast treatment limits the damage. Also called face drooping, slurred speech, arm weakness, mini stroke, TIA. Look for Face drooping on one side; Arm weakness or drifting down. Note the time symptoms started, call emergency services immediately, keep the person calm and seated or lying with the head slightly raised, and do not give food or drink."},{"symbol":"asthma_attack","source":"symbolic_books/medical/entries_firstaid.json","kind":"medical","hash":"618d1d3c","text":"An asthma attack is a tightening of the airways that makes breathing hard and noisy. Also called wheezing, cannot breathe tight chest, inhaler, shortness of breath. Look for wheezing, coughing, tight chest; struggling to speak a full sentence. Sit the person upright, help them use their own quick-relief inhaler as directed, keep them calm, and move them away from triggers. Call emergency help if it does not ease."},{"symbol":"anaphylaxis_signs","source":"symbolic_books/medical/entries_firstaid.json","kind":"medical","hash":"c6174033","text":"Anaphylaxis is a rapid, whole-body allergic reaction that can close the airway and drop blood pressure. Also called allergic reaction, swelling of throat, hives and trouble breathing, bee sting allergy, peanut allergy. Look for hives, swelling of the face, lips or tongue; wheezing, throat tightness, trouble breathing."},{"symbol":"bee_wasp_sting","source":"symbolic_books/medical/entries_firstaid.json","kind":"medical","hash":"6ae2f0cd","text":"A sting injects venom that causes pain and swelling; a honeybee leaves its stinger and venom sac in the skin. Also called bee sting, hornet sting, stung by a wasp, stinger. Look for sharp pain, a red welt, and local swelling; a small black stinger in the skin. Move away from the nest, scrape the stinger out with a card edge or fingernail, wash, and apply a cool pack. Watch for allergy signs for at least 30 minutes."},{"symbol":"spider_bites","source":"symbolic_books/medical/entries_firstaid.json","kind":"medical","hash":"ac7201fd","text":"Most spiders are harmless, but black widows and brown recluses have bites that need medical care. Also called spider bite, black widow, brown recluse, venomous spider. Look for black widow: shiny black spider with a red hourglass underneath; severe cramps and sweating; brown recluse: tan spider with a violin mark; a blister that can turn into a sore."},{"symbol":"scorpion_sting","source":"symbolic_books/medical/entries_firstaid.json","kind":"medical","hash":"533ce015","text":"Most scorpion stings hurt like a bee sting, but the bark scorpion of the US Southwest can cause serious nerve symptoms, especially in children. Also called stung by a scorpion, bark scorpion. Look for sharp burning pain, numbness, and tingling; drooling, odd eye movements, or twitching muscles in severe stings. Wash with soap and water, apply a cool pack, keep the area still, and get medical advice or call poison control. Shake out shoes and clothes in scorpion country."},{"symbol":"poison_ivy_rash","source":"symbolic_books/medical/entries_firstaid.json","kind":"medical","hash":"2ce2ea30","text":"Poison ivy, oak, and sumac carry an oil called urushiol that causes an itchy, blistering rash on contact. Also called itchy rash from a plant, leaves of three, urushiol, contact dermatitis. Look for streaks or patches of itchy red bumps and blisters; rash shows up hours to days after touching the plant. Wash skin with soap and cool water as soon as you can, then wash clothing and tools. Cool compresses and calamine can ease the itch."},{"symbol":"jellyfish_sting","source":"symbolic_books/medical/entries_firstaid.json","kind":"medical","hash":"4ecdcdbe","text":"Jellyfish tentacles carry stinging cells that keep firing; treat by removing them and soaking in hot water, not by rubbing. Also called jellyfish, portuguese man o war, stingray, sea urchin. Look for lines of welts where a tentacle touched; burning pain and itching. Rinse the area with seawater (not fresh water), pick off tentacles with tweezers or a gloved hand, and soak in hot water, as hot as is comfortable, for 20 to 45 minutes. Vinegar helps with some species."},{"symbol":"low_blood_sugar","source":"symbolic_books/medical/entries_firstaid.json","kind":"medical","hash":"420893ba","text":"Low blood sugar in a person with diabetes causes shakiness, sweating, and confusion and is fixed fast with sugar. Also called hypoglycemia, diabetic passed out, shaky and sweaty, insulin shock, blood sugar low. Look for shaky, sweaty, hungry, and anxious; confusion, slurred speech, or odd behavior. If they are awake and can swallow, give fast sugar such as juice, glucose tablets, or regular soda, then a snack. Call for help if it does not improve in about 15 minutes."},{"symbol":"fever_adult","source":"symbolic_books/medical/entries_firstaid.json","kind":"medical","hash":"6abadd71","text":"A fever is a body temperature of about 38 C (100.4 F) or higher, usually a sign the body is fighting an infection. Also called high temperature, running a fever, hot and chills. Look for hot skin, chills, sweating, and aches; temperature above 38 C (100.4 F). Rest, drink fluids, and dress lightly. Over-the-counter fever reducers can help if used as the label says. Watch for signs of getting worse."},{"symbol":"vomiting_diarrhea","source":"symbolic_books/medical/entries_firstaid.json","kind":"medical","hash":"8b1c64bc","text":"Vomiting and diarrhea are the body's way of clearing a gut infection or toxin; the main danger is dehydration. Also called stomach bug, food poisoning, traveler's diarrhea, gastroenteritis, upset stomach. Look for frequent loose stools or vomiting; dry mouth, dark urine, dizziness. Rest, take frequent small sips of clean water or oral rehydration solution, and eat bland food when you can. Wash hands carefully and keep clear of food prep."},{"symbol":"abdominal_pain_red_flags","source":"symbolic_books/medical/entries_firstaid.json","kind":"medical","hash":"c7026ff1","text":"Most belly pain is minor, but pain that is severe, constant, or moving to the lower right can be appendicitis or another emergency. Also called stomach ache, appendicitis, severe stomach pain, belly pain. Look for pain that starts near the navel and moves to the lower right; rigid, tender belly, fever, vomiting. Rest, take nothing by mouth if you may need surgery, and get medical care quickly if pain is severe or getting worse."},{"symbol":"childbirth_field","source":"symbolic_books/medical/entries_firstaid.json","kind":"medical","hash":"8cadf10c","text":"When a baby is crowning and cannot be reached by help in time, you can support the delivery, keep the baby warm, and wait for emergency services. Also called baby coming now, giving birth, delivery in the field, woman in labor. Look for contractions a minute or two apart, urge to push; the baby's head is visible."},{"symbol":"newborn_care","source":"symbolic_books/medical/entries_firstaid.json","kind":"medical","hash":"533509d8","text":"A newborn loses heat quickly, so drying, warming, and watching the breathing are the first jobs. Also called baby just born, keep a newborn warm, infant born outside. Look for baby cries or breathes within seconds; wet, slippery, and cold skin. Dry the baby, wrap in warm dry cloths with the head covered, and hold skin-to-skin against the mother. Keep the face clear and note the time of birth."},{"symbol":"miscarriage_bleeding","source":"symbolic_books/medical/entries_firstaid.json","kind":"medical","hash":"2794d0ec","text":"Bleeding, severe pain, or fainting in a pregnant woman can be serious and needs medical help. Also called bleeding while pregnant, miscarriage, pregnancy emergency. Look for vaginal bleeding with cramps or pain; dizzy, pale, faint, or severe one-sided belly pain. Have her lie down on her left side, keep her warm and calm, and get emergency help. Note how many pads she is soaking."},{"symbol":"altitude_sickness_levels","source":"symbolic_books/medical/entries_firstaid.json","kind":"medical","hash":"cdffd80c","text":"Altitude sickness is an illness from too little oxygen at height; mild forms ease with rest, but fluid on the lungs or brain is life-threatening. Also called AMS, HAPE, HACE, mountain sickness, can't breathe at altitude. Look for mild: headache, nausea, poor sleep, tiredness; HAPE: breathlessness at rest, wet cough, pink froth. Stop climbing, rest, drink water, and descend if symptoms are getting worse. Dangerous forms need immediate descent."},{"symbol":"poisoning_swallowed","source":"symbolic_books/medical/entries_firstaid.json","kind":"medical","hash":"5d1ed70b","text":"For a swallowed poison, the quickest help is poison control or emergency services, who tell you exactly what to do for that substance. Also called swallowed something poisonous, child ate pills, drank cleaner, poison control. Look for an opened container, plants, berries, or pills nearby; vomiting, drowsiness, confusion, or burns around the mouth."},{"symbol":"heavy_vs_minor_bleeding","source":"symbolic_books/medical/entries_firstaid.json","kind":"medical","hash":"d5b1fe6e","text":"Heavy bleeding is blood that spurts, pools, soaks through cloth, or does not slow after 10 minutes of firm pressure; a minor cut slows and stops with pressure. Also called is the bleeding serious, how bad is the bleeding, bleeding will not stop, bleeding a lot. Look for spurting, pulsing, or fast flowing blood; blood soaking through several layers or pooling on the ground."},{"symbol":"tourniquet_apply","source":"symbolic_books/medical/entries_firstaid.json","kind":"medical","hash":"f51f5c91","text":"A tourniquet is a tight band, ideally a commercial windlass device, and a last resort for life-threatening bleeding from an arm or leg when firm direct pressure has not stopped it. Also called CAT tourniquet, windlass tourniquet, TQ, strap to stop bleeding, tourniquet time. Look for life-threatening bleeding from an arm or leg; a traumatic amputation."},{"symbol":"tourniquet_bleeding_continues","source":"symbolic_books/medical/entries_firstaid.json","kind":"medical","hash":"0817a1cd","text":"If blood keeps flowing after a tourniquet is on, it is usually not tight enough or not high enough, and the fix is to tighten it or add a second one. Also called tourniquet not working, still bleeding with tourniquet, second tourniquet, tourniquet too loose. Look for blood still flowing or spurting below the band; a pulse can still be felt below it."},{"symbol":"wound_packing","source":"symbolic_books/medical/entries_firstaid.json","kind":"medical","hash":"eee166d3","text":"Packing means pushing gauze or clean cloth deep into a bleeding wound and pressing hard on it; it is used where a tourniquet will not work, such as the neck, armpit, or groin. Also called pack the wound, gauze in the wound, hemostatic gauze, wound that tourniquet cannot reach, neck bleeding. Look for deep wound at the neck, armpit, groin, or the top of a limb; blood welling from the depth of the wound."},{"symbol":"child_infant_choking","source":"symbolic_books/medical/entries_firstaid.json","kind":"medical","hash":"295534c3","text":"For an infant under one year, a choking rescue uses five back blows and five chest thrusts, not abdominal thrusts; a child over one uses abdominal thrusts like an adult. Also called baby choking, toddler choking, back blows infant, chest thrusts infant. Look for cannot cry, cough, or breathe, silent or high-pitched noise; blue lips or face."},{"symbol":"child_infant_cpr","source":"symbolic_books/medical/entries_firstaid.json","kind":"medical","hash":"68884f31","text":"CPR for children and infants keeps blood and oxygen moving; it is similar to adult CPR but uses less force, and breaths matter more because children often stop breathing first. Also called baby CPR, infant not breathing, child not breathing, rescue breaths for a baby. Look for not breathing or only gasping; limp and unresponsive."},{"symbol":"aed_use","source":"symbolic_books/medical/entries_firstaid.json","kind":"medical","hash":"811e7bf0","text":"An AED is a portable device that checks the heart's rhythm and, if needed, gives a shock; it talks you through each step. Also called defibrillator, automated external defibrillator, heart paddles, shock machine. Look for person is unresponsive and not breathing normally; voice prompts after you turn it on."},{"symbol":"naloxone_overdose","source":"symbolic_books/medical/entries_firstaid.json","kind":"medical","hash":"a1abccdb","text":"An opioid overdose slows or stops breathing, and naloxone (Narcan) is a medicine that can reverse it for a short time. Also called narcan, overdose, passed out from pills, blue lips pinpoint pupils, fentanyl overdose. Look for will not wake, slow or stopped breathing; blue or gray lips and skin."},{"symbol":"fracture_sprain_dislocation","source":"symbolic_books/medical/entries_firstaid.json","kind":"medical","hash":"864a1d39","text":"A fracture is a broken bone, a sprain is a stretched or torn ligament, and a dislocation is a joint forced out of place; the field answer is the same: stabilize and get care. Also called is it broken or sprained, sprain versus fracture, twisted ankle or broken ankle. Look for fracture: point pain on the bone, deformity, cannot bear weight; sprain: pain and swelling at a joint, some use possible."},{"symbol":"infant_hypothermia","source":"symbolic_books/medical/entries_firstaid.json","kind":"medical","hash":"c6a6741f","text":"Babies lose heat fast, and a cold infant may have bright red, cold skin and very low energy, not just shivering. Also called baby too cold, newborn cold, bright red cold skin baby, low energy baby. Look for bright red, cold skin; very low energy, weak cry, or refusing to feed. Take the baby indoors, remove damp clothing, and hold the baby skin to skin under dry blankets, with the head and a hat covered. Warm the room. Get urgent care at once."},{"symbol":"frostbite_dont","source":"symbolic_books/medical/entries_firstaid.json","kind":"medical","hash":"86143ad0","text":"Frostbitten skin is numb and easily damaged; rubbing, high heat, or thawing then re-freezing makes it worse. Also called rub frostbite, thaw frozen fingers, stove on frostbite, heating pad frostbite. Look for pale, waxy, hard, or blotchy skin that is numb; tingling and then loss of feeling."},{"symbol":"cold_water_shock","source":"symbolic_books/medical/entries_firstaid.json","kind":"medical","hash":"f9347b02","text":"Cold-water shock is the gasp and panic in the first minute after falling into very cold water, and it can cause drowning before hypothermia sets in. Also called fell through the ice, fell in cold water, capsized, cold shock, cold water immersion. Look for a sudden gasp, rapid breathing, and panic; swimming ability lost within minutes."},{"symbol":"call_911_dispatcher","source":"symbolic_books/medical/entries_firstaid.json","kind":"medical","hash":"505e0e3f","text":"When you call 911, stay on the line and give your location and what happened; the dispatcher will tell you what to do next. Also called what to say when I call 911, how to call for help, emergency call, giving my location. Look for your exact location, with a road, landmark, or GPS coordinates; what happened and how many people are hurt."},{"symbol":"describe_symptoms","source":"symbolic_books/medical/entries_firstaid.json","kind":"medical","hash":"5e4e3419","text":"Clear information helps a clinician decide fast: when it started, what makes it worse or better, what medicines and allergies the person has. Also called what to tell the doctor, how to explain symptoms, medical history for an emergency crew. Look for start time and how it began; what makes it better or worse."},{"symbol":"medication_questions","source":"symbolic_books/medical/entries_firstaid.json","kind":"medical","hash":"c4e16964","text":"For a missed dose, an expired medicine, or a drug interaction, read the label and ask a pharmacist; never share prescription drugs. Also called missed a pill, expired medicine, share a prescription, can I take this with that. Look for label directions for a missed dose; an expiration date. Check the label or the pharmacist or Poison Control line for a missed dose, and keep medicines in a cool, dry place. Ask a pharmacist before mixing medicines. Keep a list of what you take."},{"symbol":"lyme_signs","source":"symbolic_books/medical/entries_firstaid.json","kind":"medical","hash":"4540a465","text":"Lyme disease is spread by blacklegged (deer) ticks, and an expanding red rash, often with fever, chills, and aches, can appear three to thirty days after a bite. Also called bullseye rash, erythema migrans, tick fever, deer tick bite, Lyme. Look for a red expanding rash, sometimes with a clear center; fever, chills, headache, and tiredness."},{"symbol":"tick_prevention","source":"symbolic_books/medical/entries_firstaid.json","kind":"medical","hash":"cdbfa39c","text":"Preventing tick bites means covering skin, using repellent, and checking your whole body after being outdoors. Also called tick check, permethrin, stay safe from ticks, repellent for ticks. Look for tick season from spring through fall, with nymphs most active in late spring and summer; brush, leaf litter, and tall grass."},{"symbol":"snakebite_new_england","source":"symbolic_books/medical/entries_firstaid.json","kind":"medical","hash":"4a1baa6e","text":"New England has very few venomous snakes: the timber rattlesnake and the northern copperhead in a few southern areas; most snakes are harmless. Also called timber rattlesnake, northern copperhead, snake bit me, venomous snake, garter snake bite. Look for venomous bites leave two puncture marks with swelling and severe pain; timber rattlesnake has a rattle and a heavy body."},{"symbol":"nail_puncture","source":"symbolic_books/medical/entries_firstaid.json","kind":"medical","hash":"7d5e9ba7","text":"A puncture wound is narrow and deep, closes quickly, and traps germs, so it can get infected and may need a tetanus shot. Also called stepped on a nail, puncture in the foot, deep puncture. Look for a small hole with little bleeding; pain that gets worse in the next days. Let it bleed a little, wash with soap and clean water, and cover. See a clinician the same day for a deep wound, a rusty or dirty object, or a wound in the foot. Ask about a tetanus shot."},{"symbol":"high_blood_sugar","source":"symbolic_books/medical/entries_firstaid.json","kind":"medical","hash":"8d4d8631","text":"High blood sugar builds slowly with thirst, frequent urination, tiredness, and fruity breath, and it can become a medical emergency. Also called hyperglycemia, diabetic ketoacidosis, very thirsty and frequent urination, fruity breath. Look for extreme thirst and frequent urination; nausea, belly pain, and fast deep breathing."},{"symbol":"ear_sinus_pain","source":"symbolic_books/medical/entries_firstaid.json","kind":"medical","hash":"d7740a28","text":"Ear or sinus pain after swimming or a cold is often a mild infection, but severe pain, fever, or discharge needs a clinician. Also called swimmer's ear, earache, sinus pressure, ear infection. Look for ear pain that hurts when you pull on it; stuffy nose and pressure around the eyes. Keep the ear dry, use a warm compress, and take over-the-counter pain medicine as the label says. See a clinician if pain lasts more than a day or two or if there is fever or drainage."},{"symbol":"frostnip_vs_frostbite","source":"symbolic_books/medical/entries_firstaid.json","kind":"medical","hash":"3696163d","text":"Frostnip is a mild cold injury that turns skin pale and numb but is reversible with warming; frostbite is a deeper freeze injury. Also called cold fingers pale, nose turned white, ears numb. Look for frostnip: pale or red skin that tingles and returns to normal when warmed; frostbite: hard, waxy, numb skin that stays white or gray."},{"symbol":"cpr_when_to_start","source":"symbolic_books/medical/entries_firstaid.json","kind":"medical","hash":"3233abf1","text":"Start CPR for anyone who is unresponsive and not breathing, or only gasping, and call 911 first. Also called should I do CPR, no pulse, not breathing what do I do, hands only CPR. Look for no response to a shout or shoulder tap; not breathing, or only occasional gasps."},{"symbol":"offline_medical_reference","source":"symbolic_books/medical/reference.json","kind":"medical","hash":"e343eded","text":"Yehsa medical entries are offline reference labels for a personal survival agent. They are not a diagnosis, a prescription, or a substitute for emergency services. In an emergency, contact local emergency services."},{"symbol":"bleeding_pressure","source":"symbolic_books/medical/reference.json","kind":"medical","hash":"88711445","text":"Serious bleeding needs direct pressure with clean cloth and urgent human help. This note does not teach surgery or medication dosing."},{"symbol":"clean_water_before_care","source":"symbolic_books/medical/reference.json","kind":"medical","hash":"30e385ae","text":"Clean water matters before wound rinsing or rehydration. Boil water when you can. This is a field reminder, not clinical instruction."},{"symbol":"insect_bite_skin","source":"symbolic_books/medical/reference.json","kind":"medical","hash":"6dafdf2e","text":"A mosquito bite is usually a small itchy spot. Wash it with clean water. Swelling of the face or throat, or trouble breathing, needs a person and emergency services, not a plant."},{"symbol":"tourniquet_last_resort","source":"symbolic_books/medical/reference.json","kind":"medical","hash":"05fc2951","text":"A tourniquet is a last resort for life-threatening limb bleeding, when direct pressure with clean cloth does not stop the blood. Put it between the wound and the heart, and note the time it went on. Do not loosen it in the field. This is not surgery and not a substitute for emergency care."},{"symbol":"frostbite_warming","source":"symbolic_books/medical/reference.json","kind":"medical","hash":"14716026","text":"Frostbite is frozen skin, often fingers, toes, ears, or the nose. Get to shelter and put on dry layers. Do not rub the skin, and do not rub snow on it. Warm it against healthy skin or in warm, not hot, water, and do not walk on a thawed foot if you still must travel; this is not a substitute for emergency care."},{"symbol":"splint_immobilize","source":"symbolic_books/medical/reference.json","kind":"medical","hash":"f5a18d1c","text":"A splint keeps a suspected broken bone still, such as a broken arm or leg. Pad it, include the joints above and below, and tie it snug enough to stop motion without cutting off circulation. Skin beyond it should stay warm and able to feel. Do not set the bone; this is not surgery or a substitute for emergency care."},{"symbol":"nosebleed_forward","source":"symbolic_books/medical/reference.json","kind":"medical","hash":"76942826","text":"A nosebleed: sit up, lean forward, and pinch the soft part of the nose for ten minutes or more. Do not tilt the head back. Spit blood instead of swallowing it. Call emergency services if bleeding will not stop, started after a head injury, or the person is dizzy or weak."},{"symbol":"choking_thrusts","source":"symbolic_books/medical/reference.json","kind":"medical","hash":"5290f8ab","text":"Choking: if an adult or child cannot cough, speak, or breathe, give abdominal thrusts until the object comes out or they become unresponsive, then start CPR and call emergency services. For an infant, use back blows and chest thrusts, not abdominal thrusts. Call emergency services."},{"symbol":"cpr_rate_depth","source":"symbolic_books/medical/reference.json","kind":"medical","hash":"96a9b8de","text":"Start CPR when a person is unresponsive and not breathing normally. Call emergency services. Compress the center of the chest at 100 to 120 per minute. Adult depth is about 5 cm (2 inches). A child is about 5 cm. An infant is about 4 cm (1.5 inches). If you are trained, give 30 compressions then 2 breaths. This is not a certification."},{"symbol":"epinephrine_auto","source":"symbolic_books/medical/reference.json","kind":"medical","hash":"f37b2f7d","text":"Anaphylaxis can include trouble breathing, swelling of the face or throat, or collapse. Use an epinephrine auto-injector as the label printed on the device says, into the outer thigh, and hold it for the time the label states. Call emergency services. This note gives no other drug dose."},{"symbol":"shock_position","source":"symbolic_books/medical/reference.json","kind":"medical","hash":"1559e49e","text":"Shock: pale, cold, fast pulse, or confusion after injury or illness. Have the person lie down, keep them warm, and raise the legs if no injury makes that unsafe. Do not give food or drink. Call emergency services."},{"symbol":"seizure_protect","source":"symbolic_books/medical/reference.json","kind":"medical","hash":"a580efdd","text":"During a seizure, keep the person from hitting nearby objects, do not restrain them, and do not put anything in the mouth. Time the seizure. Call emergency services if it lasts more than a few minutes, repeats, or the person is injured, pregnant, or does not wake."},{"symbol":"chemical_eye_flush","source":"symbolic_books/medical/reference.json","kind":"medical","hash":"4c377547","text":"Chemical in an eye: flush with clean running water for many minutes, from the nose outward, and remove contact lenses if that is easy. Do not use drops you have not been told to use. Call emergency services or a poison center."},{"symbol":"altitude_descend","source":"symbolic_books/medical/reference.json","kind":"medical","hash":"6953a1ef","text":"Altitude sickness: headache, nausea, or unusual tiredness after going higher. Stop climbing, rest, and descend. Confusion, trouble walking, or breathlessness at rest needs emergency services and a descent. This is not a drug dose."},{"symbol":"heart_attack_signs","source":"symbolic_books/medical/reference.json","kind":"medical","hash":"8f513ffd","text":"Heart attack signs can include chest pressure, pain spreading to the arm, neck, or jaw, shortness of breath, sweat, or nausea. Call emergency services immediately and have the person rest. Do not wait to see if it passes. This is not a diagnosis."},{"symbol":"burn_cool_water","source":"symbolic_books/medical/reference.json","kind":"medical","hash":"c947d927","text":"A burn: cool it with cool running water, not ice. Do not put butter, oil, or ointment on it. Cover it with a clean cloth. Call emergency services for a large burn, a burn on the face, hands, or genitals, or any burn with breathing trouble."},{"symbol":"tick_removal","source":"symbolic_books/medical/reference.json","kind":"medical","hash":"a943b26f","text":"Tick removal: use fine tweezers, grasp the tick close to the skin, and pull straight out with steady pressure. Do not twist, burn, or cover the tick with oil. Wash the skin. Watch for a spreading rash or fever and get medical care. This is not a diagnosis."},{"symbol":"oral_rehydration","source":"symbolic_books/medical/reference.json","kind":"medical","hash":"d633480e","text":"Oral rehydration for dehydration from diarrhea, using clean water: one liter of water, six level teaspoons of sugar, and half a level teaspoon of salt. Sip it. Call emergency services if the person is confused, cannot drink, or is a limp infant. This is not a drug dose."},{"symbol":"sprain_rice","source":"symbolic_books/medical/reference.json","kind":"medical","hash":"4f76ef50","text":"A sprain: rest, ice wrapped in cloth, a snug compression bandage, and elevation (RICE). Do not force the joint. Call emergency services or a clinician if the limb looks deformed, will not bear weight, or the pain is severe."},{"symbol":"spinal_still","source":"symbolic_books/medical/reference.json","kind":"medical","hash":"d5577bfe","text":"Spinal precautions: if the neck or back may be injured, do not move the person, do not twist the head, and keep them still. Call emergency services. Only move them if the place itself is immediately dangerous, and then keep the head and body in line."},{"symbol":"cut_needs_stitches","source":"symbolic_books/medical/reference.json","kind":"medical","hash":"f5a0bdb5","text":"A cut may need stitches when it gapes open, is deep, is on the face, keeps bleeding through pressure, or has something stuck in it. Cover it with clean cloth and call emergency services or a clinician. This note does not teach how to stitch."},{"symbol":"mosquito_camp","source":"symbolic_books/survival/camp.json","kind":"survival","hash":"684a4dfc","text":"Cover skin and avoid standing water when you can, so fewer mosquitoes bite. A plant on the skin does not replace that, and it does not treat diseases mosquitoes can carry."},{"symbol":"camp_site","source":"symbolic_books/survival/camp.json","kind":"survival","hash":"2924fa17","text":"Camp site: pick flat ground out of a drainage, off the ridge, and clear of dead limbs. Face the opening away from the night wind. A spot near a stream is handy and also a flood risk, so step uphill a little. This is planning, not a permit to camp where rules forbid it."},{"symbol":"camp_sleep","source":"symbolic_books/survival/camp.json","kind":"survival","hash":"d9bcac02","text":"Sleep: insulation under you matters as much as the cover over you. Change out of wet clothes before you lie down, and keep a dry layer in the bag. Food and scented items stay outside the sleep spot. A closed shelter still needs air. This note does not treat cold injury."},{"symbol":"camp_kitchen","source":"symbolic_books/survival/camp.json","kind":"survival","hash":"f0e232ff","text":"Kitchen: cook and store food downwind of where you sleep, and seal it so animals are not fed. Drown the fire or stove and feel for heat before you leave. Strain cloudy water, then boil it, before it goes in the pot. Known food only. This is not a foraging menu."},{"symbol":"camp_sanitation","source":"symbolic_books/survival/camp.json","kind":"survival","hash":"aabd9829","text":"Sanitation: carry waste out when the land requires it. Otherwise dig a small cathole well away from water, camp, and trails, then cover it. Soap stays out of the creek. Hands are cleaned away from the stream. This is a hygiene reminder, not a medical treatment."},{"symbol":"camp_leave","source":"symbolic_books/survival/camp.json","kind":"survival","hash":"0d38d16e","text":"Leave the site: pack out what you carried in, put back natural cover you moved, and drown the fire until it is cold. The next person should not be able to tell you cooked there. A waypoint you saved stays on your device. Erasing the camp from the land is the point."},{"symbol":"white_tailed_deer","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_animals.json","kind":"survival","hash":"fd6103a6","text":"The white-tailed deer is the common deer of the East, brown with a white belly and a tail it raises like a white flag when alarmed. Also called whitetail, white-tail deer, deer, buck, doe. Look for brown coat, white underside, tail raised like a white flag; bucks carry antlers from late summer to winter. Deer are the main big-game animal in New England; check your state's season and tag rules before hunting."},{"symbol":"moose","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_animals.json","kind":"survival","hash":"b84125b0","text":"The moose is the largest deer, with a long face, a hanging dewlap, and broad flat antlers on bulls. Also called Alces, bull moose, cow moose. Look for huge with a long face, dewlap, and broad flat antlers on bulls; dark body on long pale legs. Watch from at least 50 yards (about 45 m). If one lays its ears back or raises its hackles, back away and put a tree between you."},{"symbol":"elk","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_animals.json","kind":"survival","hash":"d5f873d9","text":"The elk is a large deer with a pale rump, a dark neck, and branching antlers on bulls. Also called wapiti, Cervus canadensis, bull elk. Look for large with a pale rump and branching antlers; bugling calls in fall. Elk are rare in New England; a big brown deer there is more likely a moose or white-tailed deer."},{"symbol":"black_bear","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_animals.json","kind":"survival","hash":"00fb9430","text":"The black bear is the only bear in the East, black or brown, with a straight face profile and no shoulder hump. Also called bear, American black bear, Ursus americanus. Look for black or brown with a straight muzzle and no shoulder hump; tall pointed ears. Make noise on the trail, store food and trash away from camp, and if you meet one, stand tall, talk firmly, and back away slowly."},{"symbol":"coyote","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_animals.json","kind":"survival","hash":"8cacd701","text":"The eastern coyote is a slim, dog-like wild canine with a bushy tail carried low and pointed ears. Also called eastern coyote, coywolf, Canis latrans, brush wolf. Look for slim dog-like with a bushy low tail and pointed ears; yips and howls at night. If one comes close, make yourself big, shout, and throw something toward it."},{"symbol":"red_fox","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_animals.json","kind":"survival","hash":"40711227","text":"The red fox is a small wild canine with a red coat, black legs, and a white tail tip. Also called fox, Vulpes vulpes. Look for red coat, black legs, white tail tip; bushy tail longer than half its body. Watch from a distance; foxes are usually shy."},{"symbol":"bobcat","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_animals.json","kind":"survival","hash":"7ea91939","text":"The bobcat is a medium wild cat with a short bobbed tail, tufted ears, and a spotted coat. Also called wildcat, Lynx rufus, bay lynx. Look for short tail with a black tip, tufted ears, spotted coat; tail tip black on top, white below. Bobcats avoid people; enjoy the sighting from where you are."},{"symbol":"porcupine","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_animals.json","kind":"survival","hash":"25cd2289","text":"The porcupine is a slow, round rodent covered in barbed quills. Also called porky, Erethizon dorsatum, quill pig. Look for round quill-covered body, slow waddle; chewed bark high on trees. Leave it alone and keep dogs away. A pet with quills needs a vet."},{"symbol":"raccoon","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_animals.json","kind":"survival","hash":"8f0d4a99","text":"The raccoon is a gray, masked, ring-tailed animal that raids camps and trash at night. Also called coon, Procyon lotor, trash panda. Look for black mask and ringed tail; handprint-like tracks near water. Lock food away at night and keep a clean camp."},{"symbol":"striped_skunk","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_animals.json","kind":"survival","hash":"02f4a424","text":"The striped skunk is a black animal with white stripes down its back that sprays a strong musk when threatened. Also called skunk, Mephitis. Look for black with white stripes down the back; stamps its feet and raises its tail before spraying. Back away slowly if it stamps or turns."},{"symbol":"beaver","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_animals.json","kind":"survival","hash":"b4736ccd","text":"The beaver is a large water rodent with a flat paddle tail that builds dams and lodges. Also called American beaver, Castor canadensis. Look for flat paddle tail, dam and cut sapling stumps; tooth-chewed pointed stumps near water. Beaver ponds flood trails and make routes longer; plan around them."},{"symbol":"snowshoe_hare","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_animals.json","kind":"survival","hash":"013ac21a","text":"The snowshoe hare is a northern hare with huge furry hind feet whose coat turns white in winter. Also called varying hare, Lepus americanus, snowshoe rabbit. Look for large back feet, coat white in winter and brown in summer; brown in summer, white in winter. Hares are legal small game in many states; check your state's rules."},{"symbol":"wild_turkey","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_animals.json","kind":"survival","hash":"36aadcaa","text":"The wild turkey is a large dark bird with a bare red-blue head and a fanned tail. Also called turkey, Meleagris gallopavo, gobbler, tom turkey. Look for large dark bird with a bare head and fanned tail; toms gobble in spring. Turkey season has its own rules; identify the bird and what is beyond it before every shot."},{"symbol":"ruffed_grouse","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_animals.json","kind":"survival","hash":"2a265317","text":"The ruffed grouse is a brown or gray barred woodland bird with a fan tail and a dark neck ruff. Also called partridge, Bonasa umbellus, grouse, pa'tridge. Look for brown barred bird with a fan tail and dark neck ruff; drumming sound like a starting engine in spring. Grouse are upland game in New England; check season rules."},{"symbol":"bald_eagle","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_animals.json","kind":"survival","hash":"d5aa50e6","text":"The bald eagle is a large raptor whose adults have a white head and tail and a yellow bill. Also called eagle, Haliaeetus leucocephalus, American eagle. Look for large dark bird with a white head and tail on adults; young birds are mottled brown for four years. Watch and photograph from a distance, especially near nests."},{"symbol":"great_horned_owl","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_animals.json","kind":"survival","hash":"14ab19c2","text":"The great horned owl is a large owl with ear tufts, yellow eyes, and a deep hooting call. Also called hoot owl, Bubo virginianus, cat owl. Look for large owl with ear tufts and yellow eyes; white throat patch. Enjoy it from a distance, especially near nests in late winter."},{"symbol":"canada_goose","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_animals.json","kind":"survival","hash":"cdcfa082","text":"The Canada goose is a large goose with a black neck and head and a white chin strap. Also called honker, Branta canadensis, goose. Look for black neck and head with a white chin strap; V-shaped flocks with loud honking. Geese are migratory birds with federal and state season rules; check both."},{"symbol":"rattlesnake","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_animals.json","kind":"survival","hash":"8a252fd7","text":"The timber rattlesnake is a heavy-bodied venomous snake with a broad triangular head and a rattle on its tail. Also called timber rattlesnake, rattler, Crotalus horridus. Look for thick body, triangular head, a rattle on the tail; dark crossbands on a yellow or dark body. Rattlesnakes are rare and protected in much of New England. Back away slowly and walk around."},{"symbol":"copperhead","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_animals.json","kind":"survival","hash":"c0ab94e8","text":"The copperhead is a venomous snake with a copper-colored head and tan hourglass bands. Also called Agkistrodon contortrix, highland moccasin. Look for copper head, tan body with hourglass bands; bands narrow on the back and wide on the sides. Copperheads live in southern New England's rocky woods. Back away slowly."},{"symbol":"cottonmouth","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_animals.json","kind":"survival","hash":"8b55ccc4","text":"The cottonmouth is a heavy, dark venomous water snake that gapes to show a white mouth. Also called water moccasin, Agkistrodon piscivorus. Look for heavy dark water snake with a white mouth when it gapes; swims with its body high on the water. Cottonmouths do not live in New England; a dark snake in water there is almost always a northern water snake."},{"symbol":"eastern_garter_snake","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_animals.json","kind":"survival","hash":"56ba15e8","text":"The eastern garter snake is a slim, harmless snake with three pale stripes along its body. Also called garter snake, garden snake, Thamnophis sirtalis. Look for slim, three pale stripes along the body; often basks on paths and rocks. It is harmless; let it go on its way."},{"symbol":"northern_water_snake","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_animals.json","kind":"survival","hash":"344ffc6c","text":"The northern water snake is a common, non-venomous banded brown snake found in and near water. Also called water snake, Nerodia sipedon. Look for banded brown snake near water; round pupils. It is not venomous; give it room."},{"symbol":"blacklegged_tick","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_animals.json","kind":"survival","hash":"6aae89a6","text":"The blacklegged tick, or deer tick, is a tiny dark tick that spreads Lyme disease, anaplasmosis, and babesiosis. Also called deer tick, Ixodes scapularis, Lyme tick. Look for tiny dark tick; adult females have an orange-red body and black plate. Check your whole body after time outside, and remove ticks with fine tweezers pulled straight out."},{"symbol":"american_dog_tick","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_animals.json","kind":"survival","hash":"9e10b10c","text":"The American dog tick is a larger brown tick with cream markings that can carry Rocky Mountain spotted fever. Also called dog tick, wood tick, Dermacentor variabilis. Look for larger brown tick with white-cream markings on the back; common in grassy fields and trail edges. Remove it with fine tweezers close to the skin and wash the bite."},{"symbol":"lone_star_tick","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_animals.json","kind":"survival","hash":"bc69f311","text":"The lone star tick is a reddish-brown tick whose females have one white dot on the back; its bites are linked to a red-meat allergy. Also called Amblyomma americanum, turkey tick. Look for reddish-brown tick with one white dot on the back of females; aggressive biter, spreading north. Remove it with fine tweezers close to the skin."},{"symbol":"black_widow","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_animals.json","kind":"survival","hash":"969ad15a","text":"The black widow is a shiny black spider with a red hourglass under its belly whose bite needs medical care. Also called widow spider, Latrodectus. Look for shiny black spider with a red hourglass underneath; messy webs under rocks, logs, and outhouse seats. Shake out gloves, boots, and firewood before use."},{"symbol":"bald_faced_hornet","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_animals.json","kind":"survival","hash":"6a76c329","text":"The bald-faced hornet is a black-and-white wasp that builds a gray paper nest in trees and defends it fiercely. Also called white-faced hornet, Dolichovespula maculata. Look for black and white wasp with a grey paper nest the size of a football; nests hang from branches or eaves. Walk wide around nests."},{"symbol":"yellowjacket","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_animals.json","kind":"survival","hash":"2d35589a","text":"The yellowjacket is a black-and-yellow wasp that nests in the ground and stings repeatedly. Also called yellow jacket, ground wasp, Vespula. Look for black and yellow wasp that nests underground; busy hole in the ground. Watch for ground nests when clearing a camp or trail."},{"symbol":"wild_boar","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_animals.json","kind":"survival","hash":"e0dbc5f9","text":"The wild boar, or feral hog, is a dark bristly pig with a long snout that can be aggressive. Also called feral hog, feral pig, wild pig, Sus scrofa. Look for dark bristly pig with a long snout; rooted-up ground. Feral hogs are not established in New England; report one to your state wildlife agency."},{"symbol":"fire_site_choice","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_fire.json","kind":"survival","hash":"46206f67","text":"A safe fire site is on bare mineral soil, clear of overhanging branches, dry grass, and roots, and downwind from your shelter. Also called where to build a fire, fire ring, keep a fire from spreading, fire pit. Look for bare soil or sand, or an existing ring; a cleared circle about 10 feet (3 m) wide. Scrape away leaves and duff to bare soil, build a ring of rocks, keep water or a shovel at hand, and keep it small. Check local rules first."},{"symbol":"fire_tinder_kindling_fuel","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_fire.json","kind":"survival","hash":"d826dc8e","text":"Tinder is fine dry material that catches a spark, kindling is pencil-sized sticks that carry the flame, and fuel is the larger wood that burns for hours. Also called what is tinder, what burns best, fire materials, birch bark, fatwood. Look for tinder: birch bark, fatwood shavings, dry grass, cattail fluff, cotton with petroleum jelly; kindling: dry twigs from pencil lead to thumb thick."},{"symbol":"fire_build_teepee","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_fire.json","kind":"survival","hash":"c68a8791","text":"A teepee fire is a cone of kindling leaned over a nest of tinder, lit from the windward side so the flame climbs. Also called how to start a campfire, teepee fire lay, start a fire. Look for tinder bundle in the center; kindling leaning inward like a tent. Place the tinder bundle, lean kindling around it, leave a gap on the windward side, light the tinder, and add small fuel as it catches. Blow gently at the base and add bigger pieces slowly."},{"symbol":"fire_wet_wood","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_fire.json","kind":"survival","hash":"28a243ad","text":"In rain, use dry material from under cover: dead branches still on trees, inner bark, and shaved wood, and protect the flame under a tarp. Also called fire in the rain, wet wood fire, damp tinder, fire in snow. Look for dead lower branches that snap; split wet logs to reach dry centers."},{"symbol":"fire_snow","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_fire.json","kind":"survival","hash":"22bccbe1","text":"A fire in snow needs a platform of green logs or stones so it does not sink and go out. Also called fire on snow, build a platform for a fire. Look for a platform of green logs, flat rocks, or a layer of sticks; a windbreak. Stamp down the snow, lay a log platform, and build the fire on top. Keep dry tinder against your body, and feed it slowly."},{"symbol":"fire_out_completely","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_fire.json","kind":"survival","hash":"db787c5c","text":"A fire is out only when the ashes are cold to the touch; drown it, stir it, and drown it again. Also called put out the campfire, drown the fire, cold fire, douse. Look for steam or hissing means it is still hot; no glowing embers. Let the fire burn down, pour water on the embers, stir the ashes and soil with a stick, and add more water until nothing hisses. Feel for heat with the back of your hand."},{"symbol":"stove_vs_open_fire","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_fire.json","kind":"survival","hash":"e8a240cd","text":"A camp stove is more controlled and leaves less trace than an open fire, but any stove, grill, or charcoal burner must never be used indoors, in a tent, or in a garage. Also called cook on a stove, charcoal grill indoors, camping stove safety. Look for stoves burn liquid or gas fuel; charcoal makes carbon monoxide. Cook outdoors on a stable, flat surface, away from tents and dry grass, and have water handy. Let it cool before refueling."},{"symbol":"fire_legal_ban","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_fire.json","kind":"survival","hash":"adda1abf","text":"Fire rules vary by state, town, and land, and bans are common in dry or windy weather, so check the official source before you light one. Also called can I build a fire, burn ban, campfire permit. Look for a burn ban or a red flag warning; state, town, or land-manager rules. Check with the local fire department, the state forest service, or the land manager, and follow any ban. If fires are banned, use a stove only if rules allow."},{"symbol":"fire_dont_burn","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_fire.json","kind":"survival","hash":"f3323dc8","text":"Never burn poison ivy, treated lumber, painted wood, plastic, or trash; the smoke is toxic. Also called toxic smoke, burn poison ivy, treated lumber, trash fire. Look for poison ivy smoke carries irritating oil; treated lumber has chemicals. Burn only clean, dry, untreated wood. Pack out trash."},{"symbol":"fire_smoke_wind","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_fire.json","kind":"survival","hash":"46ca5559","text":"Smoke drifts downwind, so sit upwind or to the side, and dry wood burns cleaner. Also called smoke in my face, sit upwind of a fire, smoky fire. Look for smoke blowing toward your seat; a smoky fire from wet wood. Move your seat upwind, burn dry wood, and keep the fire small. If it keeps smoking, stir and add dry fuel."},{"symbol":"fire_signal","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_fire.json","kind":"survival","hash":"52fa5b86","text":"A signal fire is a fire built to be seen from a distance, with green boughs for white smoke by day and bright fuel at night. Also called fire to be found, smoke signal, three fires. Look for three fires in a triangle is a distress signal; green leaves make dense smoke. Build it on an open spot, keep tinder dry and ready, and light it when an aircraft or searchers are close. Add green boughs for smoke by day."},{"symbol":"fire_lighters_ferro","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_fire.json","kind":"survival","hash":"0213ada9","text":"A ferrocerium rod throws hot sparks when scraped, and it works wet; matches and lighters are easier but fail when wet or empty. Also called how to use a ferro rod, fire steel, waterproof matches, bic lighter. Look for scrape the rod down with a sharp spine, keeping it close to the tinder; carry two ways to light. Hold the rod near the tinder and pull the striker backward fast. Carry a lighter, matches in a waterproof case, and a ferro rod as a backup."},{"symbol":"fire_bank_ember","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_fire.json","kind":"survival","hash":"1e7276e8","text":"A viable ember is glowing red and spreads when you blow on it; a dead ember is black and cool. Also called viable ember, char cloth, keep a fire overnight. Look for a bright orange glow when blown on; char cloth or punky wood catches a spark. Place the ember in a nest of fine tinder, fold the tinder around it, and blow gently until it flames. Bank a fire at night with large logs and ash."},{"symbol":"hungry_in_woods","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_food.json","kind":"survival","hash":"4f1941a9","text":"If you are hungry in the woods, eat the food you carried and focus first on water, shelter, warmth, and being found, because a person can go weeks without food but only days without water. Also called I am hungry, no food in the woods, need to eat, starving lost. Look for a hunger that feels urgent but is not an emergency; packed food, even small amounts."},{"symbol":"food_danger_zone","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_food.json","kind":"survival","hash":"bbd0d5df","text":"The danger zone is 40 to 140 F (4 to 60 C), where bacteria multiply fast, so perishable food should not stay out more than two hours, or one hour above 90 F. Also called how long can food sit out, bacteria grow in food, two hour rule, food temperature danger zone. Look for perishable food left out on a counter or picnic table; food not kept cold in a cooler."},{"symbol":"food_safe_cook_temps","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_food.json","kind":"survival","hash":"94d853fe","text":"Use a food thermometer: 165 F (74 C) for poultry and leftovers, 160 F (71 C) for ground meats and egg dishes, and 145 F (63 C) for whole cuts of meat and fish with a three-minute rest. Also called what temperature to cook chicken, how done should venison be, meat thermometer, ground meat temperature. Look for poultry and reheated food: 165 F; ground beef, pork, and ground game: 160 F."},{"symbol":"food_leftovers_safety","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_food.json","kind":"survival","hash":"ecf0193a","text":"Leftovers are safe for three to four days in the fridge and picnic or cooler food is safe only if it stayed at 40 F or below; when in doubt, throw it out. Also called is this food still good, leftovers how long, cooler food melted ice, can I eat this. Look for how long it sat in the danger zone; a cooler with ice still present and food cold."},{"symbol":"food_field_hygiene","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_food.json","kind":"survival","hash":"b1999c96","text":"Wash hands and tools, keep raw meat separate from ready-to-eat food, and cook to a safe temperature. Also called wash hands before eating, keep raw meat separate, clean cutting board outdoors. Look for hands washed with soap or sanitizer; separate cutting boards and knives. Wash hands with soap and treated water before touching food, bag raw meat separately, wash utensils, and keep the cooler in shade."},{"symbol":"food_outage","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_food.json","kind":"survival","hash":"f7f5094a","text":"A closed refrigerator keeps food safe about 4 hours without power; a full freezer holds for about 48 hours (24 if half full) if the door stays shut. Also called power outage food, how long does a fridge stay cold, freezer without power, throw out food. Look for a thermometer in the fridge at 40 F or below; ice crystals still in the freezer food."},{"symbol":"jerky_drying_meat","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_food.json","kind":"survival","hash":"7b9dceff","text":"Safe jerky starts with heating the meat to a safe internal temperature before or during drying, and the drying alone does not kill bacteria. Also called dry meat, venison jerky, dehydrating meat. Look for lean meat sliced thin; heat to 160 F (71 C), poultry to 165 F, then dry at 130 to 140 F (54 to 60 C). Follow USDA guidance: marinate in the fridge, steam or oven-heat to the right internal temperature, then dry. Store in a cool dry place."},{"symbol":"home_canning_safe","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_food.json","kind":"survival","hash":"03c54a0e","text":"Low-acid foods such as meat and vegetables must be canned in a pressure canner following the USDA guide, because boiling-water canning cannot make them safe from botulism. Also called canning venison, botulism, low-acid canning, pressure canner. Look for a pressure canner with a tested gauge; a bulging lid, spurting liquid, or a bad smell. Follow the USDA Complete Guide to Home Canning for your food, jar size, and altitude. Check each jar seal before storage."},{"symbol":"food_allergies","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_food.json","kind":"survival","hash":"6ec0efc7","text":"Food allergies can cause hives, swelling, or trouble breathing within minutes; carry any prescribed epinephrine and tell your group. Also called food allergy outdoors, allergic reaction to nuts, epinephrine on a trip. Look for hives, swelling of the lips or tongue, wheezing; vomiting or dizziness after eating. Know your allergens, tell your group, carry medicine, and avoid new wild foods. Use epinephrine as prescribed and call 911."},{"symbol":"fish_cleaning_spoilage","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_food.json","kind":"survival","hash":"87428110","text":"Gut and chill fish quickly; cook it to 145 F; spoiled fish smells sour, feels slimy, and has cloudy eyes. Also called how to clean a fish, is the fish bad, fish smell. Look for fresh: clear eyes, firm flesh, mild smell; spoiled: sour smell, slime, sunken or cloudy eyes. Gut the fish soon after catching, keep it on ice, wash your hands, and cook it to 145 F. Check the state health department's fish advisories for mercury and other contaminants."},{"symbol":"shellfish_closures","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_food.json","kind":"survival","hash":"a16e3d28","text":"Shellfish can carry toxins from algal blooms, and cooking does not remove them, so follow the official closure notices. Also called clams closed, paralytic shellfish poisoning, red tide, mussels safe. Look for a posted closure or red tide notice; numbness around the mouth after eating shellfish. Check the state shellfish closure page before you harvest; if you cannot confirm that the area is open, do not eat shellfish from it."},{"symbol":"hunt_before_you_go","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_hunting.json","kind":"survival","hash":"5992b2ac","text":"Before a hunt you need a valid license and any tags for the animal, the hunter orange your state requires, and this season's official rules, which change every year. Also called hunting license, deer tag, what do I need to hunt, hunter orange, blaze orange. Look for license and tags on you, not at home; orange hat and vest where the state requires it."},{"symbol":"hunt_legal_light_note","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_hunting.json","kind":"survival","hash":"450ecc85","text":"Legal shooting light is the window each day when hunting is allowed; many states use about 30 minutes before sunrise to 30 minutes after sunset, but the exact rule is set by each state and sometimes by species. Also called shooting hours, legal shooting time, half hour before sunrise, when can I shoot. Look for sunrise and sunset for your exact spot; the state's own rule for the species."},{"symbol":"hunt_firearm_safety","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_hunting.json","kind":"survival","hash":"0b7bae21","text":"Field firearm safety comes down to four habits: treat every gun as loaded, keep the muzzle pointed in a safe direction, keep your finger off the trigger until you are ready to shoot, and be sure of your target and what is beyond it. Also called gun safety, hunting gun safety, muzzle control, treat every gun as loaded, firearm rules. Look for muzzle never sweeps a person, including yourself; finger rests along the frame, outside the trigger guard."},{"symbol":"hunt_target_beyond","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_hunting.json","kind":"survival","hash":"ab1fc3d5","text":"Before any shot you must be certain of the species, sex if it matters for your tag, and that a safe backstop such as rising ground stops the bullet; anything uncertain means no shot. Also called identify before you shoot, not sure what it is, is it a deer, backstop, what is behind the target. Look for whole animal seen, not just a patch of brown or a sound; no house, road, trail, or person in the line of fire."},{"symbol":"hunt_shot_placement","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_hunting.json","kind":"survival","hash":"4143a7e6","text":"An ethical shot aims at the heart and lungs from a broadside or slightly quartering-away angle within the range you have practiced, so the animal goes down quickly. Also called where to shoot a deer, ethical shot, vital zone, heart lung shot, after the shot. Look for the animal standing still and broadside; a clear lane with no branches in the path."},{"symbol":"hunt_blood_trail","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_hunting.json","kind":"survival","hash":"13143072","text":"Following a hit deer means starting at the spot it stood, finding hair and blood, and moving slowly from sign to sign while marking each spot. Also called blood trail, tracking a wounded deer, following blood, lost blood trail, how long to wait before tracking. Look for bright red, frothy blood often means a lung hit; dark blood or green matter can mean a gut hit and a longer wait."},{"symbol":"hunt_lost_animal","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_hunting.json","kind":"survival","hash":"7697a21c","text":"When a hit animal cannot be found, the ethical next steps are a careful grid search, a second person's eyes, and, where the state allows it, a leashed tracking dog. Also called can't find my deer, lost the blood trail, wounded deer lost, deer ran off. Look for the last marked sign; water, thick cover, and downhill paths nearby."},{"symbol":"field_dressing_deer","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_hunting.json","kind":"survival","hash":"19102c30","text":"Field dressing removes the internal organs soon after the kill so the body heat can escape and the meat cools quickly. Also called gutting a deer, field dress, clean a deer, how to gut a deer. Look for gloves on, sharp knife, clean water or wipes; belly opened shallow so the gut is not cut. Tag the animal first if your state requires it, open the body from the pelvis toward the ribs with the blade edge up, remove the organs, then prop the cavity open in shade."},{"symbol":"meat_care_temperature","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_hunting.json","kind":"survival","hash":"e21e5597","text":"Game meat stays safe when it is cooled below 40 °F (4 °C) quickly; in warm weather that means skinning, quartering, and icing within hours. Also called meat care warm weather, is my deer meat spoiled, hanging a deer, cooling venison, game meat spoiled. Look for air temperature above 40 °F means the clock is running; a sour or rotten smell, slime, or green color means spoiled."},{"symbol":"cwd_awareness","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_hunting.json","kind":"survival","hash":"3b2423b9","text":"Chronic wasting disease is a fatal prion disease of deer, elk, and moose; no human case has been confirmed, but health agencies advise people not to eat meat from an infected animal. Also called CWD, chronic wasting disease, zombie deer, sick looking deer, deer disease. Look for a thin, drooling, stumbling, or unafraid deer; animals found dead."},{"symbol":"game_cooking_temps","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_hunting.json","kind":"survival","hash":"fd5a23a5","text":"Game meat is safest cooked to a measured temperature: whole cuts of venison to at least 145 °F (63 °C) with a 3-minute rest, ground venison to 160 °F (71 °C), and wild birds to 165 °F (74 °C). Also called venison temperature, cook deer meat, ground venison temperature, wild game cooking. Look for a food thermometer in the thickest part; ground meat cooked through. Use a thermometer, keep raw game apart from cooked food, and wash hands and boards after handling raw meat."},{"symbol":"tree_stand_safety","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_hunting.json","kind":"survival","hash":"3fd54c0d","text":"Tree-stand safety means staying attached to the tree from the moment you leave the ground until you are back on it, with a full-body harness and a lifeline or tether. Also called tree stand, deer stand fall, safety harness, full body harness, climbing a stand. Look for full-body harness worn and connected; stand straps and chains checked before each season."},{"symbol":"hunt_fence_crossing","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_hunting.json","kind":"survival","hash":"f8a9359a","text":"Crossing a fence, ditch, log, or stream with a firearm is safe only with the action open and the gun unloaded, set down on the far side with the muzzle pointed away. Also called crossing a fence with a gun, climbing over a log with a gun, crossing a stream with a rifle. Look for action open and chamber empty; muzzle pointed away from you and others."},{"symbol":"small_game_tularemia","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_hunting.json","kind":"survival","hash":"680cc923","text":"Tularemia is a bacterial disease people can catch from handling or eating rabbits, hares, and rodents; gloves and thorough cooking prevent most cases. Also called rabbit fever, tularemia, cleaning rabbits, squirrel hunting safety. Look for a rabbit or squirrel that is slow, sick, or found dead; white spots on the liver. Wear gloves when skinning and cleaning, cook the meat thoroughly, and wash hands and tools afterward."},{"symbol":"waterfowl_rules_link","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_hunting.json","kind":"survival","hash":"36660606","text":"Ducks, geese, and other migratory birds are hunted under federal rules plus state rules, which differ from deer rules, including shot type and a federal duck stamp for many hunters. Also called duck hunting rules, goose hunting, migratory bird hunting, duck stamp, waterfowl regulations. Look for a federal duck stamp and state permits where required; non-toxic shot required for waterfowl."},{"symbol":"turkey_hunting_safety","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_hunting.json","kind":"survival","hash":"6fa1cf53","text":"Turkey hunting has its own risks because hunters wear camouflage and use calls, so another hunter can mistake a person for a bird. Also called turkey hunting, spring turkey safety, gobbler hunting, turkey call safety. Look for no red, white, or blue clothing, which can look like a gobbler's head; a tree trunk wider than your shoulders at your back."},{"symbol":"quartering_packing_out","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_hunting.json","kind":"survival","hash":"18b62b69","text":"Quartering means cutting a large animal into its four legs plus the backstraps, neck, and other meat so it can be carried out in loads and cooled faster. Also called quarter a deer, pack out meat, gutless method, boning out a deer. Look for clean game bags for each quarter; a sharp knife and a spare blade. Skin one side at a time, take the front and hind quarters, backstraps, and tenderloins, bag each piece, and hang or lay the bags in shade to cool before carrying."},{"symbol":"skinning_hanging_deer","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_hunting.json","kind":"survival","hash":"0fe94d7d","text":"Skinning removes the hide so meat can cool and stay clean; hanging lets the carcass cool and drain in cold, shaded, animal-proof conditions. Also called skin a deer, hang a deer, how long to hang venison, deer gambrel. Look for a stable gambrel or rope and a strong limb or beam; air at or below 40 °F (4 °C) for hanging. Hang by the hind legs or the head, skin from the top down with the blade edge out, and keep the carcass cold and covered from flies and animals."},{"symbol":"basic_butcher_cuts","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_hunting.json","kind":"survival","hash":"625810cf","text":"Home butchering divides a deer into a few simple groups: backstraps and tenderloins for steaks, hind-quarter roasts, shoulder and neck for stew, and trim for grinding. Also called butcher a deer, venison cuts, backstrap, tenderloin, deer processing at home. Look for silver skin and fat trimmed off; a clean, cold work surface. Work cold and clean, trim fat, silver skin, and bloodshot meat, wrap tightly, label, and freeze."},{"symbol":"tag_transport_checkin","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_hunting.json","kind":"survival","hash":"dffe9396","text":"Most states require you to tag an animal right away, keep the tag with it during transport, and report or check in the harvest within a set time. Also called tag a deer, check in deer, register harvest, transport deer legally. Look for the tag filled out and attached as your state requires; the state's online, phone, or station check-in. Read your state agency's current tagging and check-in page before the season and follow it exactly."},{"symbol":"cwd_processor_request","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_hunting.json","kind":"survival","hash":"e0249e70","text":"If you hunt where chronic wasting disease is found, ask a processor to process your animal by itself so its meat is not mixed with others. Also called deer processor CWD, individual processing, commercial processing venison. Look for the processor's policy on batch versus individual processing; test results before you eat the meat. Ask for individual processing, get the animal tested, and keep the meat frozen and unused until the result comes back."},{"symbol":"recover_game_water_cover","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_hunting.json","kind":"survival","hash":"e08dbcf8","text":"Getting an animal out of water, swamp, or thick cover comes after your own safety: cold water and deep mud can kill. Also called deer in the water, deer in a swamp, retrieve deer thick brush. Look for water depth, current, and temperature; ice that may not hold. Bring a partner and a rope, wait for light if needed, and reach the animal from shore or shallow water where you can."},{"symbol":"ground_blind_safety","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_hunting.json","kind":"survival","hash":"32089fe6","text":"A ground blind hides you at ground level, but it also hides you from other hunters, so safe setup matters. Also called ground blind, pop-up blind, hunting blind safety. Look for orange on the outside of the blind where required; a clear, safe shooting lane. Put orange on and around the blind as your state requires, know your shooting lanes and backstops, and keep the gun pointed out the window only when ready."},{"symbol":"youth_hunter_mentor","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_hunting.json","kind":"survival","hash":"f3f51c09","text":"Mentoring a new or young hunter means close supervision, short trips, and teaching safety before success. Also called take a kid hunting, mentor hunt, youth hunter, new hunter safety. Look for the new hunter within arm's reach when handling a gun; a completed hunter-education course where required. Keep sessions short and comfortable, review the firearm rules often, and check your state's youth and apprentice rules."},{"symbol":"bear_moose_camp_safety","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_hunting.json","kind":"survival","hash":"01133fe7","text":"Black bears are drawn to carcasses, gut piles, and camp food, and a moose can charge if crowded, especially a cow with a calf or a bull in the rut. Also called bear at my deer, bear near camp, moose charge, bear safety hunting. Look for fresh bear sign near a gut pile or meat pole; a moose with ears back and hackles raised. Hang meat away from sleeping areas, store food sealed, approach a kill carefully and make noise, and give moose plenty of room."},{"symbol":"field_dressing_birds","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_hunting.json","kind":"survival","hash":"7b9997f5","text":"Birds keep best when the crop and entrails are removed soon after the kill and the body is cooled. Also called clean a turkey, gut a pheasant, field dress grouse, dress a duck. Look for gloves on; crop and intestines removed. Remove the entrails and crop, wipe the cavity, and cool the bird quickly; pluck or skin at home."},{"symbol":"lead_ammo_awareness","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_hunting.json","kind":"survival","hash":"9ce6f6e9","text":"Lead bullets can break into small fragments in meat, and scavengers such as eagles can be poisoned by lead in gut piles; non-lead options exist. Also called lead bullet fragments, copper bullets, non-toxic shot, lead in venison. Look for fragments spread several inches from the wound channel; federal non-toxic shot rules for waterfowl. Trim generously around the wound channel, consider copper or other non-lead ammunition, and follow any rules where you hunt."},{"symbol":"hunting_ethics_waste","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_hunting.json","kind":"survival","hash":"b90ee122","text":"Ethical hunting means taking only shots you can make cleanly, using the animal well, and respecting landowners, other hunters, and the law. Also called hunting ethics, fair chase, wanton waste, respect landowners. Look for permission in hand on private land; a plan to recover and use the meat. Practice before the season, pass on doubtful shots, recover and use what you take, and leave gates and land as you found them."},{"symbol":"land_access_trespass","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_hunting.json","kind":"survival","hash":"21bcd413","text":"Hunting on private land needs the owner's permission, and posting and trespass rules differ from state to state; Yehsa has no property-owner map. Also called hunting private land, landowner permission, posted land, trespass hunting. Look for posted signs or paint marks on trees; a written or verbal permission you can show. Ask permission early, carry the owner's contact, learn your state's posting rules, and check official public-land maps."},{"symbol":"trapping_regulated","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_hunting.json","kind":"survival","hash":"52445396","text":"Trapping usually needs its own license or education course and has strict rules on trap types, placement, checking times, and seasons. Also called trapping license, how to trap, trap rules, snare rules. Look for the state's trapper-education requirement; trap check intervals. Take your state's trapper-education course and follow its current rules."},{"symbol":"game_camera_etiquette","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_hunting.json","kind":"survival","hash":"dd80d11d","text":"Trail cameras record whatever passes, including people, so place them where you have permission and think about privacy. Also called trail camera, game camera privacy, cellular trail cam. Look for the landowner's permission; trails used by hikers or neighbors. Get permission, aim cameras at game trails rather than paths people use, and check whether your state limits cellular cameras."},{"symbol":"wind_scent_hunting","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_hunting.json","kind":"survival","hash":"e3a100c9","text":"Deer rely on smell, and your scent drifts downwind in a widening cone, so hunters set up with the wind blowing from the deer toward them. Also called wind direction hunting, scent cone, deer smell me, play the wind. Look for wind direction from a light feather, milkweed fluff, or powder; thermals rising in the morning warmth and sinking in the evening."},{"symbol":"dragging_deer_safety","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_hunting.json","kind":"survival","hash":"f986b834","text":"Dragging a deer is hard work that strains the back and heart, especially in cold weather and for people who do not exercise. Also called drag a deer, heart attack dragging deer, deer sled, moving a deer out. Look for chest pain, shortness of breath, or dizziness; a long or uphill drag. Use a sled or cart, get help, take frequent breaks, and quarter a large animal rather than drag it far."},{"symbol":"hunting_alone_plan","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_hunting.json","kind":"survival","hash":"12e26832","text":"A solo hunter should leave a plan with someone: where you will park and sit, when you will be back, and when they should call for help. Also called solo hunting, hunting alone safety, tell someone where I hunt. Look for a marked map or pin of your stand; a check-in time. Leave the plan, carry a whistle, light, and first aid, and stick to your return time or update it."},{"symbol":"deer_scat","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_hunting.json","kind":"survival","hash":"4d1505fc","text":"Deer scat is a pile of small, dark, oval or acorn-shaped pellets, often 20 to 50 together, that shows where deer have fed or bedded. Also called deer droppings, deer poop, deer pellets, deer poo, deer dung. Look for pellets about 1/2 to 3/4 inch (1.5 to 2 cm) long, often with a small point or dimple at one end; dark and shiny when fresh, dull and dry or crumbly when old."},{"symbol":"deer_track","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_hunting.json","kind":"survival","hash":"e18c28de","text":"A deer track is a split, heart-shaped hoof print made by two pointed toes, about 2 to 3 1/2 inches (5 to 9 cm) long. Also called deer track, deer prints, deer hoof print, whitetail track, deer sign. Look for two teardrop toes forming a heart with the point toward travel; two dewclaw dots behind the print in mud, snow, or when running."},{"symbol":"deer_rub","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_hunting.json","kind":"survival","hash":"6b4cafd0","text":"A rub is a sapling or small tree where a buck has scraped off bark with his antlers, mostly in fall before and during the rut. Also called buck rub, antler rub, rubs on trees, scraped bark, deer sign. Look for bark stripped from one side of a small tree, often 1 to 4 inches (2.5 to 10 cm) thick, from knee to waist height; pale fresh wood, shredded bark curls on the ground, and broken twigs."},{"symbol":"deer_scrape","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_hunting.json","kind":"survival","hash":"1090205d","text":"A scrape is a patch of bare earth that a buck paws clear under an overhanging branch and marks with scent, mostly in the weeks before the rut. Also called buck scrape, deer scrape, scrape under a branch, licking branch, deer sign. Look for a pawed oval of bare dirt, often 1 to 3 feet (30 to 90 cm) across; a chewed or broken licking branch about head height above it."},{"symbol":"deer_sign_reading","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_hunting.json","kind":"survival","hash":"0ee314bb","text":"Deer sign is the tracks, scat, rubs, scrapes, beds, and browsed plants that deer leave, and reading it shows where they travel, feed, and rest. Also called deer sign, signs of deer, how to scout for deer, deer scouting, where are the deer. Look for trails and tracks between thick cover and food; scat piles, oval beds pressed in leaves or snow."},{"symbol":"map_orient_compass","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_navigation.json","kind":"survival","hash":"7ecd69c1","text":"Orienting a map means turning it so north on the map points to north on the ground, after allowing for declination, so the map matches what you see. Also called set the map, orient the map, line up map and compass, turn the map to north, map and compass basics. Look for the map's north arrow or meridian lines; the compass needle settled and away from metal."},{"symbol":"declination_basics","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_navigation.json","kind":"survival","hash":"6edee8ec","text":"Declination is the angle between where a compass needle points (magnetic north) and true north on the map; it differs by place and slowly changes over the years. Also called compass doesn't match the map, compass and map disagree, magnetic variation, declination angle, adjust for declination. Look for the declination diagram and year printed on the map; a compass with an adjustable declination setting."},{"symbol":"north_types","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_navigation.json","kind":"survival","hash":"30e2ae53","text":"True north points to the North Pole, magnetic north is where a compass needle points, and grid north follows the vertical grid lines printed on a map; the small angles between them matter over distance. Also called grid north, true north vs magnetic north, three norths, which north. Look for the three-arrow diagram in a topographic map's margin; grid lines (UTM) on the map."},{"symbol":"bearing_take_follow","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_navigation.json","kind":"survival","hash":"3215edd2","text":"A bearing is the direction to a target measured in degrees clockwise from north; you take it from the map or a landmark, then walk it by sighting from object to object. Also called follow a bearing, take a bearing, walk a compass heading, shoot a bearing, azimuth. Look for the direction-of-travel arrow on the compass; the needle boxed in the orienting arrow."},{"symbol":"contour_terrain_features","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_navigation.json","kind":"survival","hash":"1f0bee7e","text":"Contour lines join points of equal elevation, so their shape and spacing show the land: close lines are steep, wide lines are gentle, and Vs pointing uphill mark streams and draws. Also called contour lines, topo map lines, reading a topographic map, saddle ridge valley, squiggly lines on a map. Look for contour interval printed in the margin; closed rings for hilltops, with tick marks for depressions."},{"symbol":"map_distance_pace","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_navigation.json","kind":"survival","hash":"13e61fd8","text":"Distance on a map is measured against its scale bar, and on the ground you track it with a pace count: the number of your double steps in 100 meters on your normal stride. Also called pace count, how far have I walked, measure distance on a map, pacing. Look for the scale bar in the map margin; your own tested pace count. Measure your pace count on a known 100 m course, count every second step while walking, and tie a knot or move a bead at each 100 m."},{"symbol":"gps_vs_map","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_navigation.json","kind":"survival","hash":"5b948410","text":"A GPS or phone gives a fast position but needs battery and sky view; a paper map and baseplate compass keep working when the screen dies or the signal drops. Also called GPS not working, phone died navigation, do I need a compass if I have a phone, GPS in canyon. Look for battery below half on a long day; deep canyons, heavy cover, or cliffs blocking the fix."},{"symbol":"lost_stop_plan","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_navigation.json","kind":"survival","hash":"763f45fe","text":"When you realize you are lost, the first job is to stop moving, calm down, and work from your last known point; staying put is usually safest if you cannot work it out. Also called I'm lost, what to do if lost, turned around in the woods, don't know where I am, STOP lost. Look for the last place you were sure of; streams, ridges, or roads you crossed."},{"symbol":"aiming_off_handrails","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_navigation.json","kind":"survival","hash":"96705e1d","text":"Terrain association uses long features to guide you: a handrail is something you walk alongside, a catching feature stops you if you overshoot, and aiming off means deliberately aiming to one side of a target on a line feature. Also called handrail navigation, catching feature, aiming off, terrain association, follow a ridgeline. Look for a stream, ridge, trail, or field edge running your way; a road, river, or lake beyond your target."},{"symbol":"phone_compass_unreliable","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_navigation.json","kind":"survival","hash":"9e0ca7bc","text":"A phone's compass is a magnetic sensor that metal, magnets in cases, vehicles, and a skipped calibration can throw off by many degrees. Also called phone compass wrong, calibrate phone compass, compass app not accurate. Look for a magnetic phone case or mount; readings that swing when you move near a truck or rifle. Calibrate by moving the phone in a figure-eight away from metal, remove magnetic cases, and cross-check against a baseplate compass or the sun."},{"symbol":"stream_crossing_choice","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_navigation.json","kind":"survival","hash":"bc2c37e4","text":"A stream is safe to cross only when it is shallow, slow, and has a clear landing; knee-deep fast water can knock an adult down. Also called crossing a river, ford a stream, safe to cross a creek, fast water crossing. Look for water above the knee or moving faster than a walk; muddy, rising water after rain. Scout for a wide, shallow, braided spot, unbuckle the pack's hip belt, face upstream with a stick or partner, and shuffle your feet."},{"symbol":"compass_parts","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_navigation.json","kind":"survival","hash":"66d04529","text":"A baseplate compass has a clear base with a direction-of-travel arrow, a rotating housing marked in degrees with an orienting arrow and lines, and a magnetic needle whose red end points to magnetic north. Also called baseplate compass, orienting arrow, compass housing, direction of travel arrow. Look for the red end of the needle; the degree ring on the housing."},{"symbol":"night_navigation","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_navigation.json","kind":"survival","hash":"b00c2bc7","text":"Moving at night is slower and riskier; it works only on a known trail or a sure handrail with a good light and a careful bearing. Also called walking in the dark, night hike navigation, lost at night. Look for a bright headlamp and spare batteries; a trail, road, or stream edge to follow. Shorten legs between landmarks, count paces, check the compass often, and stop and camp if the ground gets rough."},{"symbol":"sun_direction_rough","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_navigation.json","kind":"survival","hash":"79637fba","text":"The sun gives only a rough direction: it rises in the east, sets in the west, and in the northern hemisphere is generally to the south at midday, with big seasonal shifts. Also called sun direction, find north without compass, shadow stick, which way is east. Look for a stick's shadow tip moving from west to east over 15-20 minutes; time of day and season."},{"symbol":"stars_north","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_navigation.json","kind":"survival","hash":"2b36891a","text":"In the northern hemisphere the North Star, Polaris, sits almost due north; the two outer stars of the Big Dipper's cup point to it. Also called north star, Polaris, big dipper north, find north at night. Look for the Big Dipper's pointer stars; a moderately bright star alone in its area. Follow the pointer stars about five times their gap to Polaris, then pick a ground landmark below it to hold your direction."},{"symbol":"mark_route_self","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_navigation.json","kind":"survival","hash":"b26afabc","text":"Marking your own route with removable flagging or noted landmarks makes the return trip easier, especially in thick woods or snow. Also called mark my trail, flagging tape, find my way back, blaze a trail. Look for bright, removable flagging tape; waypoints saved only if you chose to. Tie flagging at eye level where you can see the next one, look back often so you know the return view, and remove the flagging on your way out."},{"symbol":"whiteout_fog_navigation","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_navigation.json","kind":"survival","hash":"c84cffc5","text":"In fog or a whiteout you lose landmarks and depth; most often the safest choice is to stop, shelter, and wait unless a sure handrail leads you out. Also called lost in fog, whiteout, can't see in snowstorm, fog navigation. Look for no visible landmarks beyond a few yards; snow or fog hiding drop-offs. Stop, get warm and sheltered, and wait it out, or follow a known handrail with compass and pace count, staying together."},{"symbol":"altimeter_navigation","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_navigation.json","kind":"survival","hash":"896b03f1","text":"An altimeter estimates your height from air pressure, and matching that height to a contour on the map helps narrow down where you are along a trail or slope. Also called altimeter navigation, barometer altitude, elevation to find location. Look for a known elevation to calibrate at the trailhead; weather changes shifting the reading. Calibrate at a known height, recheck at known points, and combine the reading with the trail, stream, or ridge you are on."},{"symbol":"map_scale_limits","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_navigation.json","kind":"survival","hash":"2fe73652","text":"A map's scale sets how much detail it can show; a 1:24,000 topographic map shows small streams and contours, while a small-scale map leaves out trails, cliffs, and new roads. Also called map scale, 1:24000, zoom limits, what a map pack shows. Look for the scale and date in the margin; missing new trails, logging roads, or beaver flooding. Use the largest-scale map you can get for foot travel and expect changes on the ground since the map's date."},{"symbol":"vehicle_vs_foot_travel","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_navigation.json","kind":"survival","hash":"6832846e","text":"On unmapped back roads the vehicle is shelter, a big visible target, and a supply cache, so staying with it is often safer than walking out. Also called car stuck on back road, leave the car or walk, unmarked forest road. Look for fuel, weather, and daylight left; distance to the last known road or house. Stay with the vehicle when stuck unless help is close and the way is sure; walking out is safer by daylight on a known road."},{"symbol":"resection_intersection","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_navigation.json","kind":"survival","hash":"f8c5e14e","text":"Resection finds your own position by taking bearings to two or three landmarks you can identify on the map and drawing back-bearings; intersection finds a distant point from two known spots. Also called triangulation, resection, find my position with compass, intersection navigation. Look for two or three distinct, mapped landmarks far apart in direction; back-bearings: add or subtract 180°."},{"symbol":"grid_coordinates","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_navigation.json","kind":"survival","hash":"888594c0","text":"Grid coordinates locate a point using the map's grid lines: read the easting (left to right) first, then the northing (bottom to top). Also called UTM coordinates, grid reference, read right then up, give my location coordinates. Look for the grid line labels along the map edges; a coordinate scale or ruler. Read right, then up; give the coordinates and the datum to rescuers if asked, or read latitude and longitude from your phone."},{"symbol":"turnaround_time","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_navigation.json","kind":"survival","hash":"7cd39566","text":"A turnaround time is the hour you turn back no matter where you are, set so you are out before dark or bad weather; a trip plan tells someone your route and return. Also called turnaround time, trip plan, leave a plan, how late to hike. Look for sunset time and forecast; your pace on the way in. Set the turnaround time before you start, keep it, and leave your route and return time with someone who will call for help."},{"symbol":"shelter_site_hazards","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_shelter.json","kind":"survival","hash":"beb3fcc5","text":"Choose a shelter site away from hazards overhead and underfoot: dead branches, rockfall, flood channels, game trails, and low cold pockets. Also called widowmaker, dead tree over camp, flood channel, rockfall, bad campsite. Look for dead limbs or leaning trees overhead (widowmakers); dry washes, gullies, and stream banks that can flood."},{"symbol":"debris_hut_build","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_shelter.json","kind":"survival","hash":"e8957f13","text":"A debris hut is a small, wedge-shaped shelter of a ridgepole, ribs, and a thick layer of dry leaves or other debris, built to trap body heat. Also called leaf hut, insulated stick hut, hut made of leaves and sticks, debris shelter. Look for a long ridgepole on a stump, rock, or tripod; ribs steep enough to shed water."},{"symbol":"lean_to_build","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_shelter.json","kind":"survival","hash":"adbff6aa","text":"A lean-to is a sloped roof with an open side, built against a crossbar between two trees or posts, often with a fire reflector wall opposite it. Also called lean to shelter, three-sided shelter, lean-to fire reflector, a-frame lean-to. Look for a crossbar between two supports at about chest height; a roof sloping toward the wind with the open side away from it."},{"symbol":"tarp_shelter_rig","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_shelter.json","kind":"survival","hash":"8bc4bef4","text":"A tarp shelter is a sheet of waterproof fabric pitched over a ridgeline and staked down; it is the fastest shelter to set up. Also called tarp A-frame, poncho tent, how to set up a tarp, tarp lean-to. Look for a taut ridgeline between two trees at waist height; tarp draped over it with edges staked low."},{"symbol":"ground_first_insulation","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_shelter.json","kind":"survival","hash":"dfcabe69","text":"The ground pulls heat out of you faster than the air does, so insulation under you matters before the roof. Also called bed of boughs, sleeping pad, insulate from the ground, warm bed. Look for a 6 to 8 inch (15 to 20 cm) pressed layer of dry boughs, leaves, or grass; a pad or pack under your torso and hips. Build the bed first, then the shelter. Pile dry leaves, pine boughs (cut ends down), or grass, add a pad or pack, and refresh each night."},{"symbol":"shelter_door","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_shelter.json","kind":"survival","hash":"f8414ef1","text":"A shelter door is anything that closes the entrance against wind and cold, such as a pack, a debris pile, or a tarp. Also called close the entrance, block the opening, door for a debris hut. Look for a gap just big enough to crawl in; a door that can be closed from the inside. Use your pack, a bundle of branches stuffed with leaves, a spare tarp, or a slab of snow. Close it from the inside after you crawl in, and leave a small gap for air."},{"symbol":"wind_vs_insulation","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_shelter.json","kind":"survival","hash":"141f6353","text":"A windbreak stops moving air from stealing heat, while insulation traps still air around you; a good shelter does both. Also called windbreak, what keeps me warm, wind vs cold shelter. Look for windbreak: a wall, tarp, or log between you and the wind; insulation: dry layers, boughs, and a bed. Block the wind first, then add insulation under and around you. Keep it dry and make the space small to hold heat."},{"symbol":"clothing_layers","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_shelter.json","kind":"survival","hash":"18d8bb5b","text":"Layering uses a wicking base, a warm insulating middle, and a windproof, waterproof outer layer that you adjust to stay dry and warm. Also called what to wear, dress in layers, wool or cotton, wet clothes, how to layer. Look for wool or synthetic base layers, not cotton; an insulating mid-layer like fleece or down. Vent before you sweat, add layers when you stop, and swap wet layers for dry ones as soon as you can. Keep a spare dry layer in a waterproof bag."},{"symbol":"fuel_in_closed_shelter","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_shelter.json","kind":"survival","hash":"1ccc1e93","text":"Burning charcoal, a stove, a lantern, or even a candle in a closed shelter can fill it with carbon monoxide, which has no smell and can kill in your sleep. Also called stove in a tent, charcoal in a shelter, candle in a snow cave, carbon monoxide in a shelter. Look for a closed tent, snow cave, car, or hut; headache and drowsiness."},{"symbol":"stuck_in_car_snow","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_shelter.json","kind":"survival","hash":"74ab8469","text":"If stranded in a car in a snowstorm, staying with the vehicle is usually safest; keep the tailpipe clear and run the engine only a little. Also called stranded in snow, stay with the car, car in a snowbank, exhaust pipe blocked. Look for blowing or falling snow with low visibility; the exhaust pipe at risk of blocking."},{"symbol":"snow_shelter_ventilation","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_shelter.json","kind":"survival","hash":"97a86b51","text":"A snow shelter uses snow's insulating quality, and it needs a vent hole and a roof about a foot thick to be safe. Also called tree well shelter, snow trench, snow cave vent, packed snow hut. Look for a tree well with low branches overhead; a trench or cave with a roof at least 12 inches (30 cm) thick. Dig a pit under an evergreen, roof it with branches and snow, line the floor with boughs, and cut a vent. Keep a tool inside and keep the entrance lower than the sleeping area."},{"symbol":"space_blanket_bivy","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_shelter.json","kind":"survival","hash":"87a261c6","text":"A space blanket is a thin reflective sheet that holds in some body heat; a bivy is a waterproof shell for a sleeping bag. Also called emergency blanket, mylar blanket, bivy sack, survival blanket. Look for a very light sheet that reflects heat back; a bivy keeps wind and rain off a bag. Wrap a space blanket around you under clothes or in a shelter, with a pad or dry layers under you. Use it as a roof or wind block too. Add insulation; it does not warm by itself."},{"symbol":"tent_storm_pitch","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_shelter.json","kind":"survival","hash":"d17ec191","text":"For a storm pitch, set the tent low and tight with the narrow end into the wind, and guy out every line. Also called set up a tent in the wind, tent in a storm, stake out a tent. Look for a flat, drained site away from dead trees; the door facing away from the wind. Choose a protected spot, stake every corner and guy line, and stow the pack inside. Put rocks or logs on the stakes in soft ground. Avoid ridges and open meadows."},{"symbol":"fire_near_shelter","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_shelter.json","kind":"survival","hash":"529f6e23","text":"Place a fire off the opening of a shelter and far enough that sparks cannot reach the roof or bedding. Also called how close should a fire be, fire next to tent, sparks on a tent. Look for a fire 6 to 10 feet (2 to 3 m) from a tarp or tent; a reflector wall behind the fire. Keep the fire small and downwind of the shelter, away from the door and dry fabric. Use a ring of rocks or a pit. Put it out before you sleep."},{"symbol":"shelter_for_conditions","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_shelter.json","kind":"survival","hash":"34692a3a","text":"Different weather needs a different shelter: a steep tarp for rain, a low wedge for wind, a snow shelter for deep snow, and netting for insects. Also called bug shelter, rain shelter, mosquito shelter. Look for rain: steep pitch and a drip line; wind: low and streamlined, back to the wind. Pick the shelter that fits what the weather is doing, and use netting or a head net when insects are bad."},{"symbol":"group_vs_solo_shelter","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_shelter.json","kind":"survival","hash":"33f2bbf8","text":"A group shelter shares body heat and takes less material per person; a solo shelter must be smaller and better insulated. Also called share body heat, sleeping together, shelter for two. Look for two people sharing a bag or insulation hold heat better; a smaller space is warmer. Share a larger shelter where possible, huddle with insulation under all, and take turns keeping the vent and fire."},{"symbol":"insulation_how_much","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_shelter.json","kind":"survival","hash":"cb08ca06","text":"In plain terms, thicker is warmer: about 3 feet of dry debris on a hut and 6 to 8 inches under you, with a small vent. Also called thickness of debris, how thick should the leaves be. Look for 3 feet (1 m) of debris on a hut; 6 to 8 inches (15 to 20 cm) under you. Add more until you can no longer feel the cold coming through, and keep it dry. Leave a vent."},{"symbol":"bear_scat","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_sign.json","kind":"survival","hash":"af8f513f","text":"Black bear scat is a large tubular or piled dropping, often full of berries, seeds, nut shells, grass, or hair, that means a bear has been in the area. Also called bear droppings, bear poop, bear dung, black bear scat, bear sign. Look for thick tubes about 1.5 to 2 inches (4 to 5 cm) wide, or loose piles; packed with berries, seeds, or grass in summer and fall."},{"symbol":"coyote_scat","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_sign.json","kind":"survival","hash":"57f3af07","text":"Coyote and fox scat is a twisted, rope-like dropping full of hair and small bones, with a tapered end, often left in the middle of a trail. Also called coyote droppings, fox scat, fox poop, coyote poop, canine scat. Look for coyote scat about 3/4 to 1 inch (2 to 2.5 cm) wide; fox scat thinner; packed with fur, bone, berries, or seeds. Scat on a trail or at a junction is a territory marker. It tells you which predators use the area."},{"symbol":"moose_scat","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_sign.json","kind":"survival","hash":"f9c24d00","text":"Moose scat is a pile of large oval pellets of compressed wood fiber in winter, or soft clumps like a cow pie in summer. Also called moose droppings, moose poop, moose nuggets, moose pellets. Look for pellets about 1 to 1 1/2 inches (2.5 to 4 cm) long, much bigger than deer; sawdust-like texture when broken. Fresh piles mean moose are using the area; give any moose plenty of room."},{"symbol":"raccoon_scat","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_sign.json","kind":"survival","hash":"cb6f4bd2","text":"Raccoon scat is a blunt-ended, tubular dropping often full of seeds and shell bits, left in piles called latrines on logs, rocks, roofs, or at tree bases. Also called raccoon droppings, raccoon poop, raccoon latrine, coon scat. Look for dark tubes about 3/4 inch (2 cm) wide with blunt ends; many droppings of different ages in one spot. A latrine means raccoons use the site regularly. Keep food and trash locked away."},{"symbol":"rabbit_scat","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_sign.json","kind":"survival","hash":"59f7cc72","text":"Rabbit and hare scat is a scatter of round, slightly flattened, pea-sized pellets of chewed plant fiber. Also called rabbit droppings, rabbit poop, rabbit pellets, hare droppings, bunny poop. Look for round, about 1/4 to 1/2 inch (0.6 to 1.3 cm) across, flattened like a thick coin; light brown and fibrous inside. Lots of pellets near brush and browsed twigs mean rabbits or hares feed and hide there."},{"symbol":"bear_track","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_sign.json","kind":"survival","hash":"f5a4c7d6","text":"A black bear track is a wide print with five toes in an arc and a broad pad, the hind print looking like a flat-footed human foot. Also called bear track, bear prints, bear paw print, black bear track, bear sign. Look for front print about 4 to 6 inches (10 to 15 cm) wide with claw dots; hind print longer, up to about 7 inches (18 cm), with a heel. Fresh tracks mean a bear is close. Make noise, keep food sealed, and keep dogs leashed."},{"symbol":"canine_track","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_sign.json","kind":"survival","hash":"f3e91316","text":"A canine track has four toes with claw marks around a triangular pad, and an X shape can be drawn through the space between toes and pad. Also called coyote track, fox track, dog track, canine prints, wolf track. Look for oval print; coyote about 2 1/2 inches (6 cm) long, fox smaller; wild canines walk in a straight, direct line; dogs wander. A straight, purposeful line of oval prints is usually a coyote or fox; a zigzagging line beside a person's tracks is usually a dog."},{"symbol":"raccoon_track","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_sign.json","kind":"survival","hash":"9d7393fa","text":"A raccoon track has five long, finger-like toes, like a small human handprint, usually in mud near water. Also called raccoon track, raccoon prints, coon track. Look for front print about 2 to 3 inches (5 to 8 cm) with separated fingers; hind print longer, like a small foot. Raccoon tracks near camp mean you should lock up food at night."},{"symbol":"moose_track","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_sign.json","kind":"survival","hash":"8b8d3d9e","text":"A moose track is a large split-hoof print over 4 inches (10 cm) long, shaped like a deer track but much bigger. Also called moose track, moose prints, moose hoof print. Look for blunt or pointed heart shape, about 4 to 6 inches (10 to 15 cm) long; deep in mud or snow, with dewclaws often showing. Fresh tracks mean moose are near; give them room, especially in fall and in calving season."},{"symbol":"turkey_track","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_sign.json","kind":"survival","hash":"e366a4db","text":"A wild turkey track is a large bird print with three long forward toes and a short back toe, about 4 to 5 inches (10 to 13 cm) long. Also called turkey track, turkey prints, bird tracks. Look for middle toe longest and straight ahead; long stride of many prints in a line. Fresh tracks near fields, roosts, or dusting spots mean turkeys use the area."},{"symbol":"bobcat_track","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_sign.json","kind":"survival","hash":"e5b4243e","text":"A bobcat track is a round print about 2 inches (5 cm) across with four toes, no claw marks, and a three-lobed pad. Also called bobcat track, cat track, lynx track, bobcat prints. Look for round, not oval like a canine; leading edge of the pad has two notches making three lobes. A round print with no claws is a cat; bobcats often walk with hind feet placed in the front prints."},{"symbol":"rabbit_track","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_sign.json","kind":"survival","hash":"f2a39232","text":"Rabbit and hare tracks come in groups of four: two long hind feet side by side in front of two small front feet, one behind the other. Also called rabbit track, hare track, bunny prints, snowshoe hare track. Look for Y-shaped or triangle-shaped track group; snowshoe hare hind prints very large and splayed in snow. The big hind prints land ahead of the front prints, so the wide end of the group points the way the animal went."},{"symbol":"water_how_much","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_water.json","kind":"survival","hash":"6a69fc27","text":"Plan on at least one gallon (about 4 liters) of drinking water per person per day for emergencies, and more in heat, with illness, or for children and nursing mothers. Also called how much water do I need, gallon per day, dehydration signs, water per person. Look for pale yellow urine means you are drinking enough; dark urine, dry mouth, headache, and dizziness mean you need more."},{"symbol":"water_safe_to_drink","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_water.json","kind":"survival","hash":"bd8bda39","text":"Clear water from a stream, lake, or spring can still carry germs, so treat it unless you know it is safe; clear does not mean clean. Also called can I drink from the stream, is creek water safe, clear water safe, wild water. Look for clear but untreated water can hold Giardia, Cryptosporidium, bacteria, and viruses; fuel, chemical, or flood water is not made safe by boiling."},{"symbol":"boil_water_method","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_water.json","kind":"survival","hash":"433ac8eb","text":"Boiling is the most reliable way to kill germs: bring water to a rolling boil for one minute, or three minutes above 6,500 feet (2,000 m). Also called boil water to make it safe, rolling boil, how long to boil, boil at altitude. Look for large bubbles breaking the surface; settle or strain cloudy water first."},{"symbol":"water_bleach_disinfect","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_water.json","kind":"survival","hash":"a7237bc5","text":"Plain unscented household bleach can disinfect clear water, but it works less well than boiling against Giardia and Cryptosporidium. Also called bleach to purify water, drops of bleach per gallon, chlorine bleach water. Look for 8 drops of 6% bleach per gallon (6 drops of 8.25%), double for cloudy water; wait 30 minutes."},{"symbol":"water_filter_use","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_water.json","kind":"survival","hash":"bde1c7da","text":"A portable filter strains out parasites and bacteria; most do not remove viruses or chemicals. Also called water filter, straw filter, pump filter, how fine a filter, backpacking filter. Look for pore size about 1 micron or less stops Giardia and Cryptosporidium; 0.3 micron or less also stops most bacteria."},{"symbol":"water_filter_then_disinfect","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_water.json","kind":"survival","hash":"958883aa","text":"Filtering removes parasites and bacteria and clears the water, and a disinfectant afterward handles viruses that pass through. Also called filter and treat, layer treatment, virus protection water. Look for filter or settle first; then boil, or add bleach or a chlorine dioxide tablet. When you are in doubt or in a place with viruses of concern, filter first and then disinfect the filtered water. This also saves disinfectant and gives it more reliable contact."},{"symbol":"water_cloudy_pretreat","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_water.json","kind":"survival","hash":"e5a264f0","text":"Cloudy water hides germs from disinfectant and clogs filters, so let it settle and strain it first. Also called muddy water, silty water, turbid water, clear up cloudy water. Look for silt or particles in the water; a clean cloth or coffee filter. Let the water sit until the silt sinks, pour the clear top through a clean cloth or coffee filter, then boil, filter, or disinfect."},{"symbol":"water_contaminated_no","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_water.json","kind":"survival","hash":"178cd686","text":"Water contaminated with fuel, chemicals, sewage, or flood debris cannot be made safe by boiling or bleach, so do not use it to drink. Also called fuel in the water, flood water, chemical spill water, dead animal in stream. Look for an oily sheen or fuel smell; flood water with sewage or farm runoff."},{"symbol":"water_container_storage","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_water.json","kind":"survival","hash":"4866f6ca","text":"Store treated water in clean, food-grade containers with tight lids, away from sun and heat. Also called what to use for a water bottle, store clean water, water jug, hydration bladder. Look for food-grade plastic or stainless steel; a lid that seals. Wash containers with soap and rinse, fill with treated water, label them with the date, and replace commercial water as the package says. In the field, use a pot, a bottle, or a clean bag."},{"symbol":"water_find_new_england","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_water.json","kind":"survival","hash":"41fc2458","text":"In New England, water is usually near: look for streams in valley bottoms, springs, ponds, and melted snow; moving water is better than still. Also called where to find water in the woods, stream or spring, snow for water, water in Maine. Look for valley bottoms and seeps where several slopes meet; springs and clear running streams."},{"symbol":"snow_melt_water","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_water.json","kind":"survival","hash":"aafe006e","text":"Melt snow or ice in a pot and treat the water; eating snow lowers body temperature and uses energy. Also called melt snow, eat snow, drink from snow. Look for a pot with a little water in it to start; clean white snow, not yellow or near a road. Start with a little water in the pot so it doesn't scorch, add snow gradually, and boil it. Melt it with a stove or fire, or in a bag against the body only if needed."},{"symbol":"rain_collect_water","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_water.json","kind":"survival","hash":"0b0808ad","text":"Rain is a good source if you collect it in clean containers, and you should treat it if it passed over roofs or leaves. Also called catch rain water, tarp water catchment, rain barrel. Look for a tarp funneling water into a clean pot; rain caught directly is cleaner. Set clean containers out, rig a tarp to funnel rain into them, and let the first few minutes of rain wash dust off. Treat the water if it is for drinking."},{"symbol":"giardia_crypto","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_water.json","kind":"survival","hash":"ba87acd6","text":"Giardia and Cryptosporidium are tiny parasites in surface water that cause days of diarrhea, cramps, and nausea; boiling or the right filter stops them. Also called beaver fever, parasite in water, cryptosporidium, giardia. Look for watery diarrhea, gas, cramps, and nausea one to two weeks after drinking; found in streams, lakes, and ponds, even clear ones. Boil water or use a filter rated for 1 micron or smaller. Wash your hands with soap and clean water before eating."},{"symbol":"water_chemical_tablets","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_water.json","kind":"survival","hash":"20e1ece2","text":"Tablets disinfect water when boiling is not possible; follow the label for dose and wait time. Also called water purification tablets, iodine tablets, micropur, aquamira drops. Look for chlorine dioxide tablets kill most germs including Cryptosporidium after the label wait time; iodine tablets are less reliable against Cryptosporidium. Read the label, pre-filter cloudy water, add the tablet, and wait the full time (often 30 minutes to 4 hours for Cryptosporidium)."},{"symbol":"water_uv_solar","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_water.json","kind":"survival","hash":"05183218","text":"UV treatment works only on clear water in a working unit, and solar disinfection needs clear plastic bottles in strong sun for many hours. Also called steripen, UV light water, sodis, solar water disinfection. Look for clear, not cloudy, water; a charged, working UV unit. Pre-filter, treat with the UV unit as directed, and carry a backup like tablets or a pot. Use solar disinfection only as a last resort."},{"symbol":"water_hygiene_scarce","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_water.json","kind":"survival","hash":"77b90460","text":"When water is short, use the cleanest water for drinking and wound care, and a little soap and treated water for hands. Also called wash hands without water, hand sanitizer, dishes in the field, hygiene when water is short. Look for a small amount of soap and water for hands; alcohol-based sanitizer when hands are not greasy."},{"symbol":"latrine_placement","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_water.json","kind":"survival","hash":"f4faa3ee","text":"Dig a latrine or cathole at least 200 feet (60 m) from water, trails, and camp to keep your drinking water clean. Also called where to go to the bathroom outdoors, cathole, toilet in the woods, poop in the woods. Look for a hole 6 to 8 inches (15 to 20 cm) deep; 200 feet from any stream, pond, or spring. Dig a small hole, use it, cover it with soil, and wash your hands. Pack out toilet paper if the rules require it."},{"symbol":"formula_unsafe_water","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_water.json","kind":"survival","hash":"cfdcff40","text":"Mix infant formula with bottled, boiled-and-cooled, or properly treated water when the tap is unsafe. Also called infant formula and boil water, safe water for baby, mix formula. Look for a boil-water notice or a flood; bottled water for babies. Use bottled or boiled water (cooled to room temperature before mixing, per the label), wash bottles with safe water, and use ready-to-feed formula if you can."},{"symbol":"water_no_go_sources","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_water.json","kind":"survival","hash":"b20ae058","text":"Never drink radiator or boiler water, seawater, urine, or water that has fuel or chemicals in it. Also called radiator water, urine, seawater, toilet tank. Look for antifreeze in radiators; salt in seawater and urine. Use another source; if you must use pool water, treat and filter it, and use a toilet tank only if it is clean, not a bowl."},{"symbol":"well_flood_disinfect","source":"symbolic_books/survival/entries_water.json","kind":"survival","hash":"5834e355","text":"A flooded well must be tested and disinfected following health-department or EPA directions; do not invent a mix. Also called flooded well, shock chlorinate a well, well water after flood. Look for flood water over the wellhead; cloudy or odd-tasting water. Use bottled or boiled water until the well is tested and cleared. Contact your health department or a certified lab, and follow their disinfection steps."},{"symbol":"food_1","source":"symbolic_books/survival/food.txt","kind":"survival","hash":"8d8e7c8b","text":"Food: eat known cultivated food or packaged rations before foraging. Do not taste unknown plants, mushrooms, or unfamiliar meat. Herbology notes in this repo are cautions, not a menu. Hunger is not a reason to experiment."},{"symbol":"food_2","source":"symbolic_books/survival/food.txt","kind":"survival","hash":"5dc024bd","text":"Pack food you already eat, with the first night's portion on top of the bag. Eat small amounts through the day. A pot and a spoon you have used at home beat a new wild food. This sets no calorie target and no meal plan for a clinic."},{"symbol":"food_3","source":"symbolic_books/survival/food.txt","kind":"survival","hash":"31017bbe","text":"Cook known food until it is hot through, and keep raw scraps away from the cooked pot. Seal or hang leftovers so animals are not invited to where you sleep. A bulging can or a sour smell means you discard it. Do not taste food to decide if it has turned."},{"symbol":"food_4","source":"symbolic_books/survival/food.txt","kind":"survival","hash":"463c9d91","text":"Berries, fish, and greens are a local-rules question, and only for kinds you already know from a source outside this note. If you are not sure, it does not go in your mouth. Mushrooms are not a trial. Cultivated food is the plan. Wild food is not a backup menu and not a dose guide."},{"symbol":"navigation_1","source":"symbolic_books/survival/navigation.txt","kind":"survival","hash":"010204d8","text":"Navigation: the sun rises generally east and sets generally west. A map and compass beat guesswork. If you are lost, staying put and making yourself visible is often safer than wandering. This is not a complete orienteering manual."},{"symbol":"navigation_2","source":"symbolic_books/survival/navigation.txt","kind":"survival","hash":"4297eeb6","text":"Walk to a landmark you can name, a boulder, a saddle, or a distinct tree, then pick the next. Count paces on a stretch you already measured so distance is a number. Stop while the last sure point is still clear. Turning around is a plan, not a failure."},{"symbol":"navigation_3","source":"symbolic_books/survival/navigation.txt","kind":"survival","hash":"59ee71b5","text":"A shoreline, a ridge, or a stream can be a handrail you keep beside you. Know which side you are on, and which bend means you have gone too far. A handrail is a landscape line. It is not a street. If it splits and you cannot say which branch, you stop."},{"symbol":"navigation_4","source":"symbolic_books/survival/navigation.txt","kind":"survival","hash":"8666db80","text":"If you are unsure, sit and sketch a circle of possible places instead of a hopeful dot. Signal from an open spot. Move only toward a feature you can name, in daylight, and camp if dusk is close. These notes do not replace a map you can read, and they do not invent a trail."},{"symbol":"shelter_fire_1","source":"symbolic_books/survival/shelter_fire.txt","kind":"survival","hash":"8e947398","text":"Shelter and fire: get out of wind and wet ground first. A small contained fire needs clear fuel, a safe distance from brush, and a way to put it out. Do not light a fire where local rules forbid it. This is a planning note, not a wilderness course."},{"symbol":"shelter_fire_2","source":"symbolic_books/survival/shelter_fire.txt","kind":"survival","hash":"1697d19b","text":"Pick the site before the flame. Choose ground that sheds rain, above a flood line, and clear of dead trees that can drop limbs. Face the opening away from the wind. A finger-width of dry insulation under you holds more heat than a tall roof. This is planning, not a building code."},{"symbol":"shelter_fire_3","source":"symbolic_books/survival/shelter_fire.txt","kind":"survival","hash":"33ab8170","text":"Build a fire only where rules allow it, on scraped mineral soil, with fuel upwind and a way to drown it. Start with tinder and pencil twigs, and add thicker wood after the flame is honest. Feel the ashes cold before you leave. A banned day or a hard wind means no flame."},{"symbol":"shelter_fire_4","source":"symbolic_books/survival/shelter_fire.txt","kind":"survival","hash":"e99c2b10","text":"Without a fire, stay dry, covered, and off the ground, and keep the wind off your back. Any flame inside a closed shelter can foul the air, so leave a vent and do not sleep against a heater. Wet clothes next to the skin steal heat even in mild air. This does not treat a cold injury."},{"symbol":"signaling_1","source":"symbolic_books/survival/signaling.txt","kind":"survival","hash":"7db7b4fe","text":"Signaling: three of anything (whistle blasts, bright flashes, fires) is a common distress pattern. A mirror or light aimed at rescuers is a signal, not a weapon. Tell another person where you are going before you travel."},{"symbol":"signaling_2","source":"symbolic_books/survival/signaling.txt","kind":"survival","hash":"83b55e72","text":"A whistle carries farther than a shout and spends less breath. Three short blasts, a pause, then three again is the pattern to repeat. If you hear a reply, answer the same way and stay where you can be seen. Yelling until you are hoarse does not mark your location."},{"symbol":"signaling_3","source":"symbolic_books/survival/signaling.txt","kind":"survival","hash":"473c0fd3","text":"Put bright gear in an open sight line, in a straight line or a large X. Flash a mirror toward a ridge or an aircraft in short groups of three, then stop so you do not dazzle someone close. Day smoke and a night flame are signals only where fire is legal and contained."},{"symbol":"signaling_4","source":"symbolic_books/survival/signaling.txt","kind":"survival","hash":"a5c30d3d","text":"An arrow of stones or packs can show the way you left, if you must leave, and only where the sky can see it. Tell a person your route, your return time, and the color of your pack before you go. If you are unsure, signal and stay visible. This is how to be found, not a radio trick."},{"symbol":"tools_cordage_1","source":"symbolic_books/survival/tools_cordage.txt","kind":"survival","hash":"9a88561e","text":"Tools and cordage: a knife, cord, and container solve more camp problems than improvising from scratch. Keep edges covered. Natural cord from bark or roots only comes from plants you are allowed to cut and can identify. This is not a weapons guide."},{"symbol":"tools_cordage_2","source":"symbolic_books/survival/tools_cordage.txt","kind":"survival","hash":"27757be0","text":"Use the knife away from your body, on a firm surface, and sheath it when you move. A dull edge slips, so a small stone belongs with the blade. Do not throw it, and do not pry with the only knife you have. A covered edge does not cut the bottle in the pack. This is handling, not a fight."},{"symbol":"tools_cordage_3","source":"symbolic_books/survival/tools_cordage.txt","kind":"survival","hash":"97a497bb","text":"Carry cord you already trust before you strip a living plant. A few meters can hang a pot clear of flame, tie a shelter ridge, or repair a strap. Learn two knots you can also undo. Wet natural fiber swells and can jam a knot you tied while it was dry."},{"symbol":"tools_cordage_4","source":"symbolic_books/survival/tools_cordage.txt","kind":"survival","hash":"c670a411","text":"A needle, a little tape, and cord stop a small tear from becoming a lost strap or a split handle. Brighten a tool handle so it shows against leaves. A lighter in a dry bag is the simple spark; practice it at home. This note does not teach you to burn the country."},{"symbol":"water_1","source":"symbolic_books/survival/water.txt","kind":"survival","hash":"00d2cf6b","text":"Water: carry known-safe water when you can. If you must treat water in the field, boiling is the straightforward heat method. Cloudy water should be settled or strained before boiling. This is an offline reminder, not a guarantee the water is safe, and not a substitute for emergency services."},{"symbol":"water_2","source":"symbolic_books/survival/water.txt","kind":"survival","hash":"d36b99a8","text":"Containers matter. A closed bottle or a pot you can heat beats a leaky bag. Keep the drinking bottle capped and off the ground, and refill it when you pass a source you mean to treat. A spare empty container is how you carry water away from the source."},{"symbol":"water_3","source":"symbolic_books/survival/water.txt","kind":"survival","hash":"ce062564","text":"Where to look: green growth on a dry slope, a dark seep, or a stream in the lowest line of a valley are clues. Flowing water is a better clue than a still pool. Clues are not proof. Settle the grit, then boil, and still treat the result as uncertain."},{"symbol":"water_4","source":"symbolic_books/survival/water.txt","kind":"survival","hash":"5f3659e5","text":"Snow and ice need melting before you drink them, and melting spends fuel you may need for warmth. Eating snow when you are already cold cools you further. Salt water and urine are not drinking water. This reminder sets no liter quota and no chemical dose."},{"symbol":"water_5","source":"symbolic_books/survival/water.txt","kind":"survival","hash":"f444097b","text":"Carry more than the first hour seems to need, then drink small amounts through the day instead of emptying the bottle at once. In heat, rest in shade before you feel dizzy. If someone stops sweating or grows confused, stop travel and get human help. This is not a medical threshold."},{"symbol":"weather_1","source":"symbolic_books/survival/weather.txt","kind":"survival","hash":"3c8c0ac6","text":"Weather: dark building clouds, sudden wind, and thunder mean you should leave high ground and open water. Cold plus wet clothing steals heat. This note does not forecast a local storm."},{"symbol":"weather_2","source":"symbolic_books/survival/weather.txt","kind":"survival","hash":"92710c1c","text":"Read the sky you have. A hard wind shift, a fast drop in temperature, and a dark towering cloud are reasons to leave ridges and lone tall trees. A shorter gap between flash and thunder only means the cell is closer. Do not wait for a forecast you cannot make. Get lower."},{"symbol":"weather_3","source":"symbolic_books/survival/weather.txt","kind":"survival","hash":"4e4f8242","text":"Wind that rises and holds one direction often comes before rain. Face the shelter away from it, and weight anything that can blow away. Blowing dust is a reason to seek a low sturdy spot, not a hilltop. This note is not a warning siren and not a local forecast."},{"symbol":"weather_4","source":"symbolic_books/survival/weather.txt","kind":"survival","hash":"c3e9afcf","text":"A clear night is often colder than a cloudy one. Put the rain layer on before you are soaked, and keep a hat where you can reach it. Stop early enough to camp in daylight. The plan is to be dry before the wind arrives, not after."},{"symbol":"weather_5","source":"symbolic_books/survival/weather.txt","kind":"survival","hash":"02e0d8d8","text":"Heat is a travel cost: still air, a high sun, and no shade. Move earlier and later, rest at midday, and cover your head. Pushing on through dizziness is a reason to stop and get help, not a goal. This note does not set a temperature rule."}],"noteVectors":"","intentLabels":["first_aid","first_aid","first_aid","first_aid","first_aid","outdoors","outdoors","outdoors","outdoors","outdoors","navigation","navigation","navigation","navigation","plants","plants","plants","math","math","math","math","scripture","scripture","scripture","scripture","off_topic","off_topic","off_topic","off_topic","off_topic","off_topic","off_topic","off_topic","off_topic","off_topic","off_topic","off_topic","realtime","realtime","realtime","realtime","dose","dose","dose"],"intentVectors":"","emergencyCount":15,"emergencyVectors":""};
